const BOOK_MD = "# Governing AI\n\n## A Reference Companion to the IAPP AIGP Body of Knowledge\n\n*Law and standards stated as of September 2026*\n\n# Before You Start\n\nHow to use this book and the rest of the app to prepare for the AIGP exam.\n\n### 1. Read the exam blueprint first\n\nStart with the IAPP **AIGP Body of Knowledge**, the official outline of everything the exam can test. Read it end to end so you know the shape of the territory before you dive into the source material.\n\n[AIGP Body of Knowledge & Exam Blueprint (PDF)](https://assets.contentstack.io/v3/assets/bltd4dd5b2d705252bc/blt0d33152fd20bc134/AIGP_Cert%20BOK.pdf)\n\n### 2. Read the primary sources in full\n\nThen read each of these foundational documents **in their entirety**. The exam leans hardest on the EU AI Act and GDPR, but every one of these shows up. All are free unless marked.\n\n**EU law**\n\n- [EU AI Act: Regulation (EU) 2024/1689](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj)\n- [Digital Omnibus on AI: Regulation (EU) 2026/1744](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng)\n- [GDPR: Regulation (EU) 2016/679](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj)\n- [EDPB Opinion 28/2024: AI models and the GDPR](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2024-12/edpb_opinion_202428_ai-models_en.pdf)\n- [Product Liability Directive: (EU) 2024/2853](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2024/2853/oj/eng)\n- [EU General-Purpose AI Code of Practice](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-code-practice)\n\n**Standards and frameworks**\n\n- [NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI 100-1)](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf)\n- [NIST AI RMF Playbook](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/)\n- [NIST Generative AI Profile (AI 600-1)](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf)\n- [OECD AI Principles](https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles)\n- [ISO/IEC 22989: AI concepts and terminology](https://www.iso.org/standard/74296.html)\n\n**Other AI-specific laws**\n\n- [South Korea AI Basic Act](https://elaw.klri.re.kr/eng_service/lawView.do?hseq=71019&lang=ENG)\n- [IAPP US State AI Governance Legislation Tracker](https://prod.iapp.org/media/pdf/resource_center/us_state_ai_governance_legislation_tracker.pdf)\n\n**AI copyright and IP case law**\n\n- [Thaler v. Perlmutter](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/25-449.html)\n- [Getty Images v Stability AI (UK)](https://www.judiciary.uk/judgments/getty-images-v-stability-ai/)\n- [Bartz v. Anthropic](https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/69058235/bartz-v-anthropic-pbc/)\n\n### 3. Learn IAPP's vocabulary\n\nThe exam uses IAPP's own definitions, so skim its free glossary alongside the Flashcards. The global tracker gives one-page summaries of each jurisdiction's AI law. Everything in this list is free.\n\n- [IAPP Key Terms for AI Governance (PDF)](https://prod.iapp.org/media/pdf/resource_center/key_terms_for_ai_governance.pdf)\n- [IAPP Global AI Law and Policy Tracker (PDF)](https://assets.contentstack.io/v3/assets/bltd4dd5b2d705252bc/blt34a8e3844fb44942/global_ai_law_policy_tracker.pdf)\n- [IAPP Certification Candidate Handbook v5.3.2](https://assets.contentstack.io/v3/assets/bltd4dd5b2d705252bc/blteb8b5d531fd78971/IAPP-Certification_Handbook.pdf)\n- [The Unofficial AIGP Resource Guide, Oliver Patel](https://oliverpatel.substack.com/p/the-unofficial-aigp-resource-guide)\n\n### 4. Read the book, then work the Mind Map\n\nRead this book straight through once. Then open the Mind Map tab, pick a section, and click its node. Read through that section's documentation in the drawer that opens, and open its sub-sections to go deeper. Then use the section's question and flashcard buttons to drill just that section in the Quiz and Flashcards.\n\n### 5. Turn the whole map green\n\nRepeat that for every section. Nodes color themselves by how you're scoring: grey (untested), then red, amber and green. When the entire map is lit up green, you've genuinely covered the material.\n\n### 6. Take practice exams\n\nFinally, go to the Test tab and sit a few timed exams under real conditions. The domain mix follows the blueprint, and some questions are \"Select N\" multi-selects with no partial credit, as on the real exam. **Once you're consistently scoring above 85%, you're ready to take the AIGP.**\n\n- [What changed in BOK v2.1, Dr. Kyle David (free video)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cmWGspcCdkc)\n- [AIGP Study Kit](https://github.com/jasondmacleod/aigp-study-kit)\n- [aigp-exam-prep (MarginX)](https://github.com/sanjaynandanj/aigp-exam-prep)\n- [IAPP official practice exam](https://store.iapp.org/aigp-practice-exam-digital/)\n\nAvoid \"braindump\" sites selling real exam questions. IAPP's candidate handbook treats using them as cheating.\n\n# Contents\n\n- About This Book\n- How to Read a Chapter\n- About the AIGP Exam\n- Part I: Foundations\n    - Chapter 1: What AI Is, and Why It Needs Governance\n    - Chapter 2: Ownership, Roles and the Value Chain\n    - Chapter 3: Policies Across the Life Cycle\n- Part II: Laws, Standards and Frameworks\n    - Chapter 4: Privacy Law Meets AI\n    - Chapter 5: Existing Law That Reaches AI\n    - Chapter 6: The Law Written for AI\n    - Chapter 7: Standards, Frameworks and Certification\n- Part III: Governing AI Development\n    - Chapter 8: Design and Impact Assessment\n    - Chapter 9: Training and Testing Data\n    - Chapter 10: Release and Monitoring\n- Part IV: Governing AI Deployment and Use\n    - Chapter 11: Deciding to Deploy\n    - Chapter 12: Assessing What You Adopt\n    - Chapter 13: Operating, Monitoring and Retiring\n- Where the Four Domains Meet\n- Appendix A: Standards and Frameworks at a Glance\n- Appendix B: Glossary\n\n# About This Book\n\nThis is a reference for people preparing for the IAPP's Artificial Intelligence Governance Professional (AIGP) certification, and for anyone newly responsible for how an organization uses AI. It covers the four domains of the AIGP Body of Knowledge (BOK) v2.1 in order: foundations, then laws, standards and frameworks, then the governance of AI development, then the governance of AI deployment and use.\n\nIt is written to be read straight through once, and then used as a reference. Chapters follow the BOK's domains and competencies rather than the order in which a project happens to hit them, so the material accumulates: every later chapter reasons with the vocabulary and the pairings installed earlier.\n\n**Every worked example is real.** Where a case is used, it is named, dated and linked, so you can read the primary source yourself. These are not invented scenarios. Cases are drawn from regulator decisions, court judgments, official reports and enforcement actions, and they are cited in each chapter's Further Reading. Where the exam tests a general principle, the book says so explicitly rather than letting one jurisdiction's facts stand in for a rule.\n\nThis is an unofficial study aid. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by IAPP. AIGP and IAPP are IAPP trademarks. Nothing here is legal advice: the cases show how real organizations and courts reasoned about particular facts, and decisions about your own obligations belong with qualified counsel.\n\n## The two timing facts that affect everything\n\n**BOK v2.1 was approved on 9 September 2025.** It sets the blueprint this book follows, and its domain weights, competency ranges and vocabulary are properties of that version. They may change when IAPP issues the next Body of Knowledge.\n\n**The EU AI Act now exists in two versions.** The Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744), in force since 27 July 2026, amended the Act as adopted (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) and moved several of its dates. Wherever the two versions differ, this book labels the fact \"as enacted\" or \"as amended by the Digital Omnibus on AI.\" The clearest case is the date from which the Annex III high-risk obligations apply: 2 August 2026 as enacted, 2 December 2027 as amended.\n\nThat matters for the exam. Version 2.1 was approved before the Omnibus was agreed, so a live exam item may be written against either version. A well-drafted stem names its anchor, such as \"as originally enacted\" or \"as amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus.\" Where it names none, the useful question is whether the fact being tested changed at all. Knowing both timelines, and which facts the Omnibus left untouched, is the safe preparation.\n\n## Status and legal vocabulary\n\nThe book uses a fixed vocabulary for the status of a legal instrument, because the exam tests the difference.\n\n| Term | Means | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Proposed | Put forward but not approved | The Commission's Digital Omnibus amendments to the GDPR, as at September 2026 |\n| Draft | Published for comment before it is final | The EDPB's 2026 guidelines on anonymisation and web scraping |\n| Withdrawn | Pulled back by the body that issued it | The Commission's proposed AI Liability Directive |\n| On appeal | A court decision under review by a higher court | The leading European decisions on AI training and copyright |\n| Adopted | Formally approved or enacted | Colorado's original AI Act, SB 24-205 |\n| In force | Legally effective | Most of the EU AI Act |\n| Applies from | In force, with obligations effective only from a later date | The Annex III high-risk obligations |\n\nAdopted and in force are the two words that most need separating. A law can be adopted and never take effect, which is what happened to Colorado's SB 24-205.\n\n## A caution about sector\n\nOne warning from the BOK shapes how to read every case in this book. Domain I is described as holding \"regardless of industry, sector or size.\" An exam answer that assumes a bank, a hospital or a large enterprise is usually the wrong one.\n\nThat has a consequence for worked examples. A real case is bounded by its own facts: the law it was decided under, the jurisdiction, the sector and the role the organization held. A case about a lender illustrates lending, and a case about an employer illustrates employment. What transfers is the governance reasoning, not the legal conclusion. Where a chapter uses a sector-specific case to make a general point, it says so. Sector specifics belong to Domain II's sectoral laws and to the lifecycle domains, and this book flags them where they appear.\n\n# How to Read a Chapter\n\nEvery chapter follows the same shape, so you always know where you are.\n\n**A lead.** The chapter opens with what it settles and which distinctions the exam tests. Where the chapter draws on real cases, they are listed in a table with their citations, so you can see the evidence before the argument.\n\n**Numbered teaching sections.** The body of the chapter, following the BOK's competencies. Bold marks a key term at its first defining use, and tables carry material that is genuinely tabular.\n\n**Exam Focus.** The tested distinctions, traps and facts, one sentence each. Remarks about the exam live here rather than in the running text, so the prose stays clean and the exam-facing summary stays findable.\n\n**Key Terms.** The chapter's important terms, each defined in the Glossary.\n\n**Further Reading.** The official texts, standards, decisions and cases behind the chapter, each linked.\n\nThree things are worth doing as you read. Note the **pairings**: a characteristic and the control that answers it, a legal role and the duty that attaches to it, a risk and the owner who answers for it. Most applied exam questions are a pairing with one half missing. Note the **status** of anything the book describes as proposed or draft, because a question may test whether you know it is not yet law. And note the **role** an organization holds under each regime, because one organization can be a developer, a provider, a deployer and a controller at the same time, and the duties divide by role.\n\n# About the AIGP Exam\n\nThe exam is built on the IAPP's **Body of Knowledge**, which sets out what a candidate should be able to do in four domains, each divided into competencies. This book follows version 2.1, approved on 9 September 2025. It allocates the exam's 100 **scored items**, the questions that count toward your result, across the four domains as ranges rather than fixed numbers.\n\n| Domain | Subject | Scored items | Part of this book |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I | Foundations: what AI governance is, and the principles and pillars of a program | 16 to 20 | Part I (Chapters 1 to 3) |\n| II | Laws, standards and frameworks | 19 to 23 | Part II (Chapters 4 to 7) |\n| III | Governing AI development | 21 to 25 | Part III (Chapters 8 to 10) |\n| IV | Governing AI deployment and use | 21 to 25 | Part IV (Chapters 11 to 13) |\n\nDomain I is the smallest by question count but has the most downstream reach, because every later domain reasons with the vocabulary and principles it installs. Domain II is where the exam stops asking what AI governance is and starts asking what the law requires. The two lifecycle domains tie for the largest share, 42 to 50 items between them. Domain III covers how an organization runs the build. Domain IV, the most scenario-heavy part of the syllabus, covers how it chooses a system, adopts it and lives with it.\n\nEach Part opener carries an **Exam at a Glance** table that breaks its domain into competencies with their question ranges, and lists what changed in version 2.1. The competency ranges do not add up to the domain totals, so learn each as published rather than trying to reconcile them.\n\nThe BOK places Domain I items mostly at the remember/understand and apply/analyze levels of Bloom's taxonomy, which is why definitional precision pays more in Domain I than anywhere else in the syllabus. The later domains ask more scenario questions, which is why the lifecycle parts of this book are built around decisions and their owners rather than definitions alone.\n\n# Part I: Foundations\n\n*Who owns it?*\n\nThe first domain answers what AI governance is and what a program that delivers it contains. It is the smallest domain by question count and the one every later part depends on, because Domains II to IV reason with the vocabulary installed here. Its three competencies map onto three questions: what a system is and what can go wrong (I.A), who answers for a decision (I.B), and what has to be written down (I.C).\n\n### Exam at a Glance\n\nUnder BOK v2.1, approved on 9 September 2025, Domain I carries 16 to 20 of the 100 scored items, the fewest of the four domains (Domain II carries 19 to 23, Domains III and IV 21 to 25 each). Each competency has its own chapter.\n\n| Competency | What it covers | Questions | Chapter |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I.A Understand what AI is and why it needs governance | Definitions and types of AI; risks and harms; the characteristics that demand governance; responsible AI principles | 4 to 6 | 1 |\n| I.B Establish and communicate organizational expectations | Roles and responsibilities; cross-functional collaboration; training and awareness; tailoring by context; developers, providers, deployers and users | 5 to 7 | 2 |\n| I.C Establish policies and procedures across the life cycle | Oversight and accountability at every stage; updating existing privacy, security, data-governance and IP policies; third-party risk | 6 to 8 | 3 |\n\nThe competency ranges sum to 15 to 21, not the domain's 16 to 20, so learn each figure as published. Questions sit mostly at the remember/understand and apply/analyze levels of Bloom's taxonomy, so definitional precision pays more here than anywhere else in the syllabus. The sector-agnostic framing is itself testable: an answer that assumes a bank, a hospital or a large enterprise is usually the wrong one.\n\nBOK v2.1 made three changes to Domain I relative to v2.0.1.\n\n- I.B now lists providers alongside developers, deployers and users, so that you see the whole value chain rather than only the internal cast. Chapter 2 explains who counts as a provider.\n- I.C's policy-review item now names data governance and intellectual property explicitly, alongside the data privacy and security policies v2.0.1 already listed.\n- I.C's third-party item now covers policies, assessments and contracts, and adds acceptable use to the areas it already named (procurement, supply chain and human resources).\n\n**Chapters.** Chapter 1 settles what counts as an AI system, what can go wrong, why ordinary software controls do not answer either question, and which principles respond. Chapter 2 puts names against decisions: roles and responsibilities, an accountability model, literacy and training by role, and the value-chain hats an organization wears as a developer, provider or deployer. Chapter 3 turns the expectations into policy: human oversight across the life cycle, a retrofit of the policies an organization already has, and the risk that arrives through third parties.\n\n## Chapter 1: What AI Is, and Why It Needs Governance\n\nGovernance of AI begins with three questions that are harder than they look: what counts as an AI system, what can go wrong with it, and why the controls an organization already has do not answer the first two. This chapter settles all three.\n\n- Section 1.1 gives a working test for what belongs on an AI inventory, then four taxonomies for classifying whatever passes it.\n- Section 1.2 sorts harms by who suffers them, which is also what decides who owns the response.\n- Section 1.3 names the seven characteristics that separate AI from conventional software, and pairs each with the control it justifies. That pairing is the spine of every framework in this book.\n- Section 1.4 sets out the six principles that answer those characteristics, and the translation problem between the four vocabularies that carry them.\n\nDomain I is the smallest of the four by question count, 16 to 20 of the exam's 100 scored items, and it is tested as sector-agnostic: an answer that assumes a particular industry, sector or size is usually wrong. Its importance is reach rather than volume, because every later domain reasons with the vocabulary and the pairings installed here.\n\nThe cases this chapter draws on are real, and each is cited where it appears.\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Moffatt v. Air Canada | 2024 BCCRT 149, decided 14 February 2024 | Confabulation, and accountability that cannot be delegated |\n| SyRI | Rechtbank Den Haag, 5 February 2020 | Group harm, opacity, data dependency |\n| FTC v. Rite Aid | FTC consent order, December 2023 | Individual harm, autonomy, oversight failure |\n| Zillow Offers | Zillow Group results, 2 November 2021 | Complexity, speed and scale, organizational harm |\n| Garante v. Luka Inc. | Garante order, 10 April 2025 | Data protection by design, vulnerable users |\n| Arup deepfake transfer | Reported January 2024 | Misuse, information integrity |\n\n### 1.1 What Counts as AI\n\n\"Artificial intelligence\" covers technology ranging from a chess engine that evaluates only the current board position to a language model that writes poetry, generates code and explains the difference between an interest rate and an APR. Such systems have almost nothing in common under the hood, yet they provoke the same regulatory questions. A clear taxonomy is therefore the precondition for any risk analysis worth doing.\n\nLearn one definition. The OECD's 2023 definition, which the EU AI Act's Article 3(1) closely tracks, holds that an **AI system** is a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers from the input it receives how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments, with systems varying in autonomy and adaptiveness after deployment. For inventory purposes the operative word is \"infers.\" A system that only executes rules written in advance by a person is not inferring.\n\nFour taxonomies then describe what is on the list. Real analysis mixes them, and each answers a different question.\n\n**Capability.** **Narrow AI** performs a specific task or narrow task family. **Artificial general intelligence (AGI)** would match human-level competence across essentially all cognitive domains, and **artificial superintelligence (ASI)** would surpass the best human performance in all of them. As of this writing every deployed system is narrow AI. The trap is breadth: a general-purpose chatbot that answers questions on law, medicine and poetry is still narrow, because generality means transfer across unrelated domains rather than the size of a training set.\n\n**Functionality.** This lens asks about memory. **Reactive machines** respond to input with no memory of the past, and Deep Blue is the canonical example. **Limited memory** systems retain recent context to inform the next decision, which describes most modern systems, from recommendation engines to a chat assistant within a conversation. **Theory of mind** and **self-aware AI** remain conceptual categories rather than deployed technology.\n\n**Learning paradigm.** How practitioners actually describe a system. **Supervised learning** trains on labeled input and output pairs, the way a spam filter learns from messages marked \"spam\" or \"not spam.\" **Unsupervised learning** finds structure in unlabeled data, as a customer-segmentation model clusters buying patterns without being told what the segments are. **Semi-supervised learning** combines a small labeled set with a large unlabeled corpus, which helps when labels are expensive. **Reinforcement learning** learns a policy that maximizes a reward signal through interaction with an environment. **Self-supervised learning** derives labels from the data itself, and is the paradigm behind modern language models.\n\n**Generative stack.** The layering behind generative AI. **Neural networks** compose trainable units, **deep learning** stacks many layers of them, and **transformers** made attention practical at scale, which enabled **large language models**. **Foundation models** are broad pretrained models adapted to downstream tasks by fine-tuning, prompting or retrieval. Two properties of this lineage matter for governance. Scale buys capability, sometimes abruptly, so a model's capabilities cannot always be listed in advance (Section 1.3 returns to this). And a pretrained model carries its training data's properties, biases included, into every downstream use.\n\nOne category has recently moved to the centre of the governance conversation. An **AI agent** perceives its environment and takes action toward goals, rather than only returning a prediction when asked. **Agentic AI** therefore moves from advising a human to acting on the world, which changes the controls required. A draft a human can ignore is advice. A payment plan created through a servicing API is an action, and it needs an action limit, an audit trail and a way to stop it. Section 11.4 designs one.\n\n### 1.2 Sorting the Harms\n\nHarms sort four ways, by who suffers them, and the category decides who owns the response.\n\nHarms to **individuals** include privacy violation, discriminatory decisions in hiring or credit, physical safety failure, manipulation and loss of autonomy. In December 2023 the FTC found that Rite Aid's facial recognition system falsely tagged shoppers as shoplifters, disproportionately women and people of colour, and banned the company from using the technology for security or surveillance for five years. The victims were individuals, but the pattern was not.\n\nHarms to **groups** include systematic bias against protected classes, amplification through feedback loops and erosion of shared factual ground. SyRI, a Dutch welfare fraud risk-scoring system, scored people living in already poor neighbourhoods. The court that struck it down in 2020 cited the real risk of discrimination and stigmatisation of citizens by socio-economic status and migration background. The harm ran to a population, which is why no single complaint could have surfaced it.\n\nHarms to **organizations** include regulatory penalty, litigation, reputational damage, intellectual property contamination, security exposure and operational disruption. Zillow Offers, an automated home-valuation business, overpaid for houses at scale and closed in November 2021 having taken a $304 million write-down and cut roughly a quarter of its workforce.\n\nHarms to **society** include misinformation and deepfakes, displacement of work, concentration of capability, and the environmental cost of training and inference. In January 2024 a finance employee at the engineering firm Arup transferred roughly HK$200 million, about US$25 million, across fifteen transactions after a video call in which the chief financial officer and colleagues were generated by AI.\n\nThe BOK adds three examples of AI risk that cut across the sort. **Misalignment with objectives** is a system optimizing the metric it was given rather than the outcome anyone wanted, such as a support dashboard tuned to the share of chats closed without a human, which rewards keeping customers away from people. **Ethics and bias risk** is unfair or harmful treatment that the data or the objective encoded. **Complexity and scalability** is the multiplier: a flaw in a manual process harms the people it touches, while one mis-weighted feature in a scoring model harms every applicant scored before anyone notices.\n\nGenerative AI adds failures of its own. NIST's Generative AI Profile (NIST AI 600-1, July 2024) names twelve risks unique to or amplified by generative AI, catalogued in Section 7.4. The most significant is **confabulation**, the formal term for hallucination: confidently stated, plausible, false output, which is a property of the paradigm rather than an occasional bug. In Moffatt v. Air Canada, an airline chatbot invented a bereavement-fare policy that did not exist, the customer relied on it, and the British Columbia Civil Resolution Tribunal held the airline liable for negligent misrepresentation. The remaining named risks include harmful bias and homogenization, information integrity (the deepfake and disinformation problem), data privacy, information security, intellectual property, and value-chain and component integration, meaning the risk inherited from models and data you did not build.\n\nThe sort is not academic. An individual harm usually triggers a rights or recourse process. A group harm triggers fairness testing and remediation. An organizational harm runs through the risk register and the board. A societal harm may have no internal owner at all, which is exactly why impact assessments exist. A harm that is not assigned to a category tends not to get a control.\n\n### 1.3 What Makes AI Different\n\nConventional IT controls assume that software is specified, deterministic and stable. AI violates all three. The BOK names seven characteristics that separate AI from ordinary software, and each one defeats a specific assumption, which is what justifies a specific control. Learn them as pairs. Most applied questions in this domain are either a characteristic asking for its control, or a control asking which characteristic it answers.\n\n| Characteristic | Assumption it defeats | Control it justifies |\n|---|---|---|\n| Complexity | Human intuition and simple review; many interacting parts and emergent behavior | Layered testing, documentation, auditability, decomposition into components |\n| Opacity | The ability to explain a specific decision to the person affected | Explainability techniques, model cards, disclosure duties, contestability |\n| Autonomy | The assumption that a human is in the decision loop | Human oversight design, action limits, interruption and override, kill switches |\n| Speed and scale | The assumption that errors are caught locally and slowly | Pre-deployment testing, continuous monitoring, defined rollback criteria |\n| Potential for harm or misuse | The assumption that capability is used as intended | Impact assessment, ethics review, security controls, red teaming |\n| Data dependency | The assumption that behavior is specified in code | Data governance, lineage and provenance, representativeness and bias testing |\n| Probabilistic outputs | Binary notions of correct and incorrect | Confidence thresholds, abstention, monitoring, user training against over-trust |\n\nRead the middle column top to bottom and you have the list of things IT governance takes for granted. Three rows carry the most weight.\n\n**Probabilistic output.** Traditional software returns the same output for the same input, and a defect is a deviation from specification. A model returns a statistical estimate, so the same input may yield different outputs, and being wrong is a property of the method rather than a bug. A claim of 97% accuracy is not a clearance. The useful follow-up questions are who the 3% are, what happens to them, and where that is written down. \"Accurate 97% of the time\" describes residual risk, nothing more.\n\n**Data dependency** makes AI governance inseparable from data governance. Provenance, representativeness and quality determine the outcome, which is why lineage, provenance and bias testing appear in every framework and, for high-risk systems, in law. SyRI is the cautionary version: the data it scored on encoded socio-economic and migration patterns, so the model reproduced them.\n\n**Opacity** is the gap between a confident output and an explanation of it. Rite Aid's system produced flags its operators could not interrogate, and the FTC's complaint turned on the absence of reasonable safeguards rather than on the model's error rate. Zillow's valuation model is the speed and scale row made financial: a model that was individually plausible in every case was collectively wrong, and the correction arrived only after the inventory had been bought.\n\n**Emergence.** The seven form a list rather than a taxonomy, and this book adds an eighth trait that sits behind several of them. **Emergence** is not on the BOK's list. It means capabilities and behaviors can appear after deployment through scale, fine-tuning, data drift or interaction with other systems. Research on emergent abilities reports behaviors that appear only past a size threshold, though how abrupt those jumps are is debated, partly because the answer depends on how capability is measured. Emergence is the reason point-in-time approval is insufficient and monitoring and reassessment are continuous. It is also why the Arup transfer succeeded: nothing in the fraud was a defect in a system, only an AI's output used as though it were a person's.\n\n### 1.4 The Principles That Answer Them\n\nIf the characteristics define the problem, principles describe what good looks like. The BOK names six responsible AI principles, and the discipline is to tie each one to a control the organization will actually run.\n\n1. **Fairness** requires equitable treatment and outcomes across groups, through bias testing across subgroups, fairness metrics chosen for the context, mitigation and documented trade-offs.\n2. **Safety and reliability** require intended function under expected and unexpected conditions, through rigorous testing, edge-case and adversarial testing, fail-safes, interruptibility and continuous monitoring.\n3. **Privacy and security** require protected personal data and systems resilient to attack, through minimization, de-identification, access control, AI-specific threat modeling and pipeline security.\n4. **Transparency and explainability** require that people know AI is in use and can understand outcomes affecting them, through disclosure, instructions for use, explanations of decisions, documentation and contestability.\n5. **Accountability** requires identified humans who answer for outcomes, through named owners, a RACI, escalation paths, audit rights and records.\n6. **Human-centricity** requires that AI augments human agency rather than overriding it, through human oversight of consequential decisions, impact assessment and design that preserves choice.\n\nThe same substance appears in four different vocabularies, which is best read as a series of stages by which principles become obligations. The voluntary **OECD AI Principles** (2019, revised 2024) name five: inclusive growth, sustainable development and well-being; respect for the rule of law, human rights and democratic values; transparency and explainability; robustness, security and safety; and accountability. The voluntary **NIST AI Risk Management Framework** names seven trustworthiness characteristics: valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; and fair with harmful bias managed. The **EU AI Act** turns similar substance into legal requirements for high-risk systems in Articles 9 to 15 (risk management, data governance, technical documentation, record-keeping, transparency and instructions for use, human oversight, and accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity), plus the Article 50 transparency duties. Section 7.1 holds the crosswalk and Section 6.2 the law.\n\nA governance professional is expected to translate between these on demand, and three distinctions recur in every one of them.\n\n**Transparency** concerns the organization: that AI is in use, what it does and what its limits are. **Explainability** concerns a specific output, and whether the person affected can understand why it came out that way. **Interpretability** is the underlying model property that makes explanation possible at all.\n\n**Responsibility** is who does the work, and **accountability** is who answers for the outcome. They are usually different people, and accountability cannot be delegated to a model or a vendor. Air Canada tested that boundary directly, arguing before the tribunal that its chatbot was \"a separate legal entity that is responsible for its own actions.\" The tribunal rejected it: the chatbot was part of the airline's website, and the airline answered for what it said. Every accountability framework in this book exists to prevent that failure in advance.\n\n**Fairness is plural.** Several fairness metrics cannot hold at the same time except in degenerate cases, so \"make it fair\" is not a specification. The governance answer is to choose metrics for the context, document the trade-off and test across subgroups.\n\nWhen controls compete, the one that honors more of the principles, with documentation and proportionality, is almost always the better choice, and a control that bundles several principles into one function is rarely adequate. Without a control attached, a principle is a statement of intent, and statements of intent do not survive contact with a delivery deadline.\n\n### Exam Focus\n\nThese are the points this chapter's material most often turns on.\n\n- Domain I is tested as sector-agnostic, and the exam's harm examples are generic.\n- The chapter's four sections track competency I.A's four performance indicators: definitions and types, risks and harms, characteristics, and principles.\n- Know the four taxonomies and the five learning paradigms, and watch the breadth trap: a model that answers across many subjects is still narrow AI, because generality means transfer across unrelated domains.\n- Harms sort by who is harmed (individuals, groups, organizations, society), and the BOK's three examples (misalignment with objectives, ethics and bias risk, complexity and scalability) cut across that sort.\n- Learn the seven characteristics verbatim: complexity, opacity, autonomy, speed and scale, potential for harm or misuse, data dependency, and probabilistic outputs.\n- Probabilistic versus deterministic output is easy to gloss over and heavily tested: a model can be wrong without any defect.\n- Emergence is this book's addition, not a tested characteristic, and the BOK's list is a set of characteristics rather than a taxonomy.\n- The BOK's six principles are the canonical set, and mapping questions expect translation into OECD, NIST AI RMF and EU AI Act terms.\n- Hold three distinctions: transparency (organization), explainability (output) and interpretability (model property), then responsibility (doing the work) versus accountability (answering for it, never delegable to a model or vendor), and fairness as plural.\n- Principles are the tiebreaker, so the answer that honors more principles with documentation and proportionality is almost always intended, and \"assign it to legal\" is a classic distractor.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe following terms are used in this chapter and defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): AI Agent, AI System, Artificial General Intelligence (AGI), Artificial Superintelligence (ASI), Complexity, Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), Deep Learning, Deterministic Output, Expert System, Exploding Gradient, Feedforward Neural Network (FFNN), Foundation Model, General AI (GAI), Generative AI, Hyperparameter, Inference, Large Language Model (LLM), Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), Machine Learning (ML), Machine Learning Algorithm, Machine Learning Model, Model, Narrow AI (Weak AI), Natural Language Processing (NLP), Neural Network (NN), Probabilistic Output, Reactive Machines, Reasoning AI, Recurrent Neural Network (RNN), Reinforcement Learning (RL), Robotics, Sample, Self-Aware AI, Semi-Supervised Learning, Semi-Supervised Machine Learning, Supervised Learning, Supervised Machine Learning, Support Vector Machine (SVM), Theory of Mind AI, Training (Model Training), Training Data, Unsupervised Learning, Unsupervised Machine Learning, Zero-Shot Learning (ZSL), Deepfake, Disinformation, Downstream Harm, Echo Chamber, F1 Score, Hallucination, Misalignment, Misinformation, Sensitive Data, Unfair and Deceptive Acts and Practices (UDAP), Unintended Use, User Training, Autonomy, Ethical Considerations, Limited Memory AI, Opacity, Potential for Harm, Speed and Scale, Contestability, Ethics by Design, Explainability, Human-Centricity, Interpretability, Interpretability Testing, Trustworthy AI.\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [OECD: what is an AI system?](https://oecd.ai/en/wonk/definition): the definitional baseline the BOK uses\n- [ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839): formal definitions for the learning paradigms and system terms\n- [Emergent Abilities of Large Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.07682): capabilities that appear only past a scale threshold\n- [NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf): AI system and AI actor definitions, and the risk framing\n- [NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf): the twelve GAI risks and the actions mapped to each\n- [OECD AI Principles](https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles): the intergovernmental baseline, adopted 2019 and revised 2024\n- [EU AI Act, Article 5 (prohibited practices)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/5/): the harms serious enough to be banned outright\n- [Moffatt v. Air Canada, 2024 BCCRT 149](https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bccrt/doc/2024/2024bccrt149/2024bccrt149.html): liability for a chatbot's misrepresentation, and the rejected \"separate legal entity\" argument\n- [FTC v. Rite Aid Corporation](https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/cases-proceedings/2023190-rite-aid-corporation-ftc-v): the consent order and the five-year facial recognition ban\n- [Digital Freedom Fund: the SyRI case](https://digitalfreedomfund.org/case-studies/the-syri-welfare-fraud-risk-scoring-algorithm/): the judgment and the discrimination risk the court cited\n- [Zillow Group Q3 2021 results](https://investors.zillowgroup.com/): the Zillow Offers write-down and wind-down\n- [Garante: fine against Luka Inc. (Replika)](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/news/ai-the-italian-supervisory-authority-fines-company-behind-chatbot-replika_en): the EUR 5m order and the age-verification findings\n- [OECD AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor](https://oecd.ai/en/incidents): real, catalogued AI incidents and near misses, and the standing source for cases added later\n\n## Chapter 2: Ownership, Roles and the Value Chain\n\nChapter 1 ended with an inventory and a blank owner column. This chapter fills it. Domain I.B asks an organization to establish and communicate its expectations, which means naming who decides what, making those people capable of deciding it, and recognizing that some of the decisions that shape a system are made by someone else entirely.\n\n- Section 2.1 puts a name against every decision, through three layers of ownership.\n- Section 2.2 gives the instrument, a RACI by task, along with stakeholder mapping and where AI risk belongs in the enterprise register.\n- Section 2.3 makes the owners capable, through training sized to the decisions they make.\n- Section 2.4 covers the roles along the value chain, and the difference between the BOK's task labels and the EU AI Act's legal categories.\n- Section 2.5 scales the whole program to the organization running it.\n\nOne failure runs through all five, and it is worth naming up front. Handing AI to a single expert is the most common instinct and the most common structural mistake. The decisions that matter are spread across the organization, so a governance function that owns AI on everyone's behalf ends up owning nothing. What follows is the alternative: shared responsibility with exactly one accountable owner per decision.\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Drivers v. Uber and Ola | Amsterdam Court of Appeal, 4 April 2023 | Human oversight that exists on paper only |\n| Samsung's employee chatbot ban | Reported May 2023 | Shadow AI as a readiness failure |\n| FTC v. Rite Aid | FTC consent order, December 2023 | Automation bias in the operating layer |\n| Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme | Report tabled 7 July 2023 | Accountability with no owner |\n| Bauserman v. Michigan UIA | Settlement approved January 2024 | Oversight failure at scale in a public body |\n\n### 2.1 Three Layers of Ownership\n\nChapter 1 separated responsibility from accountability: many people do the work, and one named person answers for the outcome. Governance builds that distinction into three layers, and the exam expects you to know what each layer owns.\n\n**The direction layer** is the board and the C-suite: the Chief Executive Officer, Chief Operating Officer, Chief Risk Officer, Chief Information Security Officer, Chief Data and Analytics Officer, Chief Privacy Officer, Chief Compliance Officer, and any Chief AI Ethics Officer. It owns risk appetite, funding, tone and final accountability.\n\n**The steering layer** is a cross-functional AI governance board or council drawn from legal, privacy, security, data science, ethics, risk, compliance and the business units. It owns the framework, the review of higher-risk use cases, escalation decisions and reporting upward.\n\n**The operating layer** covers data scientists and AI engineers, data engineers, AI operations, product owners, risk and compliance staff, and frontline users. It owns building, assessing, monitoring, documenting and flagging anomalies.\n\nA growing number of organizations add a **Chief AI Ethics Officer** to hold the ethical governance remit explicitly. It is a signal of maturity rather than a legal requirement, and no instrument in Chapter 6 or 7 compels one. On an exam, an answer that treats the role as mandatory is wrong.\n\nThe three layers are a division of labour, not a hierarchy of importance. The operating layer is where most governance failures actually surface, because it is where the system meets the person affected by it.\n\n### 2.2 RACI by Task\n\nA **RACI** matrix names, for each task, who is **Responsible** for doing it, who is **Accountable** for the outcome, who is **Consulted** before the decision and who is **Informed** of it.\n\nApplied per governance task rather than per department, it forces two things an org chart hides: exactly one accountable owner per task, and an explicit list of who must be consulted before the decision. Governance failures usually trace to a task with no accountable owner, or to a decision taken without the function that would have caught the problem.\n\nThree rules make it work in practice.\n\n- **One Accountable, always.** Two accountable names is the same as none, because each can assume the other is watching. This is the single most testable point about RACI.\n- **Accountable is not the most senior person available.** It is the person with the authority and the information to answer for the outcome.\n- **Consulted is not Informed.** The distinction matters legally as well as operationally, because a consultative role that carries no decision rights is also what preserves the independence of functions the law requires to be independent.\n\nThat last point is worth stating precisely, because it is tested. Under GDPR Article 38 a data protection officer must be independent: no instructions on the exercise of the DPO's tasks, and no conflict of interest from other duties. A DPO or a privacy function therefore sits as **Consulted**, never Accountable, on processing they monitor. A RACI that makes the DPO accountable for a system they also supervise has built the conflict the article forbids.\n\n**Stakeholder mapping** identifies everyone, internal and external, affected by a system or able to influence it: the business sponsor, affected employees, the people scored, the communities implicated, the vendors in the chain and the regulators with jurisdiction. It is a control rather than paperwork, because a stakeholder who is never identified is a risk nobody owns. External stakeholders, civil society and affected communities among them, are the ones internal processes most reliably miss.\n\n**Integration into enterprise risk management.** AI risk kept in a separate register gets reviewed separately and funded last. The mature pattern is to tag AI risks inside the existing **enterprise risk management (ERM)** portfolio, with AI-specific fields for the system, the model version, the affected population and fairness exposure, rather than running a parallel process. AI governance then reaches the board through risk reporting that already has its attention. A standalone AI register that never touches ERM is the wrong answer on an exam.\n\n### 2.3 Literacy and Readiness\n\nOwners are only as good as their grasp of what they own. The BOK's indicator is to create and deliver a training and awareness program covering AI terminology, strategy and governance for all stakeholders. Four design consequences follow.\n\n**Depth by role.** One deck for the whole organization satisfies nobody, because a board member, a data scientist and a contact-centre agent need different things. Baseline awareness for everyone, and depth that follows the decisions a person actually makes.\n\n| Audience | What they need |\n|---|---|\n| Executives and board | Strategic alignment, regulatory exposure, reputational and enterprise risk, how to read an AI risk register |\n| Developers and data scientists | Governance by design, documentation standards, bias and fairness testing, reproducibility, the approval workflow |\n| Legal, risk and compliance | Regulatory fluency, impact assessment practice, vendor and contract risk, audit readiness |\n| Product and business owners | Use-case vetting, success criteria, user impact, their accountability for outcomes |\n| Frontline users | What the system can and cannot do, when to override, how to report an anomaly, recourse options |\n| IT and security | Monitoring, incident response, control ownership, AI-specific attack surface |\n| Everyone | Basic literacy, the organization's principles, how to escalate a concern |\n\nThe frontline row deserves emphasis, because people asked to flag anomalies have to be taught what one looks like. Oversight assigned to staff who cannot recognize a failure mode is oversight in name only.\n\n**The legal floor.** Article 4 of the EU AI Act has required providers and deployers to address AI literacy since 2 February 2025, and it reaches any organization within the Act's scope. As amended by the Digital Omnibus, from 27 July 2026 it is a duty to take measures to support literacy rather than to guarantee a level of it. Read the change carefully, because it is exactly the kind of date-and-status distinction the exam tests: the obligation did not disappear, its standard moved.\n\n**Training against automation bias.** **Automation bias** is the documented human tendency to over-trust machine output, and it is why oversight that exists on paper still fails. It is the practical face of Chapter 1's point that AI outputs are probabilistic. The FTC's Rite Aid order is the clearest enforcement example: the system produced flags, staff acted on them, and the Commission's case turned on the absence of reasonable safeguards around a system whose errors fell disproportionately on women and people of colour. Training is the cheapest countermeasure, because users who understand error modes, confidence and a system's limits are materially better overseers. Interface design carries the other half, by making disagreement and override easy.\n\nHuman oversight also has a legal minimum, and \"solely automated\" is the hinge. In Drivers v. Uber and Ola the Amsterdam Court of Appeal found the ride-hailing companies in breach of the GDPR over decisions made by opaque algorithms that managed, fined and deactivated drivers. The case is worth knowing for two reasons: it shows that purported human review is examined for substance rather than accepted on assertion, and it shows that an oversight claim which survives scrutiny depends on the reviewer having the authority, the information and the time to disagree.\n\n**Measuring and maintaining.** Attendance counts are not evidence of literacy. Better measures are pre- and post-assessment, the share of staff trained within a stated window, the number of AI projects with complete documentation, and the volume of risk escalations raised by trained staff. That last one is a leading indicator rather than a lagging one: it shows whether people believe reporting is safe. A literacy program with zero escalations has not been tested.\n\n**Change management.** Workforce readiness goes beyond knowledge. New AI systems change workflows, job content and required skills, and change handled badly generates resistance that later surfaces as **shadow AI**: employees using unapproved tools because the approved path is too slow. Samsung's May 2023 ban on employee use of external chatbots is the standard illustration. An engineer had uploaded internal source code to a public tool, and the company's response was a blanket prohibition. Read as governance, the episode shows both halves of the problem: the leak is an acceptable-use failure, and the reason staff went around the approved path is a readiness failure. A prohibition treats the symptom. Readiness work is therefore governance work, not a communications exercise.\n\n### 2.4 Roles Along the Value Chain\n\nEvery owner named so far works for the organization. Many of the decisions that shape its systems are made by others, and two vocabularies describe who does what along that chain. Keeping them apart is one of the most reliably tested distinctions in Domain I.\n\n**The BOK's vocabulary describes tasks.**\n\n- A **developer** (data scientists, machine learning engineers, data engineers, research scientists) handles data curation and rights, model selection and training, fairness and robustness evaluation, documentation and testing.\n- A **provider** places the system on the market under its own name, which the BOK says can include a team that fine-tunes someone else's base model.\n- A **deployer** (software engineers, AI operations, product managers, infrastructure) handles fit-for-context assessment, integration, human oversight in operation, monitoring, incident escalation and user instructions.\n- A **user** (internal staff, external customers, decision-makers relying on output) operates within intended bounds, applies judgment, reports anomalies and exercises recourse.\n\nThe BOK deliberately calls developer and deployer **task labels, not legal categories**. One organization can be the developer of one system and the deployer of another, because the labels describe what you are doing rather than what you legally are. Questions about task labels are answered in terms of tasks.\n\n**The EU AI Act's categories carry legal duties.** Its **provider** develops a system or has it developed and places it on the market or puts it into service under its own name or trademark, whether or not anything was fine-tuned. A company that builds its own application on another party's general-purpose model is that application's **downstream provider**, and the general-purpose model's own provider owes it information under Article 53.\n\n| Role | BOK task label | EU AI Act category and core duties |\n|---|---|---|\n| Developer | Builds and tests the model | No separate category |\n| Provider | Places the system on the market under its own name | Places on the EU market or puts into service under its own name: risk management, data governance, technical documentation, conformity assessment, CE marking, registration, post-market monitoring, incident reporting |\n| Deployer | Integrates, oversees and monitors in context | Uses the system under its authority in a professional capacity: use per instructions, competent human oversight, relevant input data, monitoring, log retention, serious-incident reporting, and a FRIA for some |\n| User | Relies on output within intended bounds | No separate category |\n| Importer | None | Places a third-country provider's system on the EU market; verifies the conformity assessment, documentation and CE marking |\n| Distributor | None | Makes a system available unmodified; verifies CE marking and documentation, and does not supply a system it believes non-compliant |\n\nProvider and deployer are where the vocabularies overlap and the duties diverge. The same organization can hold both roles for the same system. A subsidiary that has a system developed by group data science and then puts it into service under its own name is its **provider** under Article 3(3), and also its **deployer** because its own staff use it. Where that system is high-risk, the deployer side carries a **fundamental rights impact assessment (FRIA)** under Article 27. The FRIA is narrower than it sounds: it binds only public bodies, private entities providing public services, and deployers of Annex III points 5(b) and 5(c) systems. It applies from 2 December 2027 as amended, 2 August 2026 as enacted.\n\n#### When a deployer becomes a provider\n\nUnder Article 25(1), any distributor, importer, deployer or other third party becomes the **provider** of a high-risk system, with all of a provider's obligations, in three situations.\n\n- **Point (a):** it puts its own name or trademark on a high-risk system.\n- **Point (b):** it substantially modifies a high-risk system that remains high-risk.\n- **Point (c):** it changes the intended purpose of a system that was not high-risk, including a general-purpose system, so that it becomes high-risk.\n\nThe rule turns what looks like a deployment question into a conformity question. It is why rebranding a vendor's tool, or pointing a general-purpose model at a new use case, can move an entire set of obligations onto the party that did it. Point (c) is the one exam writers reach for, because the trigger is a change of purpose rather than a change of code.\n\n#### Why the split matters\n\nMost of the risk that downstream parties inherit is set upstream, in training data, architecture and thresholds. Most of the harm happens downstream, in context, where the deployer decides how the output is used and who oversees it. The role model exists so that neither party can point at the other, and so that a governance professional can say who owes what to whom. Accountability follows the role, and outsourcing a system does not outsource accountability.\n\n### 2.5 Fitting the Program to the Organization\n\nThe BOK frames Domain I as holding regardless of industry, sector or size, so the same structures have to be scaled to an organization's size, sector, maturity, jurisdictions and risk profile.\n\n**Proportionality runs by system, not by company.** A high-consequence system earns the full RACI and stakeholder map. A low-consequence one earns a register line and a named owner. Treating every system identically is as much a failure as treating none of them seriously, because it spends the governance budget where the risk is not.\n\n**Jurisdictions stack.** An organization operating in several jurisdictions carries layered law, and the duties attach per entity and per role rather than to the group as a whole.\n\n**Maturity sets the ceiling.** A mid-sized firm early in its AI maturity can support a council but not yet an ethics officer.\n\n**Small organizations need less structure and the same discipline.** A forty-person startup might have no council at all: an accountable founder, a responsible engineer, and a monthly review with outside counsel. It would still need one owner per decision, a written record, and training sized to the team. Nothing in the structures above depends on an organization being large or regulated in a particular sector. The same council, RACI and curriculum would work at a hospital, a retailer or a software company, which is exactly why Domain I is examined as sector-agnostic.\n\n### Exam Focus\n\n- Domain I is tested as sector-agnostic, and an answer that assumes a bank or a particular industry is usually wrong.\n- Know what each layer owns: direction owns appetite, funding, tone and final accountability; steering owns the framework, high-risk review, escalation and upward reporting; operating owns building, assessing, monitoring, documenting and flagging anomalies.\n- A Chief AI Ethics Officer is a maturity signal and never a legal requirement.\n- RACI is the instrument the BOK's \"define roles and responsibilities\" indicator points at, with exactly one Accountable party per task, Consulted parties heard before the decision, and Informed parties told of it.\n- A DPO or privacy function is Consulted, never Accountable, on processing it monitors, because GDPR Article 38 requires independence.\n- Stakeholder mapping is a control, because an unidentified stakeholder is an unowned risk, and external stakeholders are the ones most often missed.\n- The mature pattern tags AI risk inside the existing ERM portfolio, so a parallel AI register is the wrong answer.\n- Training covers AI terminology, strategy and governance for all stakeholders, with baseline awareness for all and depth by role, and it is judged by effectiveness measures rather than attendance.\n- Article 4 of the EU AI Act has required AI literacy measures since 2 February 2025, and the Digital Omnibus softened the standard from 27 July 2026 without removing the duty.\n- Provider was added to the BOK's roles in v2.1. Keep the BOK's developer, provider, deployer and user apart from the AI Act's provider, deployer, importer and distributor, and remember that task labels are not legal categories.\n- The Article 25(1) deemed-provider triggers (name or trademark, substantial modification, changed intended purpose) reach distributors, importers and other third parties as well as deployers, and they turn a deployment question into a conformity question.\n- The Article 27 FRIA binds only public bodies, private entities providing public services, and deployers of Annex III 5(b) and 5(c) systems.\n- Human oversight is examined for substance, not for its existence, and \"solely automated\" is the hinge.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe following terms are used in this chapter and defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): Adoption, AI Governance Stakeholder, Cross-Functional Collaboration, RACI Matrix, Stakeholder Mapping, Training and Awareness Program, AI Deployer, AI Developer, AI User, Automation Bias, Shadow AI, Provider, Downstream Provider.\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/): GV1 to GV6, roles, accountability, culture and third-party governance\n- [ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)](https://www.iso.org/standard/42001): clause 5 on leadership and Annex A organizational controls\n- [NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf): AI actors and the Govern function as the cross-cutting foundation\n- [EU AI Act, Article 4 (AI literacy)](https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-4): the legal expectation, in force 2 February 2025\n- [EU AI Act, Article 3 (definitions of provider, deployer, importer, distributor)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/): the legal role definitions\n- [EU AI Act, Article 25 (responsibilities along the AI value chain)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/): the rule that turns deployers into providers\n- [EU AI Act, Article 27 (fundamental rights impact assessment)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/27/): who owes a FRIA and when\n- [EU AI Act, Article 38 (position of the data protection officer)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-38-gdpr/): independence, and why a DPO is Consulted rather than Accountable\n- [Drivers v. Uber and Ola (Amsterdam Court of Appeal, 4 April 2023)](https://fountaincourt.uk/2023/04/amsterdam-court-upholds-appeal-in-algorithmic-decision-making-test-case-drivers-v-uber-and-ola/): automated decisions, opaque algorithms and the limits of asserted human review\n- [FTC v. Rite Aid Corporation](https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/cases-proceedings/2023190-rite-aid-corporation-ftc-v): automation bias, safeguards and the five-year facial recognition ban\n- [Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme (report, 7 July 2023)](https://robodebt.royalcommission.gov.au/publications/report): an automated system whose unlawful decisions had no accountable owner\n- [Bauserman v. Michigan Unemployment Insurance Agency](https://stpp.fordschool.umich.edu/research/policy-brief/case-over-michigan-unemployment-insurance-agencys-faulty-automated-system): a public body's automated fraud detection and its $20m settlement\n- [IAPP Key Terms for AI Governance](https://iapp.org/resources/article/key-terms-for-ai-governance/): stakeholder and role terminology\n\n## Chapter 3: Policies Across the Life Cycle\n\nChapter 1 gave the organization a vocabulary, and Chapter 2 gave its decisions owners. This chapter completes Part I with the rulebook, the subject of BOK competency I.C, establishing policies and procedures across the life cycle.\n\nMost governance failures are not caused by a broken rule. They are caused by a rule written for a world that has changed. Three assumptions run through most policy binders, and AI breaks all three: that a human makes the decision, that data sits in a database, and that a vendor sells what the contract describes. This chapter takes human oversight first (3.1), then privacy, security, data-governance and intellectual-property policy (3.2), then third-party risk (3.3), and closes by drawing the three together (3.4).\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Drivers v. Uber and Ola | Amsterdam Court of Appeal, 4 April 2023; non-compliance ruling 5 October 2023, EUR 584,000 | Human in the loop, examined and rejected |\n| Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme | Final report tabled 7 July 2023 | Review that was ritual rather than real |\n| Bauserman v. Michigan UIA | Class settlement finally approved 30 January 2024, USD 20 million | An automated determination treated as final |\n| Samsung generative AI restriction | Reported 2 May 2023 | Shadow AI and the acceptable-use policy |\n| FTC v. Evolv Technologies | FTC complaint and stipulated order, 26 November 2024 | Vendor claims, and where the harm landed |\n| Garante v. DeepSeek | Garante limitation order, 30 January 2025 | Supply concentration reaching a whole market at once |\n| Moffatt v. Air Canada | 2024 BCCRT 149 | Accountability cannot be delegated |\n\n### 3.1 Human Oversight by Design\n\nAutonomy bites here. The more a system can act without asking, the more deliberately the human has to be placed, and placement is a design decision rather than a staffing one.\n\nThe EU High-Level Expert Group on AI set out three levels of oversight, and they remain the working vocabulary.\n\nUnder **human-in-the-loop** oversight, a person reviews and approves each consequential decision before it takes effect. It suits high-stakes, lower-volume decisions: medical triage, loan denial, custody or benefits determinations. Under **human-on-the-loop** oversight, a person supervises operation, monitors outputs and can intervene or halt. It suits higher-volume, moderate-risk operation where per-decision review is impractical. Under **human-in-command** oversight, people set the objectives, bounds and escalation rules and keep the ability to override or deactivate. That is overall program governance and the fallback for every deployed system.\n\nStakes and volume decide between the first two, per use case. The third applies to everything.\n\n#### Where the law reaches the design\n\nTwo EU instruments turn these levels into obligations, and they reach different parties.\n\nGDPR **Article 22** restricts decisions based solely on automated processing that produce legal effects or similarly significantly affect a person. For oversight design, \"solely automated\" is the hinge: meaningful human review before the decision takes effect moves a process outside the article, and cosmetic review does not. Chapter 4 covers the exceptions, the safeguards and the case law, including the point from the SCHUFA ruling that a score an underwriter draws on strongly may itself be the decision rather than an input to it.\n\nEU AI Act **Article 14** requires high-risk systems to be designed so that competent humans can effectively oversee them: able to understand the outputs, to disregard or override them, and to interrupt the system through a stop button or similar procedure. That is a design duty and it sits with the **provider**. The deployer's duty to assign oversight to people with the necessary competence, training and authority sits in **Article 26(2)**. Both apply from 2 December 2027 as amended by the Digital Omnibus on AI, and from 2 August 2026 as enacted (Chapter 6). Check every date question for which of those two it means.\n\nNeither article protects a US applicant. The American hook runs through the adverse-action reasons that ECOA and Regulation B require, which Chapter 5 covers. The instruments differ, the practical demand does not: someone has to be able to say why.\n\n#### What makes oversight real\n\nArticle 22 speaks of human intervention rather than human presence, and the difference is the whole section. A human who rubber-stamps every recommendation satisfies no obligation and creates a worse problem than no human at all, because the review now supplies legal cover for a decision the machine has already made. Call it **automation bias with a witness**.\n\nOversight needs four conditions at once. Lose any one and the witness returns.\n\n- **Competence.** The overseer understands the system's limits and failure modes. This is what the literacy work in Chapter 2 buys.\n- **Information.** The interface presents confidence and reasons, not only a recommendation. A score with no basis cannot be meaningfully reviewed.\n- **Authority.** The overseer can reverse the output without career cost, which in practice means overrides must be removed from productivity measures.\n- **Time and volume.** A review load small enough for real attention. An overseer with three hundred files a day is not reviewing, they are clearing.\n\nThe record that proves all four is an override log, reviewed on a schedule by someone with the authority to act on what it shows.\n\nTwo decisions make the failure modes concrete. In **Drivers v. Uber and Ola**, the Amsterdam Court of Appeal found the companies in breach of the GDPR over opaque algorithmic decisions that managed, fined and deactivated drivers, and it rejected reliance on a human in the loop because the human role was not meaningful. A later ruling ordered EUR 584,000 in penalty payments for continued non-compliance. The pair is the authority that a nominal human presence does not satisfy an oversight duty. The **Robodebt** Royal Commission reached the same place from the public sector: an automated income-averaging program raised debts across a large population, many of them wrong, and the Commission traced the harm to governance rather than to the algorithm, finding that those who approved and ran it never tested whether it was lawful and that the human review was ritual rather than real.\n\n### 3.2 Retrofitting Policy for AI\n\nThe BOK makes two things preconditions for AI-specific policy, and states both directly: a **data classification policy**, so you know what is sensitive, and a **data inventory**, so you know where it is. Without them, monitoring and managing AI training data is severely disadvantaged, and no amount of policy language compensates. Most organizations discover the gap late, when they find that \"prompt\" and \"model output\" were never classification categories and that the inventory stops at the database rather than following data into an analytics environment.\n\n#### Where legacy policy breaks\n\nSeven areas, each with the assumption AI breaks and the gap to close.\n\n| Policy area | The assumption AI breaks | The gap to close |\n|---|---|---|\n| Purpose limitation | Data collected for one purpose stays there | A compatibility analysis or new lawful basis before training on repurposed data; a registry tagging each dataset with its approved purposes |\n| Retention | Deleting the record deletes the data | Rules covering derived data, checkpoints, embeddings, caches and the trained model itself, plus a stated position on what a deletion request means for a model |\n| Minimization | Minimization applies to storage | Minimization applied to features and training sets: if fewer features perform equivalently, use fewer |\n| De-identification | Removing names anonymizes | A standard that survives quasi-identifier combinations and model-based re-identification, with re-anonymization required when datasets are combined |\n| Access control | Access is to databases | Access control over prompts, model endpoints, training environments, feature stores and model registries |\n| Incident response | Incidents mean a breached database | Playbooks for the six AI-specific threats below |\n| Intellectual property | The organization knows what it owns and has licensed | Rules on who owns outputs under vendor terms, what confidential material may enter third-party prompts, and what licensed content may be used in training |\n\nTwo rows repay attention. **Retention** is where AI policy most often contradicts itself, because an erasure request can reach a **checkpoint** (a saved snapshot of a model's parameters) and an **embedding** (a numerical representation of text used to compare meaning), and the organization has to state a position rather than discover one. **De-identification** is where confidence most often outruns reality: an \"anonymized\" extract still carrying ZIP code, birth date and loan amount carries three **quasi-identifiers**, fields that name no one alone but can single a person out in combination. Removing names pseudonymizes rather than anonymizes, as Chapter 4 explains.\n\n#### Threats the playbooks must anticipate\n\nSix threats, and the taxonomy is worth learning in its three groups.\n\nThree target what a model has learned. **Model inversion** reconstructs training inputs by probing the model's outputs. **Membership inference** determines whether a specific person's record was in the training set, which is itself a disclosure: revealing that someone was a customer, or was declined, says something about them. **Unintended memorization** is a model reproducing fragments of sensitive training data on request, such as a customer's details.\n\nTwo are attacks with an actor. **Data poisoning** corrupts training data to shape model behavior, a training-time attack. **Prompt injection** is input crafted at inference time to override a large language model's instructions or safety filters, delivered directly or indirectly through retrieved content, such as an uploaded document carrying instructions of its own.\n\nOne needs no adversary at all. **Function creep** expands data use beyond its stated purpose through gradual reuse, as when service transcripts drift into marketing. Nothing malicious happens; the boundary simply moves.\n\nConfabulation, a falsehood with no attacker behind it, is a different failure and belongs with the model's behaviour rather than its security (Chapter 7).\n\n#### Shadow AI and acceptable use\n\nThe most common real-world AI policy failure involves no sophisticated attack. An employee pastes customer data into an unapproved tool because it is the fastest way to finish the task.\n\nSamsung's restriction on employee generative AI use, reported on 2 May 2023, is the standard case. Engineers had uploaded proprietary source code to a public chatbot, and the company restricted generative AI tools on company devices and networks, describing the measure as temporary while it built a secure alternative. Read as governance, the episode has two halves: the leak is an acceptable-use failure, and the reason staff used an unapproved path is a readiness failure. That is why a ban is the wrong answer on its own. A prohibition without a sanctioned alternative does not remove the behavior, it drives it to personal devices and out of sight, which is worse than the exposure the ban was meant to prevent.\n\nAn **acceptable use policy** (AUP) therefore has to answer three questions: what data may enter a prompt, which tools are approved, and what happens when someone needs a tool that is not on the list. That third question is the one most AUPs omit and the one that determines whether the policy holds. The durable control pairs the rule with a sanctioned tool and an exception route with a named owner.\n\n### 3.3 The Supply Chain Problem\n\nStandard **third-party risk management** (TPRM) was built for a different kind of product. The checks it runs are sound and mostly irrelevant here. Solvency, insurance and certification checks miss training data of unknown origin or rights status. Standard security questionnaires miss model behavior that changes with each vendor update. Service contract templates leave liability for harmful or discriminatory output unallocated. Named-subcontractor disclosure misses the sub-tier of data labelers, base-model suppliers and open-source dependencies.\n\nThis is **emergence** in commercial form: a black box inside a black box. The organization cannot inspect the training data and may not be told when the model version changes. Point-in-time diligence decays fast against a product like that, because the thing being assessed is not the thing that will be running next quarter.\n\nTwo cases show the exposure. **FTC v. Evolv Technologies** concerned an AI weapons screening system whose detection capability was overstated while schools buying it were locked into multi-year contracts; the proposed order bars unsupported AI claims and lets affected K-12 customers cancel. The harm landed on the deployers, because schools relied on the vendor's performance claims for the safety of children. **Garante v. DeepSeek** shows the other direction: a single regulator's limitation order on a model provider reached every downstream deployer and user in Italy at once. Model supply is a governance dependency, not only a procurement choice.\n\n#### Rebuilding vendor review\n\nFour parts, and they run in sequence.\n\n**One: flag AI at intake.** Ask whether the product includes AI, machine learning, large language models or automated decision-making, whether your data will train the vendor's models, and whether personal, biometric or sensitive data is processed. Flagged deals get supplemental review by legal, risk and technical stakeholders. Vendors do not all volunteer their AI use, so the question has to be asked rather than inferred from the product description.\n\n**Two: run AI-specific due diligence.** Training-data origin and rights, fairness and robustness test evidence, explainability support, model-change practices, security of the machine learning supply chain, and named sub-tier dependencies, meaning who labeled the data and whose base model sits underneath. A standardized supplier questionnaire mapped to risk tiers keeps that repeatable. Where the vendor is a general-purpose AI model provider, also seek the documentation it owes downstream providers under EU AI Act **Article 53**.\n\n**Three: put terms in the contract.** Six clauses carry the weight.\n\n1. **Data-use limits:** no customer data used for retraining without explicit consent.\n2. **Fairness and performance:** warranties, plus evidence of testing and remediation of disparate impact.\n3. **Model-change notification:** advance notice of updates that change behavior or intended use.\n4. **Incident notification:** time-bound reporting and defined responsibilities. A 24-hour window is a negotiated term, not a legal rule, and treating it as one is an error.\n5. **Audit rights:** the right to audit or inspect high-risk vendor systems and to receive documentation, exercised in proportion to risk.\n6. **Termination and portability:** exit conditions, model deactivation rights, and data return or deletion.\n\n**Four: monitor continuously.** Vendor model updates change behavior mid-contract, so diligence is a recurring process rather than a gate. The emerging instrument for seeing the sub-tier chain is an **AI system bill of materials** (AI-BOM) listing a system's upstream inputs: the model and its version, the embedding model, the retrieval corpus, and the open-source libraries. Open-source components need the same licensing, vulnerability and bias review as commercial ones.\n\n#### Accountability cannot be delegated\n\nIf the system misstates a fee, the organization answers to the customer and the regulator, not to its vendor. **Moffatt v. Air Canada** is the canonical illustration: when a chatbot gave a passenger incorrect information about bereavement fares, the airline argued the chatbot was a separate legal entity responsible for its own actions, and the British Columbia Civil Resolution Tribunal rejected that argument outright.\n\nThe EU AI Act reaches the same result structurally, attaching obligations to whoever places a system on the market or uses it rather than to whoever built it furthest upstream. Upstream builders keep duties of their own, and those relieve the downstream party of none of its own. Outsourcing the build does not outsource the responsibility, and \"the vendor is responsible because it built the model\" is a distractor rather than a defence.\n\n### 3.4 Three Assumptions, Three Fixes\n\nLegacy policy assumed that a human decides, that data stays in a database, and that a vendor sells what the contract describes. Each assumption has a matching repair. Oversight design makes review real by giving the human competence, information, authority and time. The policy retrofit follows data into prompts, checkpoints and embeddings, and reaches features rather than only storage. Vendor review keeps asking after signature, through intake flagging, diligence, contract terms and continuous monitoring.\n\nThe three repairs share one shape: a named owner, a stated rationale, and a record revisited when the system, the vendor or the law changes. That last clause is what separates a governance program from a policy binder. Every control in this chapter has a version number because every control here is expected to be rewritten.\n\nPart II takes up the question the rulebook raises but cannot answer. Which of these rules does the law actually require, and of whom? An Irish applicant, a Colorado borrower and a customer in Seoul do not live under the same rules, and nothing in a register says which is which.\n\n### Exam Focus\n\nCompetency I.C carries the largest share of Domain I items.\n\n- Human-in-the-loop approves each decision before it takes effect, human-on-the-loop supervises and can intervene, and human-in-command, which applies to every system, sets the bounds and keeps the kill switch.\n- Stakes and volume choose between the first two levels, per use case.\n- Under GDPR Article 22 \"solely automated\" is the hinge: meaningful review before the decision takes effect moves a process outside the article, and cosmetic review does not.\n- EU AI Act Article 14 is the provider's design duty and Article 26(2) is the deployer's staffing duty. Neither they nor GDPR Article 22 protect US applicants.\n- Real oversight needs competence, information, authority, and time and volume at once. Lose one and the result is automation bias with a witness.\n- The BOK states directly that a data classification policy and a data inventory must exist before AI-specific policy can work, and classification categories usually stop short of prompts and model outputs.\n- Retrofitted minimization reaches features, and retention reaches checkpoints, embeddings and the trained model.\n- Inversion reconstructs inputs, membership inference reveals presence in the training set, and memorization reproduces fragments. Poisoning strikes at training, prompt injection at inference, and function creep needs no attacker.\n- A ban without an approved path produces shadow AI, so the right answer pairs acceptable-use rules with a sanctioned tool and an exception route. A restriction alone does not remove the behavior.\n- The vendor response runs intake flagging, AI-specific diligence, contract clauses and continuous monitoring, and intake must ask about AI rather than infer it.\n- Standard third-party checks (solvency, insurance, SOC 2, generic security questionnaires) do not test training-data rights, model-change behavior or sub-tier dependencies.\n- A 24-hour vendor incident-notification window is a negotiated term, not a legal rule.\n- Accountability is non-delegable, and \"the vendor built the model, so the vendor is responsible\" is a distractor.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe following terms are used in this chapter and defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): Automation, Human in the Loop, Human Oversight, AI Data Retention Policy, Membership Inference Attack, Model Inversion Attack, Prompt Injection, Shadow AI, AI Vendor Due Diligence, Sub-Tier Dependencies, Third-Party Risk Management (TPRM).\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [EU AI Act, Article 14 (human oversight)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/): the oversight design requirements for high-risk systems\n- [EU AI Act, Article 26 (obligations of deployers)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/): the deployer's oversight staffing duty in Article 26(2)\n- [GDPR Article 22 (automated individual decision-making)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-22-gdpr/): the right and its safeguards\n- [EU High-Level Expert Group on AI, Ethics Guidelines for Trustworthy AI (2019)](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/ethics-guidelines-trustworthy-ai): the source of the in-the-loop, on-the-loop and in-command levels\n- [ICO: explaining decisions made with AI](https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/): what meaningful human involvement looks like in practice\n- [NIST AI RMF Playbook](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/): Govern and Manage actions on human oversight and override\n- [ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839): controllability and predictability as trustworthiness vocabulary\n- [NIST AI 100-2, Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2023.pdf): the structured attack taxonomy: poisoning, evasion, privacy, abuse\n- [OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications](https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/): prompt injection, insecure output handling and the LLM attack surface\n- [MITRE ATLAS](https://atlas.mitre.org/): adversarial threat landscape for AI systems, MITRE ATT&CK style\n- [EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2024-12/edpb_opinion_202428_ai-models_en.pdf): lawful basis, purpose limitation and the consequences of anonymity in model training\n- [ICO guidance on AI and data protection](https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/guidance-on-ai-and-data-protection/): retention, minimization and rights in an AI context\n- [The Secret Sharer: unintended memorization in neural networks](https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08232): the research behind the memorization and extraction risk\n- [CISA artificial intelligence resources](https://www.cisa.gov/ai): secure development and supply-chain guidance\n- [NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1), third-party and value-chain risk](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf): GOVERN 6 and the Map function on dependencies\n- [EU AI Act, Article 25 (responsibilities along the AI value chain)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/): how obligations follow the value chain\n- [EU AI Act, Article 53 (obligations for providers of general-purpose AI models)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/53/): the documentation a model provider owes downstream\n- [ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)](https://www.iso.org/standard/42001): Annex A controls for third-party relationships\n- [NIST AI 600-1, value-chain and component integration risk](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf): the generative-AI version of third-party risk\n- [Drivers v. Uber and Ola (Amsterdam Court of Appeal, 4 April 2023)](https://apps.eurofound.europa.eu/platformeconomydb/amsterdam-court-of-appeal-rules-uber-violated-data-protection-rights-110150): robo-firing, and a human-in-the-loop defence rejected\n- [Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme (report, 7 July 2023)](https://www.royalcommission.gov.au/robodebt/report): ritual review and an unlawful automated program\n- [Bauserman v. Michigan Unemployment Insurance Agency](https://www.michigan.gov/ag/news/press-releases/2024/01/30/class-action-settlement-approved-by-court-of-claims): an automated fraud determination treated as final\n- [Samsung restricts staff use of generative AI (CNBC, 2 May 2023)](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/05/02/samsung-bans-use-of-ai-like-chatgpt-for-staff-after-misuse-of-chatbot.html): shadow AI and the acceptable-use policy\n- [FTC v. Evolv Technologies](https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2024/11/ftc-takes-action-against-evolv-technologies-deceiving-users-about-its-ai-powered-security-screening): overstated vendor claims and deployer exposure\n- [Garante v. DeepSeek (order of 30 January 2025)](https://www.garanteprivacy.it/home/docweb/-/docweb-display/docweb/10097450): a single order reaching every downstream deployer\n- [Moffatt v. Air Canada, 2024 BCCRT 149](https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bccrt/doc/2024/2024bccrt149/2024bccrt149.html): accountability is not delegable to a separate legal entity\n\n# Part II: Laws, Standards and Frameworks\n\n*What must we do, and how do we prove it?*\n\nThe second domain is where the exam stops asking what AI governance is and starts asking what the law requires. It has two halves that reward different skills. Chapters 4 and 5 cover law that already reached AI without naming it, where the work is seeing the hook: which existing obligation a new use case has just landed on. Chapters 6 and 7 cover law written for AI and the standards that operationalize it, where the work is knowing the mechanism and which instrument does what.\n\n### Exam at a Glance\n\nDomain II carries 19 to 23 of the 100 scored items, the third-heaviest domain, behind only Domains III and IV. Its four competencies map onto this part's chapters.\n\n| Competency | What it covers | Questions | Chapter |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| II.A How existing data privacy laws apply to AI | Transparency, choice, lawful basis. Controller duties, including automated decision-making | 4 to 6 | 4 |\n| II.B How other existing laws apply to AI | IP, non-discrimination, consumer protection, product liability | 4 to 6 | 5 |\n| II.C Main elements of AI-specific laws | EU AI Act, South Korea's AI Basic Act, US federal and state AI laws | 6 to 8 | 6 |\n| II.D Main industry standards and tools | OECD, NIST AI RMF and Playbook, ISO/IEC 22989, 42001, 42005 | 3 to 5 | 7 |\n\nII.A and II.B reward seeing the hook, II.C knowing the mechanism, and II.D knowing which instrument does what. BOK v2.1, approved on 9 September 2025, changed the domain in three ways.\n\n- II.C was renamed from \"the main elements of the EU AI Act\" to \"the main elements of AI-specific laws\", its weight rose from 5 to 7 questions to 6 to 8, and the domain introduction now names South Korea's AI Basic Act and US federal and state AI laws.\n- II.A's first indicator became \"transparency, choice, lawful basis\", widening the focus from consent to every GDPR Article 6 basis, and controller obligations now name automated decision-making explicitly.\n- II.D dropped NIST ARIA and added ISO/IEC 42005, making the core ISO three 22989, 42001 and 42005, and its weight fell from 4 to 6 questions to 3 to 5.\n\nBecause v2.1 was approved before the Omnibus was agreed, a live item may quote either timeline. Read the stem for \"as originally enacted\" or \"as amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus\", and if there is no anchor, ask whether the tested fact changed at all. Work each matching problem as instrument, role, duty, and look for the one detail in the fact pattern that decides the instrument. Precision about dates and status is worth as much as the rules themselves.\n\n**Chapters.** Chapter 4 takes privacy law, from the GDPR's automated-decision rules to lawful basis, minimisation, controller duties and special-category data. Chapter 5 takes the laws that already reached AI without naming it: intellectual property, non-discrimination, consumer protection and product liability. Chapter 6 covers laws written for AI, anchored by the EU AI Act, with South Korea's AI Framework Act and US federal and state measures beside it. Chapter 7 turns to the voluntary OECD, NIST and ISO frameworks that regulators, auditors and contracts increasingly treat as the measure of due care.\n\n## Chapter 4: Privacy Law Meets AI\n\nPrivacy law is the floor under every AI governance program. The GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679, regulated AI for the five years before the AI Act existed, and the AI Act now sits beside it rather than above it: the GDPR governs processing of personal data, the AI Act the placing of a system on the market and into service. A high-risk technical file does not cure an unlawful basis, and a chatbot outside every risk category can still breach a transparency duty.\n\nTwo properties of AI press hardest. Appetite: development wants volume and variety, and it wants data collected for something else, which purpose limitation and minimisation restrain (4.4, 4.5). Inference: a model can produce a fact about a person, an ethnic origin or a health condition, that no form asked for and no field recorded (4.1, 4.3). Scope decides whether the rest applies: Article 3 reaches controllers and processors in the EU, and those outside it that offer goods or services to people in the EU or monitor their behaviour there, so a model trained elsewhere but served in the EU raises a transfer question (4.10).\n\n- Section 4.1: the framework, and the personal data definition.\n- Section 4.2: controller, processor and joint controller.\n- Section 4.3: the six lawful bases and special category data.\n- Section 4.4: purpose limitation and the compatibility test.\n- Section 4.5: minimisation and accuracy in features and training sets.\n- Section 4.6: Articles 12, 13 and 14, and what a notice cannot carry.\n- Section 4.7: the DPIA, the Article 35(4) list and the FRIA.\n- Section 4.8: Article 22, and the score that is itself the decision.\n- Section 4.9: the data subject rights that meet a model.\n- Section 4.10: transfers after Schrems II.\n- Section 4.11: enforcement and the DPO.\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| SCHUFA | Court of Justice, Case C-634/21, 7 December 2023 | A score that is itself the decision, and duties that travel up the vendor chain |\n| Dun and Bradstreet Austria | Court of Justice, Case C-203/22, 27 February 2025 | What an explanation under Article 15(1)(h) must contain |\n| Garante v OpenAI | Garante decision No. 755 of 2 November 2024, EUR 15 million; annulled by the Court of Rome, judgment No. 4153/2026 | Transparency enforcement, and its volatility on appeal |\n| Garante v Luka Inc. (Replika) | Garante order of 10 April 2025, EUR 5 million | No legal basis identified, and design duties on a consumer generative product |\n| Clearview AI | Garante decision of 10 February 2022, EUR 20 million; Dutch DPA decision of 16 May 2024, EUR 30.5 million | Scraped biometric data, and parallel enforcement |\n| Garante v Foodinho | Garante order of 5 July 2021; further order of 22 November 2024, EUR 5 million | Impact assessment and contestability in algorithmic management |\n| Meta Ireland transfers | Irish Data Protection Commission decision IN-20-8-1, 22 May 2023, EUR 1.2 billion | Transfers outside the EEA, and the safeguards required after Schrems II |\n\n### 4.1 The GDPR as the Framework the AI Act Sits Beside\n\nPersonal data is any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person. A full postcode with a birth date names nobody alone, but **quasi-identifiers** of that kind let a model single someone out from an extract no one would call identifying.\n\n**Pseudonymized** data keeps a keyed reference and stays personal data; **anonymous** data sits outside the GDPR, and re-identification must be impossible by the means reasonably likely to be used by anyone, not only by the organization holding the data. Opinion 28/2024, adopted on 17 December 2024, applies that standard to models, anonymous only where it is insignificantly likely that a training subject's data could be extracted, directly or by querying the model.\n\nPersonal data includes data derived from other data: an attribute a model predicts is personal data about that person, and where it falls in Article 9 the prohibition is engaged by the prediction rather than only by collection (4.3).\n\n### 4.2 Controller, Processor and Joint Controllership\n\nA **controller** determines the purposes and means of processing (Article 4(7)); a **processor** acts on the controller's behalf (Article 4(8)); the test looks at who decides in fact. Where a provider uses the customer's data to improve its own models it has determined a purpose of its own, so the parties are **joint controllers** under Article 26 for that processing, whatever the agreement says. Controllership tends toward a platform the customer cannot control, and Article 28(10) makes a processor that exceeds its instructions a controller for that processing. Joint controllership needs no joint notice: the arrangement binds the parties, not the data subject, who may request against either. The AI Act's provider and deployer are not the GDPR's controller and processor (Chapter 6).\n\n### 4.3 Lawful Basis, and the Special Category Prohibition\n\nArticle 6 lists six **lawful bases** with no hierarchy: consent, contract necessity, legal obligation, vital interests, public task, legitimate interests. **Legitimate interest**, Article 6(1)(f), is the basis most training runs on, and the least safe one: it requires a documented three step test of a real and present interest, necessity, and a balancing exercise in which the data subject's reasonable expectations carry real weight, the standard Opinion 28/2024 applies.\n\n**Special category data** works the other way round: Article 9 begins with a prohibition, not a permission. The categories are racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, trade union membership, genetic data, biometric data used to uniquely identify a person, health data, and sex life or sexual orientation. **Clearview AI** built a facial recognition database by scraping images; Italy fined it EUR 20 million in 2022 for biometric data with no valid basis and a commercial interest that did not qualify as legitimate, and the Netherlands fined it EUR 30.5 million in 2024 on comparable findings. **Garante v Luka Inc.** (Replika), at EUR 5 million in April 2025, is the generative case: no legal basis identified, an inadequate privacy policy, and no age verification at registration or in use.\n\n### 4.4 Purpose Limitation and the Compatibility Test\n\n**Purpose limitation**, Article 5(1)(b), requires data collected for specified, explicit and legitimate purposes and not further processed incompatibly. Training is a separate purpose from collection, and separate again from inference, the use of a trained model. An organization that collects data to deliver a service and later trains on it has two lawful routes: show the new purpose is compatible, or rest it on a fresh basis and inform the people concerned under Article 13(3).\n\nArticle 6(4) sets the compatibility factors: the link between the two purposes, the context of collection and the relationship with the person, the nature of the data, the consequences of the further processing, and the safeguards in place. Article 9 applies to the secondary use as well, which narrows the answer for special category data.\n\n**Function creep** is the failure this doctrine exists to catch: data drifts from one feature to the next, and service transcripts move into model tuning. The control that holds is a register tagging each dataset with its approved purposes.\n\n### 4.5 Minimisation and Accuracy in Features and Training Sets\n\n**Minimisation**, Article 5(1)(c), asks for personal data that is adequate, relevant and limited to what is necessary. The discipline runs at every stage: take what the purpose requires at collection, drop features that contribute nothing and mask or remove sensitive fields before training, and keep the input to the set the decision needs.\n\nAn unused field widens the breach surface and imports proxies: postcode or account history correlate with characteristics the organization must not use to decide (Chapter 5). Data masking, **tokenization**, **pseudonymization**, on-device processing, **federated learning** and **differential privacy** all reduce exposure, and none makes data anonymous on its own; **synthetic data** carries the same caution.\n\n**Accuracy**, Article 5(1)(d), concerns the data and its currency rather than a performance metric: a model trained on a stale or mislabelled history inherits both defects. Article 5(1)(e) forbids keeping data in identifiable form longer than the purpose requires.\n\n### 4.6 Transparency: Articles 12, 13 and 14\n\n**Article 12** requires information to be concise, transparent, intelligible, easily accessible and in clear and plain language. **Article 13** governs information collected from the person, and **Article 14** governs information obtained anywhere else, within a month unless providing it proves impossible or would involve disproportionate effort.\n\nThe practical instrument is a **layered notice**, whose first layer states the fact that changes the reader's position. A notice supplies no lawful basis and cannot substitute for the explanation owed to an individual about a particular decision (4.9).\n\n**Garante v OpenAI** is the worked example of transparency enforcement: the decision of 20 December 2024 imposed a EUR 15 million fine and a six month public information campaign for breaches of the transparency principle and information duties, plus the absence of age verification. The Court of Rome annulled the fine in judgment 4153/2026, so it is cited as an annulled enforcement, not a standing penalty.\n\n### 4.7 The DPIA and the Fundamental Rights Impact Assessment\n\nA **data protection impact assessment** (DPIA) is required by Article 35 where processing is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons. Article 35(3) names three triggers: systematic and extensive automated evaluation of personal aspects, including profiling, with decisions that produce legal effects or similarly significantly affect a person; large scale special category processing; and systematic monitoring of a publicly accessible area on a large scale. The operational trigger is the **Article 35(4) list**: each authority publishes the operations requiring a DPIA, and those lists add items the article does not.\n\nThe assessment has four parts under Article 35(7): a description of the processing and its purposes, an assessment of necessity and proportionality, the risks to data subjects, and the measures to address them. Article 35(9) requires the controller to seek the views of data subjects where appropriate. The recurring AI failures are a boundary drawn round the model rather than the processing, and technical risk described instead of human impact.\n\n**Foodinho**: the Italian authority fined the company EUR 2.6 million in 2021 over a rider management algorithm covering roughly 19,000 riders, finding breaches of Articles 35 and 37(7): no procedure for riders to obtain human intervention or contest a decision that could remove them from work.\n\nA **fundamental rights impact assessment** (FRIA) under Article 27 of the AI Act is the deployer's, covering non-discrimination, freedom of expression and the rights of the child; the DPIA is the controller's and protects personal data.\n\n### 4.8 Automated Decision-Making Under Article 22\n\n**Article 22** gives a person the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing, including profiling, which produces legal effects concerning them or similarly significantly affects them, unless the decision is necessary for a contract, is authorised by law with suitable safeguards, or is based on explicit consent. Those exceptions carry the Article 22(3) safeguards: human intervention, the right to express a point of view, and the right to contest the decision.\n\nThe hinge is the phrase **solely automated**: a process moves outside the article where a human genuinely intervenes before the decision takes effect, and stays inside where the human is nominal. What Article 22 adds is that the human must be able to change the outcome.\n\n**SCHUFA**, Case C-634/21, decided by the Court of Justice on 7 December 2023: a credit information agency's automated probability value was itself a decision within Article 22(1) where the lender drew strongly on it when establishing, implementing or terminating a contract, so the agency, and not only the lender, had to find an exception and provide the safeguards. Duties travel up the chain to whoever produces the score: no party escapes by adding a step.\n\n### 4.9 Data Subject Rights in an AI Context\n\n| Right | Article | The AI problem |\n|---|---|---|\n| Access | 15 | Explaining a decision whose logic is distributed across weights |\n| Rectification | 16 | Correcting a record whose influence is already in the model |\n| Erasure | 17 | Deleting an influence rather than a row |\n| Restriction | 18 | Pausing use inside a system that keeps learning |\n| Objection | 21 | Profiling is intrinsic to many systems rather than incidental |\n| Automated decisions | 22 | Obtaining human intervention that is real |\n\nAccess carries the most developed case law: Article 15(1)(h) entitles a person subject to an automated decision to meaningful information about the logic involved, and about the significance and envisaged consequences of the processing. **Dun and Bradstreet Austria**, Case C-203/22, decided on 27 February 2025, supplies the standard: a concise and intelligible explanation of the procedure and the principles actually applied, sufficient for the person to understand and to challenge the outcome. The Court rejected an algorithm dump and a summary of general categories, and sends claimed trade secrets to the authority or court to balance.\n\nDeleting a row is straightforward; removing its influence from a trained model is not, and the regulation says neither that erasure reaches the model nor that it cannot. What it requires is a documented position rather than a silent assumption.\n\n### 4.10 International Transfers After Schrems II\n\nOutside the EEA, Articles 44 to 49 govern. The lawful routes are an **adequacy decision**, finding essentially equivalent protection; **standard contractual clauses** (SCCs) or another Article 46 instrument, which move protection with the data; binding corporate rules; and the narrow Article 49 derogations, which are no plan for routine operations.\n\nRemote access counts: where a team outside the EEA can reach data held inside it, that access is itself a transfer, as the Board's Guidelines 05/2021 treat as settled. For the United States, the adequacy decision for the EU-US Data Privacy Framework of July 2023 covers importers certified under it.\n\nAfter **Schrems II**, Case C-311/18 of 16 July 2020, an Article 46 instrument is a starting point rather than an answer. The controller must carry out a **transfer impact assessment**: identify the law and practice of the destination that may impinge on the safeguards, decide whether they are effective, and adopt supplementary measures where they are not, as the Board sets out in Recommendations 01/2020.\n\n**Meta Ireland** is not an AI case, and that is the point: Ireland's Data Protection Commission fined Meta EUR 1.2 billion in May 2023 over the legal basis for transferring EU user data to the United States, and ordered the transfers suspended.\n\n### 4.11 Enforcement, Supervisory Authorities and the DPO\n\n**National supervisory authorities** enforce the GDPR in their own member state, with a one stop shop lead authority for cross-border processing. The **fine tiers** in Article 83 are up to EUR 10 million or 2 percent of total worldwide annual turnover, whichever is higher, and up to EUR 20 million or 4 percent for the most serious infringements.\n\nThe **data protection officer** sits inside this picture, and independence is the tested point. **Article 38(3)** provides that the officer shall not receive instructions regarding the exercise of their tasks, shall not be dismissed or penalised for performing them, and shall report directly to the highest level of management. **Article 38(6)** allows other tasks and duties only where they do not result in a conflict of interest, so an officer who also owns the processing decisions they monitor cannot give independent advice. Foodinho's order included a finding under Article 37(7) on the officer's position.\n\n### Exam Focus\n\nThese points belong to competency II.A, whose first indicator names transparency, choice and lawful basis, and whose controller obligations name automated decision-making explicitly.\n\n- The six Article 6 bases have no hierarchy, so \"consent is the strongest basis\" is a distractor. Consent fails wherever the service cannot realistically be refused.\n- Legitimate interest is the usual basis for training and the one with the heaviest documentation: interest, necessity, balancing, and a right to object under Article 21. An undocumented balancing test is an assertion, not a basis.\n- Article 9 is a prohibition with listed exceptions, not a permission, and it is engaged by a special category attribute a model infers, not only by one that was collected.\n- Purpose limitation: training is a separate purpose from collection, and secondary use needs either a compatibility assessment under the Article 6(4) factors or a fresh basis plus notice under Article 13(3).\n- Minimisation reaches features and training sets, not only storage; if fifteen features perform like fifty, use fifteen. Accuracy concerns the data and its currency, not the model's scores.\n- Article 14 reaches data obtained from a third party or scraped, and its disproportionate effort exemption must be documented.\n- A notice cannot create a lawful basis, cannot make an incompatible purpose compatible, and cannot replace the Article 15(1)(h) explanation of an individual decision.\n- The DPIA triggers are in Article 35(3), but the operational trigger is the authority's Article 35(4) list. A DPIA is the controller's and protects personal data; the AI Act Article 27 FRIA is the deployer's and covers a wider set of fundamental rights. Neither substitutes for the other.\n- Article 22 turns on \"solely automated\", the exception has to be one of the three in Article 22(2), and the Article 22(3) safeguards are human intervention, expressing a view, and contesting the decision.\n- SCHUFA (C-634/21) makes an upstream score an Article 22 decision where the downstream party draws strongly on it, so scoring vendors and model providers owe those duties.\n- Dun and Bradstreet Austria (C-203/22) sets the Article 15(1)(h) standard: a concise, intelligible explanation of the logic actually applied, not an algorithm dump, with trade secrets balanced by the authority or the court.\n- Erasure has no settled automatic answer for a trained model, so the answer to look for is a documented position rather than a silent assumption.\n- Remote access from outside the EEA is a transfer, and after Schrems II an SCC or a BCR needs a transfer impact assessment and supplementary measures where the safeguards are ineffective.\n- Enforcement patterns to recognise: parallel action by several authorities (Clearview AI), volatility on appeal (the annulled OpenAI fine), and process findings that outlast the penalty (Foodinho).\n- Under Article 38(3) the DPO takes no instructions, cannot be dismissed or penalised for the role, and reports to the highest management level; Article 38(6) bars conflicting duties.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe following terms are used in this chapter and defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): Choice, Consent, Notice, Secondary Use, Deidentification, Differential Privacy, Federated Learning, Privacy, Privacy by Design, Automated Decision-Making (ADM), Data Leak, Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA), Data Subject Access Request (DSAR), Dun & Bradstreet Austria Ruling (C-203/22), SCHUFA Ruling (C-634/21), Personally Identifiable Information (PII), Anonymization, Article 22 GDPR, Article 35 GDPR, Article 9 GDPR, Biometric Data, Breach Notification, Cross-Border Data Transfer, Data Controller, Data Minimization, Data Protection Officer (DPO), Data Subject Rights, EDPB Guidelines 03/2026 on Web Scraping, EDPB Opinion 28/2024, General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), Pseudonymization, Purpose Limitation, Right to Erasure, Special Category Data, Third-Party Processor.\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj): the text of Articles 5, 6, 9, 12 to 18, 21, 22, 25, 28, 35, 38, 44 to 49 and 83\n- [EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/opinion-of-the-board-art-64/opinion-282024-on-certain-data-protection-aspects-related-to_en): the anonymity test for a trained model, and the three step legitimate interest test\n- [EDPB Guidelines 03/2026 on web scraping in the context of generative AI (draft)](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/public-consultations/guidelines-032026-on-web-scraping-in-the-context-of-generative-ai_en): why consent does not scale for a training corpus, and when legitimate interest is the usual basis\n- [EDPB Guidelines 07/2020 on the concepts of controller and processor](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/guideline/guidelines-072020-on-the-concepts-of-controller-and-processor-in-the-gdpr_en): the functional test for controllership, and joint controllership\n- [EDPB Guidelines 05/2021 on the interplay between Article 3 and Chapter V](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/guideline/guidelines-052021-on-the-interplay-between-the-application-of-article-3-and-the_en): remote access as a transfer\n- [EDPB Recommendations 01/2020 on measures that supplement transfer tools](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/our-work-tools/our-documents/recommendations/recommendations-012020-measures-supplement-transfer_en): the transfer impact assessment after Schrems II\n- [European Commission, EU-US data transfers](https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/international-dimension-data-protection/eu-us-data-transfers_en): the adequacy decision for the Data Privacy Framework\n- [EU AI Act, Article 27 (fundamental rights impact assessment)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/27/): what a FRIA covers, and why it sits beside a DPIA rather than inside it\n- [ICO guidance on AI and data protection](https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/guidance-on-ai-and-data-protection/): minimisation, retention, accuracy and rights in an AI context\n- [ICO: explaining decisions made with AI](https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/): what a meaningful explanation contains in practice\n- [SCHUFA, Case C-634/21 (CJEU, 7 December 2023)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62021CJ0634): a score the lender draws on strongly is itself an Article 22 decision\n- [Dun and Bradstreet Austria, Case C-203/22 (CJEU, 27 February 2025)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=celex:62022CJ0203): the Article 15(1)(h) standard for explaining an automated decision\n- [Garante v OpenAI (decision announced 20 December 2024, annulled by the Court of Rome, judgment 4153/2026)](https://www.garanteprivacy.it/home/docweb/-/docweb-display/docweb/10085432): transparency enforcement against a generative service, and its annulment\n- [Garante v Luka Inc. (Replika), order of 10 April 2025](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/news/ai-the-italian-supervisory-authority-fines-company-behind-chatbot-replika_en): no legal basis identified, and design duties on a consumer generative product\n- [Clearview AI (Garante, 10 February 2022)](https://www.garanteprivacy.it/home/docweb/-/docweb-display/docweb/9751323): scraped biometric data, and the failure of a commercial legitimate interest\n- [Garante v Foodinho, order of 5 July 2021](https://www.garanteprivacy.it/home/docweb/-/docweb-display/docweb/9677377): Article 35 and contestability in algorithmic management\n- [Garante v Foodinho, further order of 22 November 2024](https://www.garanteprivacy.it/home/docweb/-/docweb-display/docweb/10074840): geolocation and the contestability of algorithmic decisions\n- [Meta Ireland transfers (Irish Data Protection Commission, 22 May 2023)](https://www.dataprotection.ie/news-media/press-releases/Data-Protection-Commission-announces-conclusion-of-inquiry-into-Meta-Ireland): transfers outside the EEA, and a binding decision driving the outcome\n\n## Chapter 5: Existing Law That Reaches AI\n\nThese are the laws that never mention artificial intelligence and reach it anyway: copyright, employment discrimination, fair lending, consumer protection and product liability. None mentions model, algorithm or training data; they apply because the conduct they regulate is not new.\n\n\"There is no AI law here\" never means \"no law here.\" A US borrower declined by an AI underwriter has no GDPR rights, and has ECOA rights instead. A vendor that promises its model always gets the answer right answers to the FTC Act, and a model trained on scraped books answers to copyright.\n\nThe four bodies of law arrive together on a single use case. Domain II.B is the competency this chapter carries; Chapter 6 sits on top.\n\n- Section 5.1 covers intellectual property: training as reproduction and distribution, the fair use four factors, output ownership, and the split on whether a model contains copies.\n- Section 5.2 covers non-discrimination: Title VII's two theories, the four-fifths rule as a screening heuristic, ECOA and Regulation B adverse-action reasons, and discrimination through a proxy rather than an attribute.\n- Section 5.3 covers consumer protection: FTC Act section 5 deception and unfairness, claim substantiation, UDAAP and the state analogues.\n- Section 5.4 covers product liability: whether software is a product, strict liability and the defect test, and the EU Product Liability Directive 2024/2853.\n- Section 5.5 gives the order of operations: where to look first when a new AI use case appears.\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bartz v. Anthropic PBC | N.D. Cal., No. 3:24-cv-05417; fair use order 23 June 2025; settlement finally approved 20 July 2026 | Fair use for training, and unlawful acquisition of the corpus |\n| Thomson Reuters v. Ross Intelligence | D. Del., No. 1:20-cv-613-SB, opinion 11 February 2025 | Market substitution, and where a commercial training use fails |\n| Getty Images v. Stability AI | [2025] EWHC 2863 (Ch), 4 November 2025 | A model is not a copy of its training data; copyright is territorial |\n| Thaler v. Perlmutter | D.C. Cir., No. 23-5233, 18 March 2025; certiorari denied 2 March 2026 | Human authorship and output ownership |\n| GEMA v. OpenAI | Regional Court of Munich I, 42 O 14139/24, 11 November 2025 | Memorisation as reproduction, and the limits of the TDM exception |\n| New York Times Co. v. Microsoft | S.D.N.Y., No. 1:23-cv-11195-SHS, complaint 27 December 2023 | Training and output claims pleaded together |\n| Mobley v. Workday | N.D. Cal., No. 3:23-cv-00770-RFL, collective certification 16 May 2025 | Title VII and ADA exposure from a vendor's screening tool |\n| Obermeyer et al. health algorithm study | Science, 366(6464), 25 October 2019 | Discrimination through a proxy and a label, not an attribute |\n| NYDFS Apple Card report | New York Department of Financial Services, 23 March 2021 | Fair lending review of a third-party underwriting model |\n| FTC Operation AI Comply | FTC press release, 25 September 2024 | Deceptive AI capability claims under section 5 |\n| FTC v. Workado | FTC order, 28 April 2025 | Substantiation of an accuracy claim |\n| Directive (EU) 2024/2853 | Revised Product Liability Directive, adopted 23 October 2024 | Software as a product, and defect in an adaptive system |\n| Benavides v. Tesla | S.D. Fla., No. 1:21-cv-21940, verdict 1 August 2025 | Defective driver-assistance software, and fault allocation |\n\n### 5.1 Intellectual Property\n\nTraining a model is copying, so the ordinary copyright questions follow: was the copy authorised, does an exception apply, does an output infringe, who owns it. Three touchpoints follow: the **inputs**, the **model** and the **outputs** (infringement or ownership).\n\n#### Training, and the split from acquisition\n\nTraining reproduces the corpus, so copyright splits into two questions courts keep apart: whether training is lawful, and whether the acquisition was lawful. US courts test training under the four **fair use** factors: purpose and character of the use (transformative or duplicative), nature of the work, amount used, and effect on the market, including dilution by competing output.\n\n**Bartz v. Anthropic PBC** held that training on lawfully acquired books was transformative fair use, but refused to excuse the separate downloading and retention of a pirated library; it settled for USD 1.5 billion, approved 20 July 2026.\n\n**Thomson Reuters v. Ross Intelligence** went the other way: no fair use for training a legal research tool on Westlaw headnotes, because Ross built a market substitute and the fourth factor, market harm, weighed against it.\n\n#### The model: a transatlantic divergence\n\n**Getty Images v. Stability AI** (English High Court) held that diffusion model weights are not an \"infringing copy\" of the training images, because they store no recognisable copy of a work.\n\n**GEMA v. OpenAI** (Regional Court of Munich) went the other way: lyrics memorised in the model parameters and reproduced in outputs and chat histories were reproductions. The TDM exception covers building the training corpus, not memorisation, and responsibility for outputs from simple prompts sits with the operator.\n\n#### Text and data mining\n\nThe EU has no fair use; Directive (EU) 2019/790 provides two **text and data mining** (TDM) exceptions. Article 3 covers research organisations and cultural heritage institutions, with no opt-out. Article 4 covers anyone with lawful access, including commercial training, unless the rightholder has reserved the right \"in an appropriate manner, such as machine-readable means\" (Article 4(3)). AI Act Article 53(1)(c) and (d) require general-purpose model providers to honour those reservations and publish a training-content summary, and open-source models are not exempt.\n\n#### Output ownership and human authorship\n\n**Thaler v. Perlmutter** holds that the Copyright Act requires a work authored in the first instance by a human being, so an AI system cannot itself be the author; certiorari was denied. US Copyright Office guidance adds that prompts alone are not authorship, and that AI-generated material must be disclosed.\n\n#### Trade secrets and open-source licences\n\n**Trade secret** law protects datasets, architecture, hyperparameters, fine-tuning methods and prompts only while they are genuinely secret, so transparency duties collide with it. The Defend Trade Secrets Act (18 U.S.C. 1836) and Directive (EU) 2016/943 require secrecy, value from it, and reasonable guarding.\n\n### 5.2 Non-Discrimination\n\nUS law has two theories, and AI triggers both. **Disparate treatment** is intentional discrimination, including deliberate use of a variable that stands in for a protected class. **Disparate impact** needs no intent: a facially neutral practice that disproportionately harms a protected group is unlawful unless justified.\n\n#### Employment and Title VII\n\nTitle VII writes disparate impact into the statute at section 703(k) (42 U.S.C. 2000e-2(k)) as three steps: the plaintiff shows a particular practice causes the impact; the employer shows it is job-related and consistent with business necessity; the plaintiff can still win by showing a refused, less discriminatory alternative. The standard is statutory, so an executive order cannot remove it.\n\nThe **four-fifths rule** (Uniform Guidelines on Employee Selection Procedures, 29 CFR 1607.4) is a screening heuristic, not a legal standard: a selection rate for a protected group below four-fifths, or 80 percent, of the rate for the highest group is generally regarded as evidence of adverse impact. It flags a disparity for investigation, not a clearance.\n\n**Mobley v. Workday** conditionally certified a nationwide collective alleging that an AI-based applicant screening system discriminated on race, age and disability: the employer is exposed for the tool it adopts even when a vendor built it.\n\n#### The Americans with Disabilities Act\n\nTitle I of the Americans with Disabilities Act (42 U.S.C. 12112) prohibits discrimination against a qualified individual with a disability and requires reasonable accommodation unless it imposes undue hardship. AI screening implicates two duties: a qualification standard that screens out a person with a disability must be job-related, consistent with business necessity and not capable of being met with reasonable accommodation; and a person disadvantaged by an automated screen may be entitled to the interactive process.\n\n#### Credit, adverse action and fair lending\n\nThe **Equal Credit Opportunity Act** (ECOA, 15 U.S.C. 1691) prohibits discrimination in credit on protected bases and places two duties on an AI lender: no intentional discrimination or deliberate proxy use, and specific principal reasons for adverse action under Regulation B (12 CFR 1002.9). The second is an explainability duty: the reasons must relate to and accurately describe the factors actually considered.\n\nThe **NYDFS Apple Card report** reviewed underwriting data for about 400,000 applicants after complaints that an underwriter gave women lower credit limits, and found no fair lending violation.\n\n#### Proxies and labels\n\nA **proxy** is a neutral variable that correlates with a protected class, and discrimination can also enter through the choice of the target label. The **Obermeyer** study shows the mechanism: a widely used commercial risk-scoring algorithm predicted health care costs rather than illness, so at any given risk score Black patients were measurably sicker than White patients, and correcting the target was estimated to raise the share identified for extra care from 17.7 percent to 46.5 percent.\n\n#### The federal pullback\n\nFederal agencies stepped back from disparate-impact enforcement across 2025 and 2026: Executive Order 14281 (23 April 2025) directed agencies to minimise disparate-impact liability, and a CFPB final rule effective 21 July 2026 states that ECOA does not provide for the effects test. Federal ECOA disparate-impact liability is therefore gone, while intentional discrimination, deliberate proxies, private plaintiffs, states and the Fair Housing Act route all remain.\n\n### 5.3 Consumer Protection\n\nSection 5(a) of the FTC Act (15 U.S.C. 45(a)(1)) declares unfair or deceptive acts or practices in commerce unlawful. An **unfair** act causes or is likely to cause substantial injury that is not reasonably avoidable and not outweighed by benefits (section 45(n)). A **deceptive** act misleads a reasonable consumer through a material representation or omission, and neither requires intent.\n\n**Operation AI Comply** (25 September 2024) brought five FTC actions on the position that there is no AI exemption from the laws already on the books. **FTC v. Workado** (28 April 2025) required competent and reliable evidence for a claimed 98 percent accuracy rate for an AI content detector. On 22 December 2025 the FTC set aside its Rytr order for failing to support a section 5 violation, while stating it would still pursue AI used to deceive consumers: what narrowed was the theory, not the duty to substantiate.\n\nAny performance claim about an AI system is a regulatory exposure until it is backed by evidence: a claim the organisation cannot substantiate is a deceptive act whether or not the product is AI. Undisclosed AI interaction in a legal, health or financial context is itself a material omission.\n\nThe **UDAAP** regime is the consumer-finance analogue: the CFPB enforces the prohibition on unfair, deceptive or abusive acts or practices under Dodd-Frank sections 1031 and 1036 (12 U.S.C. 5531 and 5536). Abusiveness covers an act that materially interferes with a consumer's ability to understand a term or condition, or takes unreasonable advantage of a lack of understanding.\n\nEvery US state has a mini-FTC act, a **UDAP** law: Massachusetts Chapter 93A allows multiple damages for deceptive conduct, and New York's General Business Law section 349 prohibits deceptive acts and practices.\n\n### 5.4 Product Liability\n\nProduct liability recognises three **defect theories**: a **design defect** means the product was made as intended but its design is unreasonably dangerous; a **manufacturing defect** means the unit deviates from the intended design; a **failure to warn** (a marketing defect) means the product lacks adequate instructions or warnings, any of which can run through **strict liability** (no negligence needed), **negligence** or **breach of warranty**. Defect is often judged by a **risk-utility test**: was the risk outweighed by the utility, and could a reasonable alternative design have reduced it?\n\n#### Is software a product?\n\nThe traditional US view treats software as a licensed service, which keeps most AI outside strict products liability. The EU settles the point for its own market: Directive (EU) 2024/2853 brings software, including AI systems, within the definition of a product \"irrespective of the mode of its supply or usage\", and AI system providers under the AI Act are treated as manufacturers.\n\n#### The revised Product Liability Directive\n\nThe Directive was adopted on 23 October 2024 and must be transposed by 9 December 2026, applying to products placed on the market after that date. Liability is no fault: the claimant proves defect, damage and causation, and it covers death and personal injury, including psychological harm, property damage, and corruption of non-professional data. Defect is presumed where the defendant fails to disclose evidence, the product breaches mandatory safety requirements, or there is an obvious malfunction; causation where the damage is typical of the defect; and either or both where technical or scientific complexity makes proof excessively difficult, with machine learning a named example.\n\n#### Defect in an adaptive system\n\nAdaptive systems make defect hard to locate: behaviour is dynamic, models drift after deployment, and outputs are probabilistic. The Directive weighs \"the effect on the product of any ability to continue to learn or acquire new features after it is placed on the market.\"\n\n**Benavides v. Tesla** is the worked example of a defect theory against an automated feature: a Florida jury found Tesla partly at fault in a fatal crash involving its Autopilot driver-assistance feature, with reporting placing Tesla's share at about USD 243 million of a total award of roughly USD 329 million; Tesla has appealed. The claimant proved only defect, damage and causation.\n\n#### The withdrawn AI Liability Directive\n\nThe Commission proposed a fault-based AI Liability Directive and withdrew it (OJ C/2025/5423, 6 October 2025), so an injured person relies on Directive (EU) 2024/2853 and national tort law, and any analysis resting on an \"AI Liability Directive presumption\" rests on an instrument that does not exist.\n\n### 5.5 Where to Look First\n\nWhen a new AI use case appears, start with the conduct, not the technology: find the statute that already regulates it, then establish your role and where the affected person is.\n\n1. **Name the regulated conduct, not the system.** Copying a work (intellectual property), selecting or assessing a person (employment, credit or housing), making a claim to a customer (consumer protection), placing something on the market (product liability).\n2. **Establish which party you are and where the affected person sits.** Provider, deployer, employer, lender, advertiser and manufacturer each carry a different duty, and copyright is territorial.\n3. **Inventory the inputs.** How was the training data acquired and licensed, is there a machine-readable opt-out to honour, and can an output be explained?\n4. **Test for proxies and labels, not attributes.** Discrimination usually enters through a neutral variable that stands in for a protected class.\n5. **Substantiate every performance claim before launch, and fix the liability allocation in the contract.** The bar on contracting out protects injured persons, so cost may be allocated between commercial parties but not away from the person harmed.\n\nThe four bodies of law apply whether or not any AI-specific law does. Chapter 6 turns to the statutes that do mention AI, and asks which entity is the provider?\n\n### Exam Focus\n\n- The four bodies of law in this chapter are intellectual property, non-discrimination, consumer protection and product liability, and none of them names AI.\n- Copyright asks a different question at each touchpoint: inputs (lawful copying for training), model (whether it contains copies), outputs (infringement or ownership).\n- Bartz and Ross split on the same question: training was fair use on the Bartz record and not fair use in Ross, and in Bartz the pirated acquisition failed even though training was fair.\n- Market substitution under the fourth fair use factor is where a commercial training use most often fails.\n- Getty (weights are not a copy) conflicts with GEMA (memorisation is a reproduction), and copyright is territorial, so where training happened decides the law.\n- The EU has no fair use: DSM Article 3 (research and cultural heritage, no opt-out) differs from Article 4 (anyone, commercial use allowed, subject to a machine-readable opt-out).\n- Thaler holds that an AI system cannot be an author, prompts alone are not authorship, and human selection, arrangement and modification of AI output can be protected.\n- Disparate treatment requires intent, including deliberate proxy use, while disparate impact needs none.\n- Title VII's three steps are causation, business necessity and a refused less discriminatory alternative, and the standard is statutory, so an executive order cannot remove it.\n- The four-fifths rule is a screening heuristic, not a legal standard: it flags a disparity for investigation and is not a clearance.\n- Under ECOA and Regulation B, \"failed to achieve a qualifying score\" is not a sufficient adverse-action reason, because the reasons must describe the factors actually considered.\n- Discrimination can enter through a proxy or the choice of the label, without any protected attribute in the feature set, as the Obermeyer study showed.\n- Unfair acts cause substantial, unavoidable injury not outweighed by benefits, deceptive acts mislead through material representations or omissions, and neither requires intent.\n- The CFPB's Regulation B rule (effective 21 July 2026) states that ECOA does not provide for the effects test, but intentional and proxy discrimination remain unlawful and the FHA impact route survives.\n- The revised Product Liability Directive covers software and AI as a product, weighs continued learning in the defect test, presumes defect or causation where complexity makes proof excessively difficult, and excludes purely economic loss.\n- Any answer resting on an \"AI Liability Directive presumption\" is wrong, because the AILD was withdrawn on 6 October 2025.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe following terms are used in this chapter and defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): Copyright, Fair Use, Fair Use (AI Training), Human Authorship Requirement, Intellectual Property, Machine-Readable Opt-Out (TDM), Patent, Text and Data Mining Exception, Trade Secret, Adverse Action Notice (ECOA/Reg B), Disparate Impact, Disparate Treatment, Four-Fifths Rule, Non-Discrimination Law, Reasonable Accommodation, Consumer Protection, UDAAP, UDAP, Deception (FTC Act Section 5), Unfairness (FTC Act Section 5), AI Liability Directive (Withdrawn), Design Defect, Product Liability, Product Liability Directive (EU) 2024/2853, Proxy Variable, Strict Liability.\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [Bartz v. Anthropic PBC, order on fair use (N.D. Cal., 23 June 2025)](https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.cand.434709/gov.uscourts.cand.434709.231.0_5.pdf): training as transformative fair use, with acquisition a separate exposure\n- [Thomson Reuters Enterprise Centre v. Ross Intelligence (D. Del., 2025)](https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/17131648/thomson-reuters-enterprise-centre-gmbh-v-ross-intelligence-inc/): no fair use where the training use builds a market substitute\n- [Getty Images v. Stability AI [2025] EWHC 2863 (Ch)](https://www.judiciary.uk/judgments/getty-images-v-stability-ai/): model weights that store no works are not an infringing copy, and copyright is territorial\n- [Thaler v. Perlmutter, D.C. Cir. opinion (18 March 2025)](https://media.cadc.uscourts.gov/opinions/docs/2025/03/23-5233.pdf): the human authorship requirement; certiorari denied 2 March 2026\n- [GEMA v. OpenAI, Munich Regional Court I (11 November 2025)](https://cms.law/en/deu/legal-updates/gema-vs.-openai-munich-regional-court-i-issues-landmark-copyright-decision): memorisation as reproduction, and the limit of the TDM exception\n- [New York Times Co. v. Microsoft (S.D.N.Y., complaint 27 December 2023)](https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/68117049/the-new-york-times-company-v-microsoft-corporation/): training and output copyright claims pleaded together\n- [US Copyright Office, Copyright and Artificial Intelligence](https://www.copyright.gov/ai/): the human authorship and disclosure guidance\n- [Directive (EU) 2019/790 (DSM Directive)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/790/oj): the Article 3 and Article 4 text and data mining exceptions and the Article 4(3) opt-out\n- [Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (EU AI Act), Article 53](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj): the general-purpose model copyright policy and training-content summary duties\n- [Defend Trade Secrets Act, 18 U.S.C. 1836](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1836): the federal trade secret definition and remedies\n- [Directive (EU) 2016/943 on trade secrets](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2016/943/oj): the EU definition of a trade secret and the secrecy condition\n- [Open Source Initiative, licences](https://opensource.org/licenses): permissive and copyleft licence terms that travel with an integrated component\n- [Mobley v. Workday, collective certification (N.D. Cal., 16 May 2025)](https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.cand.408645/gov.uscourts.cand.408645.128.0.pdf): Title VII and ADA exposure through a vendor's screening tool\n- [Obermeyer et al., Dissecting racial bias in a health algorithm, Science (25 October 2019)](https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aax2342): discrimination through a proxy and a label\n- [NYDFS report on the Apple Card investigation (23 March 2021)](https://www.dfs.ny.gov/reports_and_publications/press_releases/pr202103231): assurance review of a third-party underwriting model\n- [Title VII section 703(k), 42 U.S.C. 2000e-2](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/2000e-2): the statutory disparate-impact standard that an executive order cannot remove\n- [Uniform Guidelines on Employee Selection Procedures, 29 CFR 1607](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/subtitle-B/chapter-XIV/part-1607): the four-fifths rule and the adverse impact screening heuristic\n- [Americans with Disabilities Act title I, 42 U.S.C. 12112](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/12112): qualification standards and the reasonable accommodation duty\n- [Equal Credit Opportunity Act, 15 U.S.C. 1691](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/1691): the prohibition on credit discrimination\n- [CFPB Regulation B, 12 CFR 1002.9](https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1002/9/): the adverse-action notification and principal reasons requirement\n- [Executive Order 14281 (90 FR 17537)](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/04/28/2025-07378/restoring-equality-of-opportunity-and-meritocracy): the April 2025 order to deprioritise disparate-impact enforcement\n- [CFPB Regulation B final rule (91 FR 21620)](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/04/22/2026-07804/equal-credit-opportunity-act-regulation-b): effective 21 July 2026; ECOA does not provide for the effects test\n- [FTC Act section 5, 15 U.S.C. 45](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/45): the unfair-or-deceptive standard, with the unfairness test at section 45(n)\n- [FTC, Operation AI Comply (25 September 2024)](https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2024/09/ftc-announces-crackdown-deceptive-ai-claims-schemes): the crackdown on deceptive AI capability claims\n- [FTC, Workado order (28 April 2025)](https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2025/04/ftc-order-requires-workado-back-artificial-intelligence-detection-claims): unsubstantiated AI accuracy claims must be backed by evidence\n- [FTC, Rytr order set aside (22 December 2025)](https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2025/12/ftc-reopens-sets-aside-rytr-final-order-response-trump-administrations-ai-action-plan): the theory was set aside; deception about AI remains enforceable\n- [Dodd-Frank Act sections 1031 and 1036, 12 U.S.C. 5531](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/12/5531): the unfair, deceptive or abusive acts or practices prohibition\n- [Directive (EU) 2024/2853 (revised Product Liability Directive)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2024/2853/oj/eng): software and AI are products; no-fault liability; presumptions; transpose by 9 December 2026\n- [Benavides v. Tesla, Inc. (S.D. Fla., verdict 1 August 2025)](https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/59932667/benavides-v-tesla-inc/): defective driver-assistance software and the allocation of fault\n- [AI Liability Directive withdrawal, OJ C/2025/5423](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/C/2025/5423/oj): the fault-based proposal was formally withdrawn on 6 October 2025\n\n## Chapter 6: The Law Written for AI\n\nChapter 5 showed that \"no AI law here\" never means \"no law here\". This chapter turns to the law that names AI: the EU Artificial Intelligence Act sorts systems into risk tiers, loads a compliance chain onto high-risk uses, imposes transparency duties on systems that talk to people or make content, and regulates general-purpose models separately. Beside it sit South Korea's framework statute and the US federal and state patchwork.\n\nThe Act exists in two versions. **As enacted**, it is Regulation (EU) 2024/1689. **As amended** by the Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 in force 27 July 2026, it moved the high-risk dates and simplified several duties; where they differ this chapter says which it means, and 6.10 sets the two calendars side by side.\n\nDuties attach to roles, not organizations, and one organization can hold several roles on one system. They also attach to dates, so an AI compliance plan is a calendar before it is a control list.\n\n- 6.1 sets out the risk-based architecture, and 6.2 and 6.3 cover the Article 5 prohibitions and the two routes into the high-risk tier.\n- 6.4 to 6.6 cover what high-risk status obliges: provider duties, deployer duties including the fundamental rights impact assessment, and the deemed-provider rule.\n- 6.7 to 6.9 cover general-purpose AI, Article 50 transparency, and penalties and enforcement; 6.10 sets the two calendars.\n- 6.11 to 6.13 turn to South Korea, the United States, and why a US group has to care.\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| EU Artificial Intelligence Act | Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 | The risk tiers, the Article 5 list, the role-based duties |\n| Digital Omnibus on AI | Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, in force 27 July 2026 | Deferred high-risk dates, December 2026 additions |\n| AI Act prohibited practices | Article 5, Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 | The closed list, the workplace carve-out |\n| GPAI duties and the GPAI Code of Practice | Articles 53 and 54, Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 | Downstream documentation; what a code proves |\n| South Korea AI Framework Act | Act No. 20676, in force 22 January 2026 | Promotion-first statute, overlapping categories |\n| Executive Order 14365 | 90 FR 58499, signed 11 December 2025 | Federal posture; an order cannot preempt |\n| TAKE IT DOWN Act | Public Law 119-12, enacted 19 May 2025 | The 48-hour platform removal duty |\n| Colorado ADMT law | Senate Bill 26-189, signed 14 May 2026 | Repealed and reenacted SB 24-205 before it operated |\n| Texas TRAIGA | House Bill 149, effective 1 January 2026 | Intent-based bans; frameworks as a defence |\n| California frontier and transparency laws | Senate Bill 53; Senate Bill 942; Assembly Bill 2013 | Compute-based thresholds, provenance |\n\n### 6.1 The Risk-Based Architecture\n\nThe Act regulates a system together with its purpose, not AI as a technology, and sorts purposes into four tiers, with duties rising with the risk.\n\n| Tier | Test | Consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prohibited | Unacceptable risk to rights or safety | Banned outright (Article 5) |\n| High-risk | Annex I safety components, or Annex III use cases | The full Chapter III obligations chain |\n| Limited | Interaction with users, or synthetic content | Transparency duties (Article 50) |\n| Minimal | Negligible risk | No new obligations, voluntary codes encouraged |\n\n### 6.2 Prohibited Practices and the Two December Additions\n\nArticle 5 bans a closed list outright. As enacted, Article 5(1) lists eight: subliminal or purposefully manipulative techniques that distort behaviour; exploitation of vulnerabilities from age, disability, or social or economic situation; social scoring by public or private actors; individual criminal-offence risk prediction based solely on profiling; untargeted scraping of facial images for recognition databases; emotion recognition in workplaces and educational institutions; biometric categorisation by sensitive traits; and real-time remote biometric identification in publicly accessible spaces, with narrow law-enforcement exceptions.\n\nThe Digital Omnibus adds two prohibitions, Article 5(1)(ba) and (bb), from 2 December 2026: nudification (AI generating or manipulating realistic intimate or sexual imagery of an identifiable person without explicit consent) and AI generating child sexual abuse material. Each catches a provider only where that is the system's intended purpose or a foreseeable outcome without reasonable safeguards. Eight as enacted, ten as amended.\n\nEmotion recognition is banned only in workplaces and educational institutions; elsewhere it carries a deployer disclosure duty under Article 50. The prohibition regime has applied since 2 February 2025.\n\n### 6.3 Two Routes to High-Risk\n\nUnder **Annex I**, a system is high-risk where it is a safety component of a product covered by EU harmonisation law, or is itself such a product: machinery, medical devices, vehicles and similar regulated goods.\n\nUnder **Annex III**, it is high-risk where used in a listed area, whatever it is built from: biometrics; critical infrastructure (energy, water, transport); education and vocational training; employment, worker management and access to self-employment; essential private and public services including credit scoring and welfare; law enforcement; migration, asylum and border control; and the administration of justice and democratic processes.\n\nArticle 6(3) lets a listed system escape the tier where it poses no significant risk to health, safety or fundamental rights, but never where it profiles natural persons, and Article 6(4) requires the provider to document that assessment before placing the system on the market and make it available to authorities.\n\n### 6.4 Provider Duties Under Articles 8 to 21\n\nA high-risk provider owes a chain of duties from before market placement through the system's life.\n\n| Article | Duty | What it requires |\n|---|---|---|\n| 8 to 10 | Risk management, data governance | An ongoing risk-management system covering known and reasonably foreseeable risks including misuse; training, validation and testing data that are relevant, representative and as free from errors and bias as possible |\n| 11 to 13 | Documentation, logging, instructions | Technical documentation under Annex IV; automatic logging for monitoring and audit; instructions stating intended purpose, limits, oversight responsibilities, metrics and contact points |\n| 14 and 15 | Oversight, accuracy, robustness, cybersecurity | Design for anomaly detection, override or shut-off and review of consequential decisions; declared accuracy, robustness and resilience |\n| 16 to 21 | Quality management, records, corrective action | A quality management system with policies, corrective and preventive action, documentation control and reporting; records kept; authorities assisted |\n| 43, 48, 49, 72, 73 | Conformity, marking, registration, monitoring | Conformity assessed, CE marking applied, registration in the EU database; post-market monitoring and serious incident reporting |\n\nArticle 17 is the duty candidates most often underestimate: a management system duty, so a policy binder does not satisfy it. EN 18286:2026, the harmonised standard for that purpose, gives a **presumption of conformity** only once cited in the Official Journal. Conformity assessment runs under Article 43: internal control without a notified body for most Annex III systems, and a notified body for Annex I product-embedded systems and for systems where harmonised standards were not applied.\n\n### 6.5 Deployer Duties and the Fundamental Rights Impact Assessment\n\nArticle 26 deployer duties: use per the instructions for use; assign oversight to people with the necessary competence, training and authority (Article 26(2)); ensure input data relevant and sufficiently representative; monitor operation and stop use or tell the provider where a risk emerges; keep logs for the period the article sets; inform workers' representatives and affected workers before a workplace system enters service; cooperate with authorities. Articles 14 and 26(2) divide the work: the provider designs for oversight, the deployer staffs it.\n\nThe **fundamental rights impact assessment** (FRIA) in Article 27 binds exactly two groups:\n\n- bodies governed by public law, and private entities providing public services, deploying any Annex III high-risk system other than a point 2 critical-infrastructure system, and\n- any deployer of an Annex III point 5(b) or 5(c) system, being creditworthiness assessment and risk assessment and pricing in life and health insurance.\n\nA private employer deploying a high-risk recruitment tool owes no FRIA; a private lender deploying credit scoring does. It must precede first use and cover the processes and frequency of use, the categories of persons likely to be affected, the specific risks of harm to them, the oversight measures in place, and the fallbacks if those risks materialise. Article 27(4) lets it build on an existing data protection impact assessment; Chapter 12 compares the FRIA, the DPIA and an ISO/IEC 42005 assessment.\n\n### 6.6 The Deemed Provider Under Article 25(1)\n\nA deployer, importer, distributor or other third party becomes the **provider** of a high-risk system under Article 25(1) where any one of three things happens:\n\n1. it puts its own name or trademark on a high-risk system already placed on the market;\n2. it makes a substantial modification to a high-risk system already placed on the market;\n3. it modifies the intended purpose of a system, including a general-purpose AI system, so that the system becomes high-risk.\n\nThe consequence is the full provider chain: risk-management system, technical documentation, conformity assessment, CE marking and registration; the original provider must cooperate and supply what the new provider needs.\n\n### 6.7 General-Purpose AI Models\n\nA **general-purpose AI model** serves multiple intended purposes and can be integrated into a variety of downstream systems: large language models, vision foundation models and multimodal models. The Commission's July 2025 guidelines treat a model trained with more than 10^23 FLOP that can generate language, images or video as indicatively general-purpose.\n\nArticle 53 obliges every provider to prepare:\n\n- technical documentation of the model, its training and testing process and its capabilities (Article 53(1)(a), Annex XI);\n- information letting downstream providers understand its capabilities and limits and comply with their own duties (Article 53(1)(b), Annex XII);\n- a **copyright policy** honouring Article 4(3) text-and-data-mining reservations, including through state-of-the-art technologies (Article 53(1)(c));\n- a public, sufficiently detailed **training content summary** on the AI Office template (Article 53(1)(d)).\n\nArticle 53(2) lifts only points (a) and (b) for open-source models and nothing for systemic-risk models, so the copyright policy and the training summary bind open-source models too, wherever training happened. A non-EU provider must appoint an authorised representative in the Union under Article 54.\n\nSystemic risk is presumed from scale: cumulative training compute above 10^25 FLOP creates a rebuttable presumption of high-impact capabilities under Article 51(2), and the provider must notify the Commission within two weeks. Article 55(1) adds model evaluation including adversarial testing, assessment and mitigation of systemic risks, serious-incident reporting to the AI Office, and adequate cybersecurity. Adversarial testing is documented, not published; external audits, public red-team reports and researcher APIs are Code of Practice extras, not Article 55 duties.\n\nArticles 53(4) and 55(2) reserve a presumption of conformity for harmonised standards; the **GPAI Code of Practice** is a voluntary, expert-prepared tool the Commission assesses as adequate, and adherence is evidence of compliance rather than a presumption. Non-signatories must demonstrate \"alternative adequate means\".\n\nTiming follows the general-purpose track: obligations since 2 August 2025, Commission enforcement including Article 101 fines from 2 August 2026, and models placed on the market before 2 August 2025 with until 2 August 2027 under Article 111(3). An organization integrating a general-purpose model must verify the model's conformity against its published documentation, reassess whether its own use raises the risk classification, and keep its own transparency duties, even where the model is open-source.\n\n### 6.8 Transparency Under Article 50\n\nArticle 50 splits the transparency duties by role. Providers must design systems so people are told they are interacting with AI (Article 50(1)) and must mark synthetic audio, image, video or text output in a machine-readable, detectable form (Article 50(2)). Deployers must disclose deepfakes (Article 50(4)), disclose their use of emotion recognition or biometric categorisation, and flag AI-generated text published on matters of public interest unless a human reviewed it under editorial responsibility. The obligations apply from 2 August 2026, and generative systems on the market before that date have until 2 December 2026 for Article 50(2) marking, a transitional rule added by the Digital Omnibus.\n\nAn opt-out or human alternative is good practice but not an Article 50 requirement, which makes it a likely distractor. Emotion recognition outside workplaces and schools is disclosed, not prohibited: the same technology moves between the Article 5 and Article 50 regimes with where it is used.\n\n### 6.9 Penalties and Enforcement\n\nEnforcement is shared. The **European AI Office**, inside the Commission, supervises and enforces the general-purpose AI obligations under an exclusive power in Article 88, issues guidance and runs the codes of practice. **National competent authorities**, one or more per member state, conduct market surveillance for everything not reserved to the AI Office; the European Data Protection Supervisor covers AI systems used by EU institutions. As amended, the AI Office also gained exclusive competence over AI systems built on a general-purpose model by the same provider or group.\n\nMarket surveillance authorities can require access to technical documentation, carry out unannounced inspections, mandate corrective action, suspend or prohibit marketing, require withdrawal or recall, and require notification of affected users. Affected individuals can complain to a market surveillance authority, and civil liability runs through national law and the revised product liability regime Chapter 5 covers.\n\nFines follow a three-step ladder; each ceiling is the higher of a fixed sum or a percentage of total worldwide annual turnover.\n\n| Tier | Ceiling |\n|---|---|\n| Breach of the Article 5 prohibitions | EUR 35 million or 7 percent |\n| Most other obligations, including Article 101 fines on general-purpose AI providers | EUR 15 million or 3 percent |\n| Supplying incorrect, incomplete or misleading information to authorities | EUR 7.5 million or 1 percent |\n\nSMEs and start-ups get the lower figure on every tier; small mid-caps, as amended, get it on the two lower tiers only, not for prohibited practices. Authorities weigh the nature and significance of the breach, intent or negligence, history and cooperation; voluntary disclosure can mitigate.\n\nAs at September 2026 no EU AI Act fine had been announced by any official source, and the AI Office describes its first instrument as a technical compliance dialogue. The Commission's enforcement powers, including Article 101 fines of up to EUR 15 million or 3 percent, have applied since 2 August 2026.\n\n### 6.10 The Two Calendars\n\nMost date questions turn on whether the obligation asked about is the enacted one or the amended one.\n\n| Date | As enacted (2024/1689) | As amended (2026/1744) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 12 Jul and 1 Aug 2024 | Published in the Official Journal, then entry into force; no obligations yet | Same |\n| 2 Feb 2025 | Prohibitions and AI literacy apply | Same, literacy duty softened from 27 Jul 2026 |\n| 2 Aug 2025 | GPAI obligations, governance bodies, penalty regime except Article 101, notified bodies | Same |\n| 27 Jul 2026 | No milestone | Digital Omnibus enters into force |\n| 2 Aug 2026 | Annex III high-risk, Article 50 transparency, Commission GPAI enforcement including Article 101 | Article 50 and GPAI enforcement only; high-risk rules and sandboxes moved |\n| 2 Dec 2026 | No milestone | Two new Article 5 prohibitions, and the Article 50(2) marking deadline for legacy generative systems |\n| 2 Aug 2027 | Annex I high-risk, legacy GPAI deadline | Legacy GPAI deadline, national sandboxes operational, Annex I moved to 2028 |\n| 2 Dec 2027 | No milestone | Annex III high-risk rules, being Chapter III Sections 1 to 3, including deployer duties and FRIAs |\n| 2 Aug 2028 | No milestone | Annex I product-embedded high-risk rules |\n| 2 Aug 2030 | Legacy high-risk systems for public authorities | Same |\n\nAnnex III rules due on 2 August 2026 now apply from 2 December 2027, and Annex I rules from 2 August 2028. Three transitional rules govern systems already in use: a high-risk system placed on the market before its category's date is caught only if its design changes significantly afterwards (Article 111(2)), though systems for public authorities must comply by 2 August 2030 regardless; generative systems on the market before 2 August 2026 have until 2 December 2026 for marking; and legacy general-purpose models keep their 2 August 2027 deadline under Article 111(3).\n\nThe Omnibus also softened the Article 4 literacy duty into a duty to take supportive measures from 27 July 2026, added Article 4a widening the legal basis for processing special-category personal data for bias detection, and let a FRIA reuse DPIA content. It did not move Article 50, which still starts on 2 August 2026.\n\n### 6.11 South Korea\n\nKorea's instrument is the Framework Act on the Development of Artificial Intelligence and the Creation of a Foundation for Trust, Act No. 20676, in force 22 January 2026; commentary's \"AI Basic Act\" is not the official title. Its regulator is the Ministry of Science and ICT (MSIT), under an Enforcement Decree. It reaches conduct abroad affecting the Korean market and regulates \"AI business operators\", divided into AI developers and AI-using business operators.\n\nKorea has no four-tier ladder and no prohibited practices; duties attach to overlapping categories, and one system can fall into several at once. **Generative AI** owes users advance notice, output labelling and a clear notice for realistic synthetic sound, images or video. **High-impact AI** needs a listed domain plus significant risk to life, safety or fundamental rights; the domains include energy, drinking water, health care, medical devices, nuclear, biometrics for criminal investigation, hiring and loan screening, transport, public-sector decisions, and student assessment in early and secondary education.\n\nHigh-impact operators owe a risk-management plan, an explanation plan covering final outputs, key criteria and an overview of the training data, user-protection measures, human management and supervision, retention of documents for the period the Act sets, and further measures the presidential committee resolves. The first four must be posted, with trade secrets omitted. A downstream operator making no significant functional change is deemed compliant with the first three, but human oversight and documentation stay with it.\n\nArticle 35's impact assessment is best efforts only: operators \"shall make efforts\", skipping it carries no fine, and the incentive is a procurement preference, not a penalty. The failures carrying administrative fines are narrow: a missing advance notice, a missing domestic representative for a foreign operator without a Korean place of business, and ignoring an MSIT order.\n\nReported figures for the fine cap, the representative thresholds and the training-compute bar behind what commentary calls **\"high-performance\" AI** could not be confirmed from the official English text of the Act or its Enforcement Decree, so they are not stated here. Two framing points are confirmed and tested: \"high-performance\" AI is a commentators' label rather than statutory wording, and MSIT's announced grace period is policy rather than statute, so the obligations applied from entry into force. For scale, Korea has no conformity assessment, no CE marking and no registration database.\n\n### 6.12 The United States\n\nThe United States has no horizontal AI act and no preemption statute. Executive Order 14365, signed 11 December 2025 and published at 90 FR 58499, sets a minimally burdensome national framework policy and a Justice Department task force to litigate against state AI laws; an order cannot itself preempt, so it seeks a congressional preemption statute, and state laws remain enforceable as at September 2026.\n\nThe one federal AI-specific statute is the TAKE IT DOWN Act, Public Law 119-12, enacted 19 May 2025: it criminalises publishing non-consensual intimate visual depictions, expressly including AI digital forgeries, and requires covered platforms to delete the material and duplicates within 48 hours of a valid request, with Federal Trade Commission enforcement from 19 May 2026.\n\nFederal guidance is reversible: Consumer Financial Protection Bureau Circulars 2022-03 and 2023-03 required creditors using complex algorithms to give specific, accurate adverse action reasons and barred a sample form that obscured the real reason, and the Bureau withdrew them in May 2025, leaving the adverse action statute and Regulation B in force.\n\nState and local law falls into three groups. **Decision laws** govern AI that shapes hiring, lending, housing and similar outcomes.\n\n| Law | Regulates | Operative | Notable feature |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Colorado Senate Bill 26-189 | Developer and deployer duties for automated decision-making technology | Signed 14 May 2026, decisions from 1 January 2027 | Repealed and reenacted SB 24-205 before it ever operated |\n| Texas House Bill 149 (TRAIGA) | Conduct: AI built or deployed with intent to discriminate, incite harm or infringe rights | Effective 1 January 2026 | Disparate impact alone is insufficient; framework compliance is a defence |\n| New York City Local Law 144 | Employers and employment agencies using automated employment decision tools | Enforcement from 5 July 2023 | Bias audit within one year, public summary, candidate notice |\n| Illinois House Bill 3773 | Employment AI with a discriminatory effect on a protected class | Operative 1 January 2026 | Bars zip code as a proxy for a protected class |\n\nThe Colorado trap: SB 24-205 never took effect. Senate Bill 25B-004 moved its operative date from 1 February 2026 to 30 June 2026, and Senate Bill 26-189 repealed and reenacted the regime before then, so SB 24-205's reasonable-care and mandatory impact assessment duties never applied. SB 26-189 keeps developer documentation duties and deployer duties of pre-use notice, post-adverse-outcome disclosure, data correction and meaningful human review, attorney-general enforced.\n\nLocal Law 144 is the earliest operational US AI audit requirement: an automated employment decision tool may not be used unless it has had a bias audit within one year, the audit is publicly available, and notices have gone to candidates or employees. It reports selection rates and impact ratios but sets no pass-or-fail threshold, so it mandates disclosure of disparate impact, not its elimination. Illinois covers the other side of the transaction, requiring advance notice, an explanation of the analysis, consent, and limits on distribution and retention under the Artificial Intelligence Video Interview Act.\n\n**Frontier-model safety laws** define their threshold by scale rather than use. California Senate Bill 53, signed 29 September 2025, and the New York RAISE Act require large developers to publish and adhere to a frontier AI framework, report critical safety incidents, and permit compliance through equivalent federal standards; both are attorney-general enforced, with no private right of action, and classify by training compute and developer revenue.\n\n**Transparency and provenance laws** address content. California Assembly Bill 2013 requires generative-AI developers to publish documentation of training data, including sources and whether it is licensed or scraped. California Senate Bill 942, operative 2 August 2026, requires large covered providers to offer a free AI detection tool, place latent provenance disclosures in generated image, video and audio content, and offer an optional visible label, moving provenance from voluntary practice to statutory duty, the US analogue of Article 50 marking on the same date.\n\n### 6.13 Why a US Group Has to Care\n\nThe EU Act reaches beyond the Union, and the test is placement or effect rather than establishment: it covers non-EU entities that place AI systems on the EU market, make them available to users in the Union, deploy them outside the Union where the output is used in the Union, or put general-purpose AI models on the market for EU-facing applications.\n\nA non-EU provider owes the conformity assessment, technical documentation, CE marking, registration and transparency duties exactly as an EU provider does, and must appoint an **EU authorised representative** in the Union under Article 22, with the same duty for a non-EU general-purpose AI provider under Article 54. The representative is the addressee authorities and users reach; appointing one reduces no duty, it adds one.\n\nFor a group under more than one regime, find the highest bar, build to it once, and add the local extras. The EU high-risk chain is that bar.\n\n### Exam Focus\n\nThis chapter maps to BOK v2.1 competency II.C, the main elements of AI-specific laws, which v2.1 widened to include South Korea's framework statute and US federal and state AI laws that apply to private-sector organizations.\n\n- Article 5 lists eight prohibitions as enacted and ten as amended, and emotion recognition is banned only in workplaces and education, carrying a deployer disclosure duty elsewhere.\n- Annex I (product safety components) and Annex III (listed uses) are separate routes to high-risk, and the Article 6(3) exit is never available to a system that profiles natural persons. A provider relying on Article 6(3) must document the assessment before placing the system on the market.\n- Duties attach to roles: provider, deployer, importer, distributor. Article 25(1) makes a party that puts its name or trademark on a high-risk system, substantially modifies one, or changes its intended purpose into a high-risk use the provider, with the full chain attached.\n- Article 14 is the provider's oversight design duty and Article 26(2) is the deployer's oversight staffing duty.\n- The Article 27 FRIA binds exactly two groups: public bodies and private providers of public services deploying any Annex III high-risk system except a point 2 infrastructure system, and any deployer of an Annex III point 5(b) or 5(c) system. It does not bind every high-risk deployer.\n- Integrating a general-purpose AI model makes an organization the provider of its own AI system, never the provider of the model.\n- Open-source general-purpose models are not exempt from the copyright policy or the training summary, because Article 53(2) lifts only points (a) and (b) and lifts nothing for systemic-risk models. 10^23 FLOP indicates a general-purpose model, and 10^25 FLOP triggers the rebuttable systemic-risk presumption.\n- Adversarial testing must be conducted and documented, not published, and external audits, public red-team reports and researcher access are Code of Practice extras that some study material wrongly presents as Article 55 duties.\n- Adherence to an adequate GPAI Code of Practice is evidence of compliance; a presumption of conformity is a different instrument, which Articles 53(4) and 55(2) reserve for harmonised standards.\n- Fines take the higher of two figures, except that SMEs and start-ups get the lower figure on every tier and small mid-caps, as amended, on the EUR 15 million and EUR 7.5 million tiers only.\n- Every date question turns on \"as enacted\" or \"as amended\": general application 2 August 2026, Annex III high-risk from 2 December 2027, Annex I from 2 August 2028, general-purpose AI since 2 August 2025 with legacy models to 2 August 2027, Article 4 literacy since 2 February 2025 with the softened duty from 27 July 2026, and Article 111(2) for legacy systems.\n- The Digital Omnibus is Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, adopted 8 July 2026 and in force 27 July 2026, and it did not move Article 50, which still starts on 2 August 2026.\n- In Korea the impact assessment is best efforts rather than mandatory, \"high-performance\" AI is a commentators' label rather than statutory wording, and MSIT's grace period is policy rather than statute. Reported figures for the fine cap, the representative thresholds and the compute bar are not confirmed from the official English text and should not be relied on as settled.\n- In the United States an executive order cannot preempt state law by itself, Colorado impact assessments and a duty of reasonable care belong to the never-effective SB 24-205 and not to SB 26-189, and the recurring contrasts are intent against effect (Texas TRAIGA against Illinois HB 3773) and disclosure against elimination of disparate impact (New York City Local Law 144).\n- US federal AI governance is largely reversible guidance, illustrated by the withdrawal of the CFPB's algorithmic adverse action circulars in May 2025 while the underlying statute and Regulation B remained in force.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe following terms are used in this chapter and defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): AI Act Milestone: February 2, 2025, AI Act Milestone: August 2, 2026, AI Act Milestone: December 2, 2027, AI Act Milestone: August 2, 2028, AI Distributor, AI Importer, AI Provider, AI Business Operator (Korea), Best-Efforts Impact Assessment (Korea), Bias Audit (NYC LL 144), CE Marking, Conformity Assessment, Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation 2026/1744), Domestic Representative (Korea AI Basic Act), EU AI Act Penalty Tiers, European AI Office, Fundamental Rights Impact Assessment, General-Purpose AI (GPAI), High-Impact AI (Korea), High-Performance AI (Korea), Market Surveillance Powers, National Competent Authorities (EU AI Act), Nudification and CSAM Prohibitions, Post-Market Monitoring, Prohibited AI, Systemic Risk (GPAI), Technical Documentation, Watermarking.\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (EU AI Act)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj/eng): the risk tiers in Articles 5, 6 and the Annexes, the provider and deployer duties, and Article 50\n- [Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng): the deferred high-risk dates and the two new Article 5(1)(ba)-(bb) prohibitions\n- [AI Act Article 5, prohibited practices](https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-5): the closed list of bans and the workplace and education carve-out\n- [AI Act Article 43, conformity assessment](https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-43): internal control against notified-body assessment\n- [AI Act Article 50, transparency obligations](https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-50): the provider and deployer split, and the marking duty\n- [AI Act Article 53, obligations for providers of general-purpose AI models](https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-53): technical documentation, downstream information, copyright policy and training summary\n- [AI Act Article 99, penalties](https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-99): the three-tier ladder and the higher-of-two rule\n- [AI Act implementation timeline (AI Act Service Desk)](https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act): when each obligation starts, as enacted and as amended\n- [European Commission, enforcement of the AI Act](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/enforcement-ai-act): the AI Office, the national authorities and the supervisory architecture\n- [GPAI Code of Practice (European Commission)](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/contents-code-gpai): the voluntary route to demonstrate compliance with Articles 53 and 55\n- [Code of Practice on marking and labelling of AI-generated content](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/commission-publishes-code-practice-marking-and-labelling-ai-generated-content): the voluntary instrument behind Article 50(2) marking\n- [Guidelines on transparency obligations for providers and deployers](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/guidelines-transparency-obligations-providers-and-deployers-ai-systems): the Commission's Article 50 guidance of 20 July 2026\n- [EN 18286, AI quality management system (CEN-CENELEC)](https://www.cencenelec.eu/news-events/news/2026/en-in-the-spotlight/2026-07-30-ai-quality-management/): the first harmonised standard produced for the Act\n- [ISO/IEC 42006:2025](https://www.iso.org/standard/42006): requirements for the bodies that audit and certify AI management systems\n- [South Korea, Framework Act on the Development of Artificial Intelligence and the Creation of a Foundation for Trust (KLRI English text)](https://elaw.klri.re.kr/eng_service/lawView.do?hseq=71019&lang=ENG): the authoritative English translation of Act No. 20676\n- [Executive Order 14365, Ensuring a National Policy Framework for Artificial Intelligence](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/12/16/2025-23092/ensuring-a-national-policy-framework-for-artificial-intelligence): the federal policy and the litigation task force\n- [TAKE IT DOWN Act, Public Law 119-12](https://www.congress.gov/119/plaws/publ12/PLAW-119publ12.pdf): the federal criminal ban on non-consensual intimate deepfakes and the 48-hour platform duty\n- [CFPB withdrawal of interpretive rules and circulars (12 May 2025)](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/05/12/2025-08286/interpretive-rules-policy-statements-and-advisory-opinions-withdrawal): the algorithmic adverse action position withdrawn\n- [Colorado Senate Bill 26-189](https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb26-189): the automated decision-making technology regime that repealed and reenacted SB 24-205\n- [Colorado Senate Bill 25B-004](https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb25b-004): the act that postponed SB 24-205's operative date to 30 June 2026\n- [Colorado Attorney General, AI and ADMT rulemaking](https://coag.gov/ai/): the enforcing authority's page on the new regime\n- [Texas TRAIGA, House Bill 149](https://capitol.texas.gov/tlodocs/89R/billtext/html/HB00149F.htm): intent-based conduct bans and framework compliance as a defence\n- [New York City Local Law 144, DCWP automated employment decision tools](https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/about/automated-employment-decision-tools.page): the bias audit, publication and notice duties\n- [Illinois House Bill 3773](https://www.ilga.gov/ftp/legislation/103/BillStatus/HTML/10300HB3773.html): discriminatory effect in employment decisions, and the zip code prohibition\n- [Illinois Artificial Intelligence Video Interview Act, 820 ILCS 42](https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/ILCS/Articles?ActID=4015&ChapterID=68): advance notice, explanation and consent for AI video analysis\n- [California Senate Bill 53, Transparency in Frontier Artificial Intelligence Act](https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB53): the compute-based frontier threshold and the incident-reporting duty\n- [California Senate Bill 942, California AI Transparency Act](https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240SB942): latent provenance disclosure and the AI detection tool\n- [California Assembly Bill 2013, generative AI training data transparency](https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240AB2013): the developer duty to document training data\n\n## Chapter 7: Standards, Frameworks and Certification\n\nRegulation usually says what must be achieved and rarely says how, and that gap is where standards live. A **standard** is a document, published by a recognised body, that says how a thing is done; a **regulation** is a legal instrument that binds named parties. Standards are voluntary and instruments are binding, and a voluntary standard becomes consequential when it is cited in law, written into a contract, or used to evidence compliance.\n\nThree mechanisms do that work: **incorporation by reference**, a law naming a standard so its content becomes required; **contractual adoption**, parties agreeing to be measured against it; and **evidentiary use**, a regulator, auditor or court treating conformity as proof of due care.\n\nNone is law itself, and one limit runs through the chapter: a certificate attests that an organization runs a management system, not that any particular AI system is safe, fair or lawful.\n\nThis chapter covers the values baseline (7.1), the NIST framework and its implementing layers (7.2 to 7.4), the ISO standards (7.5 to 7.8), the EU harmonised standards and the certifiers (7.9), the four terms most often confused (7.10), and a practical map (7.11).\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| OECD Recommendation on AI | OECD/LEGAL/0449, adopted 22 May 2019, revised 2023 and 2024 | The values baseline, and a definition a binding law tracks |\n| NIST AI RMF and Playbook | NIST AI 100-1, January 2023, plus the AI RMF Playbook | Four functions, GOVERN as cross-cutting, the characteristics |\n| NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile | Published 26 July 2024 | Twelve generative risks, and a profile carrying legal weight |\n| ISO/IEC 22989 | Published 2022 by ISO/IEC JTC 1/SC 42 | The vocabulary layer, and verification against validation |\n| ISO/IEC 42001 | Published December 2023, first edition | The certifiable management system standard |\n| ISO/IEC 42006 | Published July 2025, first edition | Who may audit and certify an AI management system |\n| EN 18286:2026 | Published by CEN-CENELEC, July 2026 | The first harmonised AI Act standard |\n| Texas TRAIGA | House Bill 149, signed 22 June 2025, effective 1 January 2026 | A voluntary framework given legal weight as a defence |\n\n### 7.1 The OECD AI Principles\n\nThe values baseline is the **OECD Recommendation on Artificial Intelligence** (OECD/LEGAL/0449), adopted on 22 May 2019, the first intergovernmental AI standard and the source of the definition later instruments use. It is not binding; its 47 adherents as of September 2026, the EU among them, make a political commitment. G20 leaders welcomed G20 AI Principles drawn from it at the Osaka Summit in June 2019.\n\nFive values-based principles are addressed to AI actors under the headings the 2024 revision gave them, alongside five recommendations to policy makers. The 2024 revision added language on information integrity, use outside the intended purpose, and misuse; there are still five principles.\n\nThe 2023 revision of the AI system definition supplies the boundary test an inventory needs: a machine-based system that infers from its input how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations or decisions. Article 3(1) of the EU AI Act closely tracks the wording, so a voluntary definition marks the edge of a binding law. Its role is policy influence rather than certification, and nothing is certified to it.\n\n### 7.2 The NIST AI Risk Management Framework\n\nThe **NIST AI Risk Management Framework** (AI RMF 1.0, published as NIST AI 100-1 in January 2023) is the US government's voluntary, sector-agnostic framework, built through open consultation. It is a framework rather than a standard, and there is no certification against it, which is the distinction most often tested. The AI RMF Playbook followed in 2023 and the Generative AI Profile in July 2024.\n\nIts normative content sits in four functions, and they are not a sequence.\n\n| Function | Job |\n|---|---|\n| GOVERN | The cross-cutting foundation: policies, roles and responsibilities, oversight, risk tolerance, and a culture of raising concerns |\n| MAP | Understand context: intended purpose and foreseeable misuse, design and data, stakeholders, risks |\n| MEASURE | Assess: metrics, evaluation including testing and red teaming, monitoring, risk documentation |\n| MANAGE | Respond: risk treatment, incident response, integration with enterprise risk, risk through retirement |\n\n**GOVERN runs through the other three rather than starting them.** It is the function most relevant to a governance professional, because culture and appetite are set there. MEASURE works against seven **trustworthiness characteristics**: valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; and fair with harmful bias managed. The AI RMF treats risk as emergent and systemic rather than a fixed catalogue of known threats, unlike the NIST Cybersecurity Framework.\n\n### 7.3 The AI RMF Playbook\n\nThe framework's 72 subcategories carry its normative content. For each, the **AI RMF Playbook** gives **suggested actions**, **transparency and documentation prompts** and **references**. It is the RMF's implementation layer, not a second framework, and because the actions are suggestions, an organization can adopt, adapt or document why it chose otherwise.\n\n### 7.4 NIST AI 600-1, the Generative AI Profile\n\n**NIST AI 600-1**, the Generative AI Profile, implements the AI RMF for a specific setting, technology or sector: cross-sectoral, published on 26 July 2024 pursuant to Executive Order 14110, later revoked but still published. It tailors the RMF without replacing it, and names twelve risks:\n\n1. CBRN information or capabilities.\n2. **Confabulation**: confidently stated false content, or \"hallucination\".\n3. Dangerous, violent or hateful content.\n4. Data privacy: leakage, memorization, inference and de-anonymization.\n5. Environmental impacts.\n6. Harmful bias and homogenization.\n7. Human-AI configuration: anthropomorphization, automation bias, over-reliance, emotional entanglement.\n8. Information integrity: misinformation and disinformation.\n9. Information security: prompt injection, data poisoning, offensive cyber capability.\n10. Intellectual property.\n11. Obscene or abusive content, including non-consensual intimate imagery and child sexual abuse material.\n12. Value chain and component integration.\n\nFour primary considerations serve as a pre-deployment checklist: **governance**, **pre-deployment testing** through TEVV (testing, evaluation, verification and validation), **content provenance**, and **incident disclosure**.\n\nTexas's **TRAIGA** (House Bill 149, signed 22 June 2025, effective 1 January 2026) expressly recognizes substantial compliance with this profile as a defence. It is not a general safe harbour, and its prohibitions turn on intent, but a legislature wrote a voluntary NIST document into a statute.\n\n### 7.5 ISO/IEC 22989, the Vocabulary Layer\n\nThe ISO and IEC standards build on one another; the vocabulary comes first. **ISO/IEC 22989**, Information technology, Artificial intelligence, Artificial intelligence concepts and terminology, was published in 2022 by ISO/IEC JTC 1/SC 42. Its more than 110 defined concepts give everyone the same words without prescribing how to run a program, and the IEC webstore publishes it free of charge.\n\n**Verification** asks whether the system was built right, against its specifications; **validation** asks whether the right system was built, fit for its intended use. **Robustness** is maintaining performance under all circumstances and **resilience** is recovering operational state after an incident. **Reliability** is distinct from accuracy. **Explainability** answers why a particular output came out as it did; **transparency** is information made available about the system.\n\n22989 also supplies roles that do not match the EU AI Act: the **AI provider** makes a system available, the **AI manufacturer** builds it, the **AI customer** acquires it, and the **AI subject** is the person affected. Map them to the Act's provider, deployer, importer and distributor.\n\n### 7.6 ISO/IEC 42001, the Certifiable Management System\n\n**ISO/IEC 42001**, Information technology, Artificial intelligence, Management system, is the first certifiable standard for an **AI management system (AIMS)**. Published in December 2023 as a first edition, it specifies requirements for establishing, implementing and continually improving an AIMS. Certifiability is what sets it apart from 22989 (terminology), 42005 (impact-assessment guidance) and 23894 (risk guidance): an organization can be audited against 42001 and issued a certificate, and cannot for the others.\n\nIt follows the **Annex SL** high-level structure common to ISO management systems on a Plan-Do-Check-Act cycle, and addresses five trustworthiness dimensions, including transparency and explainability and fairness and non-discrimination.\n\nImpact assessment is a requirement of the system, not an add-on: clause 6.1.4 has the organization define an AI system impact assessment process, clause 8.4 has it perform those assessments at planned intervals or on significant change, and Annex A objective A.5 adds controls on the process and its documentation.\n\nCertification is a market expectation, not a legal requirement. It gives no presumption of conformity with the EU AI Act and does not satisfy the Article 17 quality management system duty for high-risk providers; what is certified is the organization's management system, not any particular AI system.\n\n### 7.7 ISO/IEC 42005 and Impact Assessment\n\n**ISO/IEC 42005**, Information technology, Artificial intelligence, AI system impact assessment, was published on 28 May 2025 by ISO/IEC JTC 1/SC 42 as a first edition of 39 pages. It is guidance on how and when to assess an AI system's impacts on people, groups and societies, what to document, and how to integrate with an AIMS. Nothing is certified to it.\n\nReassessment runs on three triggers: a change in intended use, a significant system change in data or model, and a new legal or policy requirement. Because 42005 is not a harmonised standard it gives no presumption of conformity, and a 42005 assessment can inform a GDPR Article 35 or EU AI Act Article 27 assessment without replacing either.\n\n### 7.8 ISO/IEC 23894 and Organizational Risk\n\n**ISO/IEC 23894**, Information technology, Artificial intelligence, Guidance on risk management, was published in 2023. It gives guidance on managing risk related to AI, built on **ISO 31000**, whose definition of risk is \"the effect of uncertainty on objectives\". It is guidance and not certifiable, and its subject is risk management of AI, not using AI to do risk management.\n\n23894 adds the AI-specific risk sources and the mapping onto the AI system life cycle.\n\nThe split is the direction of the question. **42005 asks about impacts on people, and 23894 asks about risk to the organization.** 23894 is nonetheless not one of the three core ISO standards named in BOK v2.1 for Domain II.D.\n\n### 7.9 ISO/IEC 42006 and the EU Harmonised Standards\n\n**ISO/IEC 42006**, Information technology, Artificial intelligence, Requirements for bodies providing audit and certification of artificial intelligence management systems, was published in July 2025 as a first edition. It sets requirements, additional to **ISO/IEC 17021-1**, for the bodies that audit and certify AI management systems against 42001: 42006 regulates the certifiers, not the certified.\n\n**CEN-CENELEC JTC 21** develops harmonised standards in support of the EU AI Act. **EN 18286:2026**, Artificial intelligence, Quality management system for EU AI Act regulatory purposes, was published by CEN and CENELEC in July 2026, the first harmonised standard produced for the Act, serving the Article 17 quality management system.\n\nUnder **Article 40**, a high-risk AI system in conformity with a harmonised standard cited in the Official Journal is presumed to conform with the requirements it covers, and under **Article 53(4)** general-purpose model providers get the same route. That presumption is a legal effect no private standard possesses, and it attaches only once the standard is cited in the Official Journal, separate from publication by CEN.\n\n### 7.10 Certification, Conformity Assessment, Attestation and Audit\n\n**Conformity assessment** is the umbrella: the process of demonstrating that specified requirements are met, by the first party (the organization), second party (a customer) or third party (an independent body). **Audit** is the evidence-gathering activity inside it: a systematic, independent examination against stated criteria, producing findings. An audit is not itself an approval.\n\n**Certification** is a subset: third-party attestation by an accredited body that an organization, its management system, or a product meets a defined standard, producing a certificate with a defined scope and validity period. ISO/IEC 42001 certification is of the organization's management system. Under the EU AI Act, **Article 43** sends most Annex III high-risk systems through internal control without a notified body, and where one is required the outcome is a declaration and CE marking, not a management-system certificate.\n\n**Attestation** is the broadest term: a formal statement by a responsible party that something is true, which does not require an accredited third party.\n\nA certificate is evidence that a management system exists and operates, not proof that a particular system is safe or lawful.\n\n### 7.11 A Practical Map\n\n\n| The question | The instrument | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| What is good practice, and where does an AI system begin? | OECD AI Principles and the 2023 definition | Values and the boundary test |\n| What process must risk management follow? | NIST AI RMF, functions and characteristics | Sector-agnostic, voluntary, no certification |\n| How do we act on each subcategory? | AI RMF Playbook | Suggested actions, prompts and references |\n| What changes for generative systems? | NIST AI 600-1 | Twelve risks and a pre-deployment checklist |\n| What do the words mean, precisely? | ISO/IEC 22989 | The shared vocabulary |\n| How do we make the program auditable and certifiable? | ISO/IEC 42001 | The certifiable management system standard |\n| How do we assess impact on people? | ISO/IEC 42005 | The method 42001 requires and does not provide |\n| How do we fold AI risk into enterprise risk? | ISO/IEC 23894, on ISO 31000 | Risk to the organization, not impact on people |\n| Who may certify us, and how? | ISO/IEC 42006 and ISO/IEC 17021-1 | Rules for the bodies |\n| How does a standard become a legal presumption? | CEN-CENELEC JTC 21 harmonised standards, EN 18286:2026 | Presumption of conformity once cited |\n\n### Exam Focus\n\n- A standard is voluntary and an instrument is binding, but a voluntary standard becomes consequential when it is cited in law, adopted in a contract, or used to evidence compliance. Know all three mechanisms.\n- The OECD Recommendation (22 May 2019, revised 8 November 2023 and 3 May 2024) is non-binding, keeps five principles under 2024 headings, separates principles for AI actors from recommendations to governments, and has a policy-influence role rather than a certification role.\n- The 2023 OECD AI system definition is closely tracked by EU AI Act Article 3(1), so a voluntary definition marks the boundary of a binding law.\n- The NIST AI RMF is a framework, not a standard, with no certification against it. GOVERN is cross-cutting rather than a first phase, and it is the function most relevant to a governance professional.\n- NIST's seven trustworthiness characteristics should be known verbatim: valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; and fair with harmful bias managed.\n- The BOK names the Playbook directly: the risk process is the AI RMF, per-subcategory guidance is the Playbook, and generative AI specifically is NIST AI 600-1.\n- NIST AI 600-1 names twelve generative risks. Confabulation is the formal term for hallucination, and automation bias is a human-AI configuration risk.\n- The AI 600-1 four primary considerations are governance, pre-deployment testing through TEVV, content provenance and incident disclosure.\n- Texas's TRAIGA expressly recognizes substantial compliance with the NIST AI 600-1 profile as a defence. It is not a general safe harbour.\n- The ISO/IEC 22989 trustworthiness cluster is the most tested vocabulary: robustness against resilience, reliability against accuracy, verification against validation, explainability against transparency. 22989 roles are not the EU AI Act's provider, deployer, importer and distributor.\n- 42001 requires the impact assessment (clause 6.1.4 and clause 8.4) and 42005 explains how to run one. 42006 regulates the certifiers rather than the certified organization, and it is a favourite distractor.\n- ISO/IEC 42006 is the 2025 edition, and 42005 is the 2025 edition. Do not rely on memory for the year.\n- 42005 asks about impacts on people and 23894 asks about risk to the organization. 42005 reassessment triggers are intended use, significant system change, and a new legal or policy requirement.\n- BOK v2.1 II.D names a closed set of three core ISO standards (22989, 42001 and 42005), so 23894 may answer an item on AI risk management but never an item on the core three.\n- EN 18286:2026 is the first harmonised AI Act standard, produced by CEN-CENELEC JTC 21, and it targets the Article 17 quality management system. Presumption of conformity under Articles 40 and 53(4) attaches only once a harmonised standard is cited in the Official Journal.\n- Conformity assessment is the umbrella, audit is the evidence-gathering activity, certification is third-party attestation by an accredited body, and attestation is the broadest term. The trap is to treat a certificate as proof that a system is safe or lawful, when it only evidences a management system.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe terms used in this chapter are defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): Attestation, AI Hazard, AI Incident, AI Management System (AIMS), AI RMF Playbook, Annex SL, Certification, Conformity Assessment, Confabulation, Content Provenance, Explainability, Generative AI Profile (NIST AI 600-1), Govern Function, ISO/IEC 22989, ISO/IEC 23894, ISO/IEC 42001, ISO/IEC 42005, ISO/IEC 42006, ISO/IEC 17021-1, Manage Function, Map Function, Measure Function, NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF), OECD AI Principles, OECD AI System Definition (2023 Update), Reliability, Resilience, Robustness, Transparency, Trustworthiness, Validation, Verification.\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [OECD Recommendation on AI (OECD/LEGAL/0449)](https://legalinstruments.oecd.org/en/instruments/OECD-LEGAL-0449): the text and its revisions\n- [OECD.AI: AI principles](https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles): the five principles\n- [OECD.AI: classification framework](https://oecd.ai/en/classification): the five dimensions\n- [OECD.AI: incidents and hazards](https://oecd.ai/en/incidents): the hazard definitions\n- [NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI 100-1)](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf): functions and characteristics\n- [NIST AI RMF landing page](https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework): the official hub\n- [NIST AI RMF Playbook](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/): actions by subcategory\n- [NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf): the twelve generative risks\n- [ISO/IEC 22989 (free from IEC)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839): the free terminology standard\n- [ISO/IEC 22989 (ISO catalogue)](https://www.iso.org/standard/74296.html): concepts and terminology, 2022\n- [ISO/IEC 42001 (ISO catalogue)](https://www.iso.org/standard/42001): the management system standard\n- [ISO/IEC 42001 (IEC webstore)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/90574): with Annex A objective A.5\n- [ISO/IEC 42005 (ISO catalogue)](https://www.iso.org/standard/42005): impact assessment guidance\n- [ISO/IEC 42005 (IEC webstore)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/107659): the assessment method\n- [ISO/IEC 23894 (ISO catalogue)](https://www.iso.org/standard/77304.html): AI risk management\n- [ISO/IEC 42006 (ISO catalogue)](https://www.iso.org/standard/42006): requirements for certifying bodies\n- [ISO/IEC 42006 (IEC webstore)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/108460): the certifiers of AIMS\n- [ISO/IEC 17021-1 (ISO catalogue)](https://www.iso.org/standard/61651.html): general certifying-body requirements\n- [ISO 31000 (ISO catalogue)](https://www.iso.org/standard/65694.html): the general risk standard\n- [CEN-CENELEC: EN 18286 in the spotlight](https://www.cencenelec.eu/news-events/news/2026/en-in-the-spotlight/2026-07-30-ai-quality-management/): the first harmonised standard\n- [CEN-CENELEC JTC 21](https://www.cencenelec.eu/areas-of-work/cen-cenelec-topics/artificial-intelligence/): the harmonised-standards committee\n- [EU AI Act, Article 40 (harmonised standards)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/40/): presumption of conformity\n- [EU AI Act, Article 43 (conformity assessment)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/43/): internal control\n- [EU AI Act, Article 53 (obligations for providers of general-purpose AI models)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/53/): the Article 53(4) route\n- [EU AI Act, Article 17 (quality management system)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/17/): the EN 18286 obligation\n- [EU AI Act, Article 3 (definitions)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/): the AI system definition\n- [EU AI Act, Article 14 (human oversight)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/): the oversight design duty\n- [EU AI Act, Article 27 (fundamental rights impact assessment)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/27/): the deployer assessment\n- [GDPR Article 35 (data protection impact assessment)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-35-gdpr/): the privacy assessment\n- [Texas Responsible Artificial Intelligence Governance Act, House Bill 149](https://capitol.texas.gov/tlodocs/89R/billtext/html/HB00149F.htm): the TRAIGA defence\n\n# Part III: Governing AI Development\n\n*Can we build it right?*\n\nThe third domain follows the build. It is organized as a chain of documented decisions that ends in release, and almost every exam item tests process discipline: which link of the chain is in play, and what record it should leave. The domain is also where the BOK's documentation requirements make the record itself examinable, so the question is often not what should have been decided but what should be written down and by whom.\n\n### Exam at a Glance\n\nDomain III covers the build and carries 21 to 25 of the 100 scored items, tied with Domain IV for the largest share.\n\n| Competency | What it covers | Questions | Chapter |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| III.A Govern designing and building the AI system | Use case, impact assessment, design risk, six named techniques | 6 to 8 | 8 |\n| III.B Govern data collection and use in training and testing | Right to use data, quality, provenance and lineage, testing | 6 to 8 | 9 |\n| III.C Govern release, monitoring and maintenance | Readiness, deployment, monitoring, retraining | 8 to 10 | 10 |\n\nThe three competencies are close in size, but III.C is the largest. The III.A technique names are the BOK's own, and the two documentation indicators make the record itself examinable. BOK v2.1 made two changes that reach Domain III.\n\n- III.A lost its \"identify applicable laws\" indicator, which v2.1 also removed from IV.B.\n- \"Model\" became \"system\" throughout, matching the BOK's shift from governing a model to governing a system in its deployment context.\n\n**Chapters.** Chapter 8 covers design and impact assessment: framing the use case, the intake gate, design risk, the impact assessment itself, requirements and the design baseline, and the documentation that has to survive contact with delivery pressure. Chapter 9 covers training and testing data: the authority to use data, quality, provenance and lineage, labelling and training methods, held-out data, and testing that goes beyond accuracy to bias and fairness. Chapter 10 covers release and monitoring: readiness before release, deployment, and the monitoring that continues after it.\n\n## Chapter 8: Design and Impact Assessment\n\nBy the time a model exists, most of its governance is already fixed. The design stage settles the purpose, the boundary, the autonomy level, the data minimisation rule and how a person can contest an outcome. A requirement met in design is a parameter; the same requirement met in production is a rebuild. This chapter covers BOK competency III.A.\n\nDesign governance does two jobs. One looks inward, at what could go wrong for the organization; the other outward, at what could go wrong for the people the system touches. The first is a risk assessment, the second an impact assessment, and the distinction is examined. A system can clear its risk register and still be struck down.\n\nHere is what this chapter settles, in the order it is tested:\n\n- 8.1 Framing the Use Case and the Boundary\n- 8.2 The Intake Gate and Design Risk\n- 8.3 The Design-Stage Impact Assessment\n- 8.4 Legal Drivers: the DPIA and the FRIA\n- 8.5 Autonomy and Requirements by Design\n- 8.6 Documentation, the Decision Record and the Design Review\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| SyRI welfare fraud risk scoring | Rechtbank Den Haag, 5 February 2020 | Group-level harm and insufficient transparency, struck down at the design stage |\n| Rotterdam welfare fraud algorithm | Rekenkamer Rotterdam, Gekleurde technologie, April 2021 | An oversight failure caught by an auditor rather than by monitoring |\n| Dutch childcare benefits scandal | Dutch government resignation, 15 January 2021; Amnesty International, October 2021 | Discrimination encoded in design, with harm no individual complaint surfaced |\n| Bauserman v. Michigan UIA | Class settlement approved 29 January 2024 | An automated finding treated as final, a design choice about autonomy |\n| Drivers v. Uber and Ola | Amsterdam Court of Appeal, 4 April 2023 | A human in the loop placed on paper but not by design |\n\n### 8.1 Framing the Use Case and the Boundary\n\nA use case starts with the **business context**: the organizational, strategic and operational environment the system will live in: the objective it supports, the expected benefit and readiness. Readiness covers leadership commitment, data governance, security and privacy maturity, risk culture and cross-functional expertise.\n\nThe **use case** specification describes the system: its intended function (classification, prediction, assistance or recommendation), intended users and environments, assumptions and constraints, **decision authority** (autonomous or human in the loop), and expected lifetime and workload. Decision authority is answered by risk appetite, not model capability: a system that auto-approves only the lowest-risk band and routes the rest to a person has chosen its autonomy before build begins.\n\nThe boundary is the part most often left implicit, and implicit boundaries are what scope creep exploits. An explicit boundary states what the system will not do and where its inputs stop: one product, one jurisdiction, one decision type. A later request to reuse the score returns through the gate as a new use case, not a configuration change (8.2).\n\nSuccess metrics are named up front, aligned to the business goal, and they conflict. Optimizing precision raises false negatives; optimizing efficiency can cost transparency. Choosing the metric is choosing which harm you accept and who bears it, so the choice belongs in the design file with a named owner.\n\nData suitability is tested separately from data availability: data can be technically perfect and still off limits, collected for another purpose (GDPR Article 5(1)(b), purpose limitation), carrying sensitive attributes, or supporting inferences the subject never expected. Decision rights are also specified, not assumed: what the system may decide alone, when a human must be involved, and how overrides and appeals work.\n\n### 8.2 The Intake Gate and Design Risk\n\nA charter describes one proposal. The **use case assessment** is the pre-development gate that decides how much scrutiny each proposal gets, asking whether it fits organizational policy, risk appetite and ethical standards. Rigour scales with six factors: decision impact, user exposure, affected rights or opportunities, automation level, applicable regulation, and context such as cultural norms or prior incidents.\n\nPast the gate, the question is what could break the design itself. **Internal design risk** arises inside the project: faulty assumptions, inadequate data, incomplete testing, poor integration and no explainability. **External design risk** arises outside it: adversarial use, regulatory change, the supply chain and public backlash. The deepest internal assumption is that the past represents the future, as when historic approvals are treated as creditworthiness; the sharpest external risks are that users will game stated inputs and that suppliers form a chain the organization does not control.\n\nThe BOK names six techniques. A **probability and severity matrix** plots likelihood against impact to prioritise treatment; it ranks risk rather than measuring it, and its NIST AI RMF equivalent is MANAGE 1.2. The **risk mitigation hierarchy** prefers avoiding a risk, then reducing, transferring, and finally accepting the residual deliberately. **Stakeholder mapping** identifies whose interests the design affects. **Use case evaluation** is the gate above. **Benchmarking** compares a system against established references. A **pre-deployment pilot** runs the system in real conditions without consequence.\n\n### 8.3 The Design-Stage Impact Assessment\n\nDesign risk asks what could go wrong with the system; the impact assessment asks what happens to the people it touches. An **AI impact assessment** looks outward at the people a system can affect, while ISO/IEC 23894 manages risk to the organization's own objectives. ISO/IEC 42001 is the certifiable management system and requires the assessment (clauses 6.1.4 and 8.4, Annex A objective A.5). ISO/IEC 42005 is uncertifiable guidance; its Annex B explains how impact findings and the 23894 risk process feed each other.\n\nClause 5 of 42005 describes a documented process set up before any single system is assessed: procedures and templates, integration with enterprise risk, compliance and privacy, timing, scope, responsibilities, thresholds that decide depth and escalation, formal approval, and monitoring. Thresholds route sensitive and restricted uses to senior approval.\n\nA complete record, as clause 6 defines it, sets the scope (system, version, uses) and describes the system. It covers data sources, characteristics and quality; the algorithms and model development; the deployment environment (geography, languages, constraints); and the interested parties, directly affected and others. It then sets out the **actual and reasonably foreseeable impacts**, benefits as well as harms, failure modes, reasonably foreseeable misuse, and the measures addressing them. A record with no benefits section, no unintended uses and no misuse analysis is incomplete.\n\nAn impact assessment is not a risk assessment: the risk assessment asks what the system does to the organization, the impact assessment what it does to people, which is why a system can pass one and fail the other. Nor is it a conformity assessment, which tests a product against a specified standard to declare compliance, not consequences for a population.\n\nSome harms are individual and surface through a complaint or an appeal; others are group-level. A scoring rule that treats a neighbourhood, a nationality or a benefit category as a signal harms a class of people, and no single member may ever see a reason to complain. **SyRI**, the Dutch welfare fraud risk scoring program, is the clearest illustration. On 5 February 2020 the District Court of The Hague held that the SyRI legislation breached the fair balance required by Article 8 of the European Convention on Human Rights, its use being insufficiently transparent and verifiable. The harm, scored through data encoding socioeconomic and migration patterns, was surfaced by civil society groups and two citizens rather than an individual grievance. The question is who is scored, and on what basis.\n\n### 8.4 Legal Drivers: the DPIA and the FRIA\n\nTwo legal instruments attach here, binding different parties. The first is the **data protection impact assessment** (DPIA). GDPR **Article 35** requires one where processing is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of people. Its triggers are systematic and extensive automated evaluation of personal aspects, including profiling, on which decisions with legal or similarly significant effects are based; large-scale processing of special category or criminal-offence data; and large-scale systematic monitoring of a publicly accessible area. It is the controller's duty, covering personal data and the rights built around it.\n\nThe second is the AI Act **fundamental rights impact assessment** (FRIA). EU AI Act **Article 27** places it on certain **deployers** of high-risk systems, notably deployers of the Annex III point 5(b) creditworthiness and credit-scoring systems. It sits on top of the provider's **risk management system** under **Article 9**: the provider runs that system, the deployer the FRIA, and neither discharges the other. The high-risk obligations apply from 2 December 2027 as amended by the Digital Omnibus on AI, and from 2 August 2026 as enacted (Chapter 6).\n\nA FRIA may build on a DPIA, but it does not replace one. **Article 27(4)** allows a FRIA already carried out under Article 35 to satisfy the Article 27 duty where it covers the required elements: a DPIA covers data protection risk, the FRIA the wider impact picture, including fundamental rights effects that are not about data at all. A limited-risk system owes no FRIA, and the deployer carve-outs mean not every high-risk deployment owes one either.\n\nOther regimes are narrower than they first appear. Canada's Directive on Automated Decision-Making requires algorithmic impact assessments, but only for federal institutions, and New York City's Local Law 144 requires a bias audit of automated employment decision tools, not an impact assessment.\n\n### 8.5 Autonomy and Requirements by Design\n\nAutonomy is a design decision, not a setting. The EU High-Level Expert Group on AI set out three levels that remain the working vocabulary. Under **human-in-the-loop** oversight a person reviews and approves each consequential decision before it takes effect. Under **human-on-the-loop** oversight a person supervises operation, monitors outputs and can intervene or halt. Under **human-in-command** oversight people set the objectives, bounds and escalation rules and keep the ability to override or deactivate, and it applies to everything. Stakes and volume choose between the first two, per use case.\n\nThe choice is legally load-bearing. GDPR **Article 22** restricts decisions based solely on automated processing with legal or similarly significant effects, and \"solely automated\" is the hinge: meaningful human review before the decision takes effect moves a process outside the article, cosmetic review does not. The AI Act **Article 14** requires high-risk systems designed so competent people can understand the outputs, override them and interrupt the system, a provider design duty. A human in the loop who rubber-stamps every recommendation satisfies no obligation and is worse than none, because the review supplies cover for a decision the machine already made. **Drivers v. Uber and Ola** is the authority: the Amsterdam Court of Appeal found automated deactivation of drivers was automated decision-making within Article 22, and rejected reliance on a human in the loop because the human role was not meaningful. **Bauserman v. Michigan Unemployment Insurance Agency** is the same failure in the public sector: an automated system raised fraud findings and triggered enforcement with no meaningful oversight, wrongly accusing more than three thousand people.\n\nWith the autonomy level fixed, requirements turn the impact findings into specifications. They are captured before design, each with its source, owner, priority and rationale, and each must be verifiable, traceable, unambiguous and measurable. They fall into four categories: functional, technical or non-functional (performance, scalability, security, privacy, usability, resilience), regulatory, and security. The register records who supplied each requirement and deliberately includes affected groups' views, heard through complaints data or a panel.\n\nThree governance requirements belong in every register, and each is cheapest at design time. **Data minimisation by design** reaches features and training sets, not only storage: if fewer features perform equivalently, use fewer, because every retained feature is a proxy risk and a liability. **Contestability** means a route by which an affected person can challenge an outcome, receive a reason and obtain review, which is why reason codes are a requirement and not a feature. **Logging** records what the system decided, on what inputs and under which version, at a granularity that can reconstruct the decision. Rotterdam's welfare fraud algorithm shows what happens when they are absent: in its **Gekleurde technologie** report of April 2021, the city's own Court of Audit found insufficient oversight of algorithm use, caseworkers unaware how their work fed the algorithm, and data reliability never established. The city paused the algorithm that year; the failure was caught by an auditor, not by monitoring, the argument for building oversight into the design.\n\n### 8.6 Documentation, the Decision Record and the Design Review\n\nDocumentation is a design artifact, not a report written after the fact. It has two dimensions: the design and build process is documented once, revised periodically, as a development standard; each system's design is documented per system, as a **design baseline**. The baseline captures system purpose and scope, stakeholders and roles, ethical and regulatory considerations, boundaries, data and model design, and risk and lifecycle management, including fallback, monitoring, retraining and retirement plans.\n\nThe part most often missing is the **record of decisions**. A design file that lists what was chosen and not why is a description, not evidence. Each entry records the decision, the rationale, the alternatives considered and why they were rejected, and the owner who decided. When a proxy feature is dropped or a threshold set where it sits, the rejected options and reasoning are what a validator, a regulator or a court will ask years later. The EU AI Act's technical documentation duty (Article 11 and Annex IV) expects enough to reproduce the design effort and explain the design rationale, a requirement to record reasoning and not only results.\n\n**Traceability** ties the file together. Version control links code, data and documentation; requirements link to design elements and test results. Data integrity is the precondition: if records or artifacts can be altered undetected, the documentation proves nothing, so training data is archived immutably, changes logged, and artifact repositories access-controlled and audited. Three documents then describe the work to others: a **datasheet** covers a dataset, a **model card** a trained model, and a **system card** the whole deployed system, supporting the transparency and instructions-for-use obligations the AI Act requires. A **quality management system** makes the documentation repeatable, and the AI Act requires one from providers of high-risk systems (Article 17), though citing a framework does not by itself satisfy that article.\n\nThe design review is the gate that closes the stage, and its power is the power to refuse. An assessment is worth only the change it causes, so its findings must feed features, training data, interfaces and monitoring before build proceeds. Review sits with governance authority: legal, risk and ethics, with executive or board endorsement for a high-risk system. Risk acceptance records who accepted the residual risk, the residual itself and the tradeoffs accepted, so acceptance is an act by a person rather than a default. The design stage is the last point at which any of this is cheap, the argument for a review that can stop a project, not one that records that a project started.\n\n### Exam Focus\n\nCompetency III.A covers the design and build of an AI system, and these points recur.\n\n- Business context (objective, expected benefit, readiness) is distinct from the use case specification (function, users, assumptions, decision authority, lifetime), and an explicit boundary is what prevents scope creep.\n- A use case assessment scales rigour by decision impact, user exposure, affected rights or opportunities, automation level, applicable regulation and context, so a recommendation engine and an eligibility model are different governance problems.\n- Internal design risk (faulty assumptions, inadequate data, incomplete testing, poor integration, no explainability) differs from external design risk (adversarial use, regulatory change, supply chain, backlash).\n- The six named techniques are the probability and severity matrix, the risk mitigation hierarchy, stakeholder mapping, use case evaluation, benchmarking and pre-deployment pilots.\n- An impact assessment looks at harm to people while a risk assessment looks at harm to the organization's objectives, and a conformity assessment does neither: it tests against a standard to declare compliance.\n- A 42005 record with no benefits, no unintended uses or no misuse analysis is incomplete, a common trap.\n- ISO/IEC 42005 is uncertifiable guidance while ISO/IEC 42001 is certifiable and requires the assessment; 42005 clause 5 sets up the process and clause 6 defines one system's record.\n- Group-level harm can strike a system down when no individual complaint would surface it, which is the SyRI holding on Article 8 ECHR and the pattern in the Dutch childcare benefits scandal.\n- GDPR Article 35 DPIA triggers are automated evaluation with significant effects, large-scale special category or criminal data, and large-scale systematic monitoring; the DPIA is the controller's duty.\n- The AI Act Article 27 FRIA is a deployer duty for certain high-risk systems, Article 9's risk management system is the provider's duty, and a DPIA does not replace a FRIA.\n- Autonomy (human-in-the-loop, human-on-the-loop, human-in-command) is chosen at design time; stakes and volume choose between the first two, and in-command applies to everything.\n- \"Solely automated\" under GDPR Article 22 is the hinge: meaningful review before the decision takes effect moves a process outside the article, and cosmetic review does not.\n- Requirements must be verifiable, traceable, unambiguous and measurable, across functional, non-functional, regulatory and security categories.\n- The record of decisions, including rejected options and their rationale, is what makes a design file evidence rather than description.\n- Residual risk is accepted by a named person, and a 42001 certificate does not by itself meet the Article 17 QMS duty.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe following terms are used in this chapter and defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): Business Context, Use Case, Use Case Assessment, Impact Assessment, Risk Assessment, Conformity Assessment, Data Protection Impact Assessment, Fundamental Rights Impact Assessment, Internal Design Risk, External Design Risk, Human in the Loop, Data Minimisation, Contestability, Design Baseline, System Card, Data Integrity, Life Cycle, Quality Management System (QMS).\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [GDPR, Article 35 (data protection impact assessment)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-35-gdpr/): when a DPIA is required and what it must contain\n- [GDPR, Article 22 (automated individual decision-making)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-22-gdpr/): the right and the \"solely automated\" hinge for autonomy design\n- [GDPR, Article 5(1)(b) (purpose limitation)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-5-gdpr/): the rule that stops historic data being repurposed without a fresh basis\n- [EU AI Act, Article 27 (fundamental rights impact assessment)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/27/): the deployer-side assessment duty for certain high-risk systems\n- [EU AI Act, Article 9 (risk management system)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/9/): the provider-side risk process that runs alongside the FRIA\n- [EU AI Act, Article 14 (human oversight)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/): the provider's duty to design systems for effective oversight\n- [EU AI Act, Article 11 (technical documentation)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/11/): the documentation duty, detailed in Annex IV\n- [EU AI Act, Article 13 (transparency and instructions for use)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/13/): what the system card and instructions for use must tell deployers\n- [EU AI Act, Article 17 (quality management system)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/17/): the QMS obligation on providers of high-risk systems\n- [ISO/IEC 42005 (IEC webstore)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/107659): the uncertifiable guidance whose clause 5 sets the process and clause 6 the record\n- [ISO/IEC 42001 (IEC webstore)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/90574): the certifiable AI management system that requires the impact assessment\n- [ISO/IEC 23894 (IEC webstore)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/82914): the organizational-risk lens that impact findings feed into\n- [NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI 100-1)](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf): the MAP function on context, purpose and intended use\n- [NIST AI RMF Playbook](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/): per-subcategory suggested actions, including MANAGE 1.2 on prioritising risk\n- [Model Cards for Model Reporting](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993): the model-card format the system card extends\n- [OECD Recommendation on AI (OECD/LEGAL/0449)](https://legalinstruments.oecd.org/en/instruments/OECD-LEGAL-0449): the values baseline that use case assessment is usually tested against\n- [EU High-Level Expert Group on AI, Ethics Guidelines for Trustworthy AI (2019)](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/ethics-guidelines-trustworthy-ai): the source of the in-the-loop, on-the-loop and in-command levels\n- [SyRI ruling (Rechtbank Den Haag, 5 February 2020)](https://www.rechtspraak.nl/organisatie-en-contact/organisatie/rechtbanken/rechtbank-den-haag/nieuws/syri-wetgeving-in-strijd-met-het-europees-verdrag-voor-de-rechten-voor-de-mens): group-level harm and insufficient transparency under Article 8 ECHR\n- [Rekenkamer Rotterdam, Gekleurde technologie (April 2021)](https://rekenkamer.rotterdam.nl/docs/onderzoeken/R.P.20.06-gekleurde-technologie.pdf): an oversight and data-reliability failure caught by an auditor\n- [Amnesty International, Xenophobic Machines (October 2021)](https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2021/10/xenophobic-machines-dutch-child-benefit-scandal/): discrimination built into the design of the Dutch childcare benefits system\n- [Bauserman v. Michigan Unemployment Insurance Agency](https://www.michigan.gov/ag/news/press-releases/2024/01/30/class-action-settlement-approved-by-court-of-claims): an automated determination treated as final, with mass remediation\n- [Drivers v. Uber and Ola (Amsterdam Court of Appeal, 4 April 2023)](https://apps.eurofound.europa.eu/platformeconomydb/amsterdam-court-of-appeal-rules-uber-violated-data-protection-rights-110150): a human-in-the-loop defence rejected as not meaningful\n\n## Chapter 9: Training and Testing Data\n\nCompetency III.B asks five things of training data: a defendable legal right to collect and use it, quality judged against defined dimensions, fitness for the intended purpose, lineage and provenance records, and a testing regime that reaches past accuracy. A trained model is a claim about data, and every claim in this chapter is evidenced by a document with a name on it.\n\nThe distinction the chapter turns on is that discrimination usually enters through the label or the proxy rather than through a protected attribute. The canonical proof is the commercial health risk algorithm studied by Obermeyer and colleagues, which used health costs as a proxy for health need. Cost tracks access, insurance and wealth as well as illness, so Black patients at the same risk score were sicker than White patients, and re-targeting the label on active chronic conditions was estimated to raise the share of Black patients identified for extra care from 17.7 percent to 46.5 percent. The model never saw race. The label carried the harm.\n\n- 9.1 Lawful basis, consent and secondary use\n- 9.2 Rights in scraped, licensed and user-supplied data\n- 9.3 Provenance and lineage\n- 9.4 Representativeness and coverage\n- 9.5 Labels, annotators and proxies: where discrimination enters\n- 9.6 Data quality, completeness and accuracy\n- 9.7 Train, validation and test splits, and leakage\n- 9.8 Bias testing and disaggregated evaluation: what the aggregate hides\n- 9.9 Fairness metrics and the impossibility result\n- 9.10 Synthetic data: uses and risks\n- 9.11 Documentation and versioning: datasheets and data cards\n- 9.12 Retention and deletion of training data\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Amazon recruiting tool | Reuters, 10 October 2018, by Jeffrey Dastin | Historical bias learned faithfully from past hiring data |\n| Optum health risk algorithm | Obermeyer et al., Science, 25 October 2019, doi 10.1126/science.aax2342 | A proxy label carrying the discrimination |\n| Clearview AI facial image database | Dutch DPA decision, 3 September 2024, EUR 30.5 million | Provenance, and collection without a lawful basis |\n| Dutch childcare benefits scandal | Amnesty International, \"Xenophobic Machines\", October 2021; government resignation 15 January 2021 | Protected attributes used directly as fraud signals |\n| Apple Card underwriting review | New York State Department of Financial Services, 23 March 2021 | A subgroup review of a third-party underwriting model |\n| NYC Local Law 144 enforcement audit | New York State Comptroller, audit 2024-N-6, 2 December 2025 | A bias audit duty, and whether it changed behaviour |\n\n### 9.1 Lawful Basis, Consent and Secondary Use\n\nThe organization confirms a **defendable legal right** to collect, process, store and analyse data before anyone judges whether it is good. Implicit consent buried in terms of use is not enough in the EU, and a privacy notice is a disclosure rather than a basis; the working standard for training data is counsel's written assertion backed by a source register.\n\n**Secondary use** is the recurring failure: repurposing data beyond its collection purpose is function creep, needing a compatibility assessment or a fresh basis, with GDPR Article 5(1)(b) purpose limitation as the usual blocker. The EDPB's Opinion 28/2024 sets a three-step legitimate interest test, and its draft web scraping guidelines reason that consent is rarely workable at scale. Garante v. OpenAI closed an investigation on 20 December 2024 with a EUR 15 million fine.\n\n### 9.2 Rights in Scraped, Licensed and User-Supplied Data\n\n**Scraped data.** Public availability is neither a licence nor a lawful basis, and personal data collected from the web remains personal data. Copyright is separate: EU law permits text and data mining under Directive (EU) 2019/790 subject to a machine-readable rights reservation, and Getty Images v Stability AI (UK judgment, 4 November 2025) tests that boundary. The Dutch data protection authority fined Clearview AI EUR 30.5 million on 3 September 2024 over the unlawful collection of facial images: the failure was in how the corpus was built, not in how a model performed.\n\n**Licensed data.** The terms that matter are volume, permitted purposes, onward sharing, derivative works, expiry and audit rights.\n\n**User-supplied data.** Prompts, uploads, feedback and employee files are user-supplied data, and the contract and notice govern them; whether the terms permit training on customer inputs and outputs is a disclosure duty and a negotiation point. EU AI Act Article 53 requires general-purpose model providers to publish a summary of the content used for training.\n\n### 9.3 Provenance and Lineage\n\n**Provenance** records where data came from and under what conditions: who collected it, when, by what method, under what licence and access rights, for what purpose, and with what limitations. **Lineage** records how and where it moved: ingestion, storage, transformation, consumption, maintenance and disposal, the systems that consumed it, and the retention and deletion operations applied.\n\nDirection matters. **Backward lineage** traces a record to its origin, which is what a contested decision needs: which records of this person entered training. **Forward lineage** traces how an element flowed downstream, which is what an incident investigation needs. Article 10 requires documented lineage and version control for high-risk datasets, and Article 15(5) expects poisoning to be addressed where appropriate.\n\nAccess logs, data minimization and retention say who touched the data, how much was collected and how long it is kept: none is provenance or lineage.\n\n### 9.4 Representativeness and Coverage\n\n**Sufficiency** is enough volume to train effectively. **Representativeness** is whether the data covers the diversity of the environment the system will be deployed in, measured against the population it will serve rather than the population that happens to be in the data.\n\nOutcomes are observed only where a prior decision was taken, so a model trained on past decisions sees a result only for the people a human or an earlier model approved. This is the selective labels problem: the model learns the world as filtered by past policy and is asked to score the population that policy excluded. Amazon's recruiting tool is the standard illustration: Reuters reported in October 2018 that a system trained on a decade of resumes from a male-dominated field penalised resumes containing the word \"women's\" and downgraded graduates of two women's colleges.\n\nEU AI Act Article 10 makes representativeness a legal standard for high-risk systems: datasets must be relevant, sufficiently representative and as free from errors and bias as possible for the groups most likely to be affected.\n\n### 9.5 Labels, Annotators and Proxies\n\n**Data annotation** attaches descriptive information to data without altering it, and in supervised learning the label is the value the model is trained to predict. **Ground truth** is the target value for a single labeled element, and it is only as true as the process behind it. A label is a decision about what to measure and over what period, and it belongs in writing.\n\nThe label is frequently not the thing the organization cares about. It is a proxy, and the gap between proxy and construct is where **measurement bias** lives. Obermeyer and colleagues studied a widely used commercial algorithm that predicted health costs and was read as a prediction of health need. Among patients with the same risk score, Black patients were measurably sicker, and re-targeting the algorithm on active chronic conditions rather than cost was estimated to raise the share identified for additional care from 17.7 percent to 46.5 percent. The model had no race variable and no discriminatory intent; it predicted the wrong quantity, and the institution had chosen that quantity.\n\nThe opposite error is the explicit signal: in the Dutch childcare benefits scandal, a risk classification system used by the tax authority from 2013 treated nationality and dual citizenship as fraud indicators, thousands of families were wrongly accused, and the government resigned in January 2021.\n\nWhere people label by hand, the controls are annotation guidelines, labeler training, a documented disagreement rule, and **inter-rater reliability**, a measure of agreement among labelers. Who labels shapes what the labels say: hate speech datasets over-represent African American English in their abusive classes, so a classifier trained on them reads dialect as offence (Davidson et al., 2019). **Reinforcement learning with human feedback** (RLHF) turns an annotator pool's rankings into a reward model and then into the model's disposition (Santurkar et al., 2023).\n\n### 9.6 Data Quality, Completeness and Accuracy\n\nQuality is judged against dimensions rather than impressions: **accuracy** (values are correct), **completeness** (fields are populated), **integrity** (relationships across datasets hold), **timeliness** (the data is current) and **consistency** (formats and rules are followed).\n\nA dataset is fit or unfit for a stated purpose, and **fitness for purpose** joins quality to use. The recurring defects are contradictions between sources, missing values, broken joins, duplicates, and schema drift after a migration.\n\nMissingness is itself a signal, because a field populated only for the customers who completed a longer process teaches the model the process as well as the population. **Data quality review** is the formal check for completeness gaps and systematic deviations. The evidence is a profile with a metric per dimension, a threshold and an owner; under Chapter 2's RACI, **data owners** approve use cases and **data stewards** keep quality and documentation.\n\n### 9.7 Train, Validation and Test Splits, and Leakage\n\nThree datasets do three jobs. **Training data** is what the model learns from. **Validation data** tunes it and chooses between candidates. **Test data** (evaluation data) is held back from both and used once, as the estimate of final performance.\n\n**Test set leakage** is overlap between training and test data, and it inflates performance. Its common forms: the same person or document appearing on both sides of a random split; near-duplicate records; a feature derived from the label; and preprocessing fitted before the split, where statistics computed over the whole dataset carry test information back into training.\n\nThe discipline that prevents it runs in one order: split before any transformation, and fit every preprocessing step on the training portion only; split by entity rather than by row; use out-of-time splits where the process is time-dependent; and version the split and hold it fixed.\n\n### 9.8 Bias Testing and Disaggregated Evaluation\n\n**Bias testing** evaluates outputs for unfair differences across demographic groups, sensitive attributes and protected characteristics. **Performance parity** measures whether metrics such as accuracy and error rates vary across groups. **Counterfactual fairness** asks whether the decision would change if a sensitive attribute were altered with everything else held constant, usually run as a flip test, and it is the closest of the two to the question a regulator or a complainant asks.\n\nAn aggregate accuracy figure is a weighted average, so a system at 97 percent overall can sit at 70 percent for a small group without the aggregate moving enough to notice.\n\nSo the reporting standard is per subgroup: accuracy alongside the false positive and false negative rates, with the subgroup sample size and an uncertainty range, against a threshold set before the test for when a gap is a finding rather than noise. Slice by more than one attribute at a time, and test the proxies rather than only the attributes. The rule is to assess **outcomes**, not features.\n\nThe regulatory layer is uneven. The New York State Department of Financial Services reviewed underwriting data for roughly 400,000 applicants on the Apple Card in 2021 and found no fair lending violation, while concluding that the laws governing credit access need modernising. The New York State Comptroller's audit of New York City Local Law 144, of 2 December 2025, found the bias audit duty weakly enforced: two complaints in two years, and one non-compliance issue across 32 companies' audits.\n\n### 9.9 Fairness Metrics and the Impossibility Result\n\n- **Demographic parity**: equal positive (approval) rates across groups.\n- **Equalized odds**: equal error rates, so true positive and false positive rates match across groups (Hardt et al., 2016).\n- **Calibration within groups**: a score means the same thing in every group.\n- The **disparate impact ratio**: one group's positive rate divided by the most favoured group's, which US enforcement guidance treats as suggesting adverse impact below roughly 80 percent.\n\nThese metrics conflict, and the conflict is a theorem rather than an implementation defect. Where base rates differ between groups, a score cannot simultaneously be calibrated within groups and satisfy equal error rates (Kleinberg et al., 2016; Chouldechova, 2016).\n\n| Measure | Option 1: equal error rates | Option 2: equal approval rates |\n|---|---|---|\n| Repayers approved (A / B) | 90% / 90% | 90% / 100% |\n| Defaulters approved (A / B) | 20% / 20% | 20% / 32.5% |\n| Overall approval rate (A / B) | 86.5% / 76.0% | 86.5% / 86.5% |\n| Disparate impact ratio (B / A) | 0.88 | 1.00 |\n| Equalized odds | Met | Not met |\n| Demographic parity | Not met | Met |\n\nOption 1 holds repayers in both groups to the same standard and leaves Group B with a lower approval rate. Neither option is neutral; each chooses which error to distribute, and to whom. Fairlearn, AIF360 and the What-If Tool will all compute these metrics, and none can say which promise the organization owes the people it scores.\n\n### 9.10 Synthetic Data\n\n**Synthetic data** is artificially generated records that imitate the structure and statistical patterns of a real dataset without copying individual records.\n\nPrivacy: a generator trained on personal data can reproduce details of real people, so synthetic records are not automatically anonymous and an anonymity claim needs a test rather than an assertion. Synthetic datasets can re-leak information about their sources (Stadler et al., 2022), and models can emit memorized training text on request (Carlini et al., 2021). Utility: synthetic data preserves the gaps and biases of its source. Provenance: a synthetic dataset has a lineage of its own, and if the real data behind the generator was collected without a basis the synthetic output inherits the problem.\n\nThe controls are ordinary ones applied honestly: a re-identification test before synthetic records enter training; real data held out to check the model performs on reality; and the same versioning as any other dataset. Synthetic data cannot fix a label problem, so a subgroup gap it appears to fill should be tested for whether it is a gap of volume or of construct.\n\n### 9.11 Documentation and Versioning\n\nThe **datasheet** is the standard description of a dataset, and its sections are the questions worth asking: motivation, composition (what it contains, what is excluded), collection process, preprocessing, uses (recommended and discouraged), distribution and maintenance. A **data card** is the shorter, decision-oriented form used at scale inside an organization.\n\nDocumentation does two jobs. It forces the questions that are easy to skip under delivery pressure: which subgroup is missing, what this label means, what was excluded and why. And it is the evidence base a regulator reads, which is why Article 10 requires documented data governance including lineage and version control. Versioning makes that verifiable, because every dataset, split and pipeline carries an identifier and a model's registry entry points to the exact versions it consumed.\n\n### 9.12 Retention and Deletion of Training Data\n\nRetention is the harder half of documentation, because the answer rarely reaches the derived artifacts. Training data is copied into extracts, feature stores, embeddings, caches, checkpoints and finally the model's parameters. A schedule that covers the source system and the training table and stops there leaves every derived copy in place, and the derived copies are where disclosure risk concentrates: membership inference tests whether a specific person's record was in the training set, and unintended memorization is a model reproducing fragments of the data on request.\n\nGDPR Article 17 supplies a right to erasure, and the ICO's guidance sets the operational expectations. The defensible position is written before it is discovered: where a record was used to train a model, deleting the record does not unwind the training, and machine unlearning is an active research direction rather than a dependable production guarantee at the scale of a large model (Bourtoule et al., 2021).\n\nKeeping data indefinitely in case it becomes useful is a retention decision by default. Collection without a basis is not cured by later deletion, which is the other half of the Clearview AI decision.\n\n### Exam Focus\n\nThese distinctions and traps recur in items on training and testing data.\n\n- Discrimination usually enters through the label or the proxy, not a protected attribute; deleting attributes does not remove it, because correlated features carry the signal, so test outcomes rather than features.\n- Obermeyer et al. (Science, 25 October 2019) is the proxy case: health cost stood in for health need, and re-targeting the label was estimated to raise the Black share identified for extra care from 17.7 percent to 46.5 percent.\n- The Amazon recruiting tool (Reuters, 10 October 2018) is the historical-bias case: the model learned past hiring patterns, penalising women's resumes and women's colleges.\n- A lawful basis comes before quality; implicit consent in terms of use is not enough in the EU, and counsel's written assertion is the standard for training data.\n- Secondary use is function creep: it needs a compatibility assessment or a fresh basis under GDPR Article 5(1)(b), and internal availability does not make data trainable.\n- EDPB Opinion 28/2024 sets a three-step legitimate interest test whose balancing step decides scraping cases; the web scraping guidelines remain a draft.\n- Public availability is neither a licence nor a lawful basis. Clearview AI (Dutch DPA, 3 September 2024, EUR 30.5 million) is the provenance case: the failure was collection, not model performance.\n- Provenance is origin and conditions; lineage is movement and transformation. Access logs, data minimization and retention are the classic distractors; backward lineage serves a challenged decision, forward lineage an incident investigation.\n- The Dutch childcare benefits scandal is the opposite error to a proxy: nationality and dual citizenship were used as fraud signals directly, built into the risk criteria.\n- Quality has five dimensions (accuracy, completeness, integrity, timeliness, consistency); sufficiency (volume) differs from representativeness (diversity of the serving population).\n- Selective labels: outcomes are observed only where a prior decision was taken, so training data reflects past policy, and the group least served is least represented.\n- Training data teaches, validation data tunes, test data judges. Leakage is overlap between training and test data, including preprocessing fitted before the split, entity duplication, and benchmark contamination.\n- An aggregate accuracy figure is a weighted average that can hide a subgroup failure, so bias testing reports subgroup metrics with sample sizes and a pre-declared threshold. Counterfactual fairness is closest to a regulator's question.\n- Demographic parity, equalized odds and calibration conflict where base rates differ, and the conflict is a theorem (Kleinberg et al., 2016; Chouldechova, 2016); choosing among them is a documented governance decision.\n- The disparate impact ratio divides one group's rate by the most favoured group's. New York City requires Local Law 144 impact ratios to be disclosed without a pass or fail threshold.\n- Rows to read in the conflict table: equal error rates leave different approval rates; equal approval rates are bought by approving more of the lower-base-rate group's defaulters.\n- Synthetic data lowers privacy risk without removing it: a generator trained on personal data can reproduce real records, and synthetic output inherits its provenance.\n- A datasheet describes a dataset, a data card summarises one, a model card describes a model, and a system card describes the deployed system.\n- Retention reaches derived artifacts: checkpoints, embeddings, feature stores and caches. Deleting a source record does not unwind a training run, and machine unlearning is not yet a production guarantee.\n- Label definitions, annotator instructions and the annotator pool are governance artifacts, and a systematic disagreement along demographic lines is a finding about the label.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe following terms are used in this chapter and defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): Data Acquisition, Data Governance, Data Quality, Data Sheet, Data Lineage, Data Provenance, Data Poisoning, Algorithmic Bias, Bias (Systematic Deviation), Bias Testing, Counterfactual Fairness, Data Dependency, Data Quality Review, Fairness, Fit-for-Purpose, Responsible AI, Training and Testing, Data Annotation, Ground Truth, Overfitting, Underfitting, Reinforcement Learning with Human Feedback (RLHF), Synthetic Data, Test Data (Evaluation Data), Transparency (System), Precision, Recall.\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [EU AI Act, Article 10 (data and data governance)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/10/)\n- [EU AI Act, Article 15 (accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/15/)\n- [EU AI Act, Article 53 (obligations for providers of general-purpose AI models)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/53/)\n- [GDPR, Article 5 (principles, including purpose limitation)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-5-gdpr/)\n- [GDPR, Article 9 (special categories)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-9-gdpr/)\n- [GDPR, Article 17 (right to erasure)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-17-gdpr/)\n- [EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2024-12/edpb_opinion_202428_ai-models_en.pdf)\n- [EDPB draft Guidelines 03/2026 on web scraping](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/public-consultations/guidelines-032026-on-web-scraping-in-the-context-of-generative-ai_en)\n- [ICO guidance on AI and data protection](https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/guidance-on-ai-and-data-protection/)\n- [ICO guidance on the right to erasure](https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/individual-rights/individual-rights/right-to-erasure/)\n- [Datasheets for Datasets (Gebru et al.)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09010)\n- [Model Cards for Model Reporting (Mitchell et al.)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993)\n- [Data Cards (Pushkarna et al.)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.01075)\n- [ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839)\n- [NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf)\n- [NIST SP 1270, Towards a Standard for Identifying and Managing Bias in AI](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.1270.pdf)\n- [NIST AI 100-2, Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2023.pdf)\n- [Suresh and Guttag, sources of harm across the machine learning life cycle](https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.10002)\n- [The Selective Labels Problem (Lakkaraju et al.)](https://www.cs.cornell.edu/home/kleinber/kdd17-selective.pdf)\n- [Kleinberg et al., Inherent Trade-Offs in the Fair Determination of Risk Scores](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.05807)\n- [Chouldechova, Fair prediction with disparate impact](https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.07524)\n- [Hardt et al., Equality of Opportunity in Supervised Learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.02413)\n- [Fairness and Machine Learning (Barocas, Hardt and Narayanan)](https://fairmlbook.org/)\n- [Fairlearn](https://fairlearn.org/)\n- [AIF360 (Trusted AI)](https://github.com/Trusted-AI/AIF360)\n- [What-If Tool](https://pair-code.github.io/what-if-tool/)\n- [EEOC Uniform Guidelines questions and answers](https://www.eeoc.gov/laws/guidance/questions-and-answers-clarify-and-provide-common-interpretation-uniform-guidelines)\n- [Synthetic Data, Anonymisation Groundhog Day (Stadler et al.)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.07018)\n- [The Secret Sharer (Carlini et al.)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08232)\n- [Extracting Training Data from Large Language Models (Carlini et al.)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.07805)\n- [Machine Unlearning (Bourtoule et al.)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.03817)\n- [Racial Bias in Hate Speech and Abusive Language Detection Datasets (Davidson et al.)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.12516)\n- [Whose Opinions Do Language Models Reflect? (Santurkar et al.)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.17548)\n- [NLP Evaluation in trouble (Sainz et al.)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.18018)\n- [Reuters: Amazon scraps secret AI recruiting tool that showed bias against women (10 October 2018)](https://www.reuters.com/article/business/amazon-scraps-secret-ai-recruiting-tool-that-showed-bias-against-women-idUSL2N1VB1FQ/)\n- [Obermeyer et al., Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations (Science, 25 October 2019)](https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aax2342)\n- [Dutch DPA decision on Clearview AI (3 September 2024)](https://www.autoriteitpersoonsgegevens.nl/en/documents/decision-fine-clearview-ai)\n- [Amnesty International, Xenophobic Machines](https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2021/10/xenophobic-machines-dutch-child-benefit-scandal/)\n- [New York State Department of Financial Services, Report on Apple Card Investigation (23 March 2021)](https://www.dfs.ny.gov/reports_and_publications/press_releases/pr202103231)\n- [New York State Comptroller, audit of Local Law 144 enforcement (2 December 2025)](https://www.osc.ny.gov/state-agencies/audits/2025/12/02/enforcement-local-law-144-automated-employment-decision-tools)\n- [Garante v. OpenAI (20 December 2024)](https://www.garanteprivacy.it/home/docweb/-/docweb-display/docweb/10085432)\n- [Getty Images v Stability AI (UK judgment, 4 November 2025)](https://www.judiciary.uk/judgments/getty-images-v-stability-ai/)\n- [Directive (EU) 2019/790 (text and data mining)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/790/oj)\n\n## Chapter 10: Release and Monitoring\n\nRelease is where a governance program stops making claims about a design and starts making them about a running system: BOK competency III.C, the gate, the testing, the documentation, the change that reopens it, and the monitoring afterwards.\n\nTwo distinctions carry the weight. An evaluation samples a distribution, while an adversary chooses its input, so normal-use testing cannot speak to hostile use. A gate is one-time and artifact-specific; monitoring never finishes, and passing a gate does not license the version that follows it.\n\nThe chapter flags where its evidence is thin: drift detection requires a baseline and a ground truth, many systems have neither, and the record holds no case of it triggering a retrain or rollback, only failures to detect (10.8).\n\nThe sections run in lifecycle order:\n\n- 10.1 Release gates and who signs\n- 10.2 Pre-deployment testing: functional, performance and adversarial\n- 10.3 Why normal-use testing fails against hostile use\n- 10.4 Model cards and system documentation as release artifacts\n- 10.5 Staged rollout: pilots, canary and shadow\n- 10.6 Change control and re-evaluation\n- 10.7 Monitoring in production: what to log and what to alert on\n- 10.8 Drift, degradation and what detecting them requires\n- 10.9 Rollback, kill switch and the safe state\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Microsoft Tay | BBC News, 25 March 2016 (launched 23 March 2016) | Adversarial and misuse testing, and what normal-use testing cannot see |\n| Tesla Autopilot recall 23V-838 | NHTSA Part 573 defect report filed 12 December 2023, 2,031,220 vehicles | A release delivered as corrective software, verified after deployment |\n| NHTSA Recall Query RQ24009 | ODI resume opened 25 April 2024 | A regulator testing whether a released remedy was adequate |\n| UK Post Office Horizon | Post Office Horizon IT Inquiry, final report volume 1, 8 July 2025 | Errors undetected for years, and over-trust in the system over the people it accused |\n| FDA predetermined change control plans | Final guidance and guiding principles for AI-enabled medical devices | The release gate, pre-authorized change and the total product life cycle |\n\n### 10.1 Release Gates and Who Signs\n\nA **release gate** is a defined point before a system reaches users at which stated criteria must be evidenced and a named person accepts the release; its record must show what was checked, what was found and who decided.\n\nA gate checks seven things:\n\n1. Documentation is complete: model cards, system cards, test reports, licences.\n2. Risk mitigations are implemented, or deferrals approved in writing.\n3. Security hardening and incident response are validated.\n4. Monitoring is instrumented and thresholds are set.\n5. Compliance is verified against the register of applicable law.\n6. Version control and traceability are intact, so the artifact released is the artifact approved.\n7. Users and operations staff are trained.\n\nSign-offs divide along functions (legal, engineering, security, privacy, governance, independent validation), with one accountable business owner accepting the release; the team that built the model should not certify it. A deferral is a gate's most informative line: a limitation accepted with a rationale, an owner and an expiry is governance; the same limitation found after release is an incident.\n\n### 10.2 Pre-Deployment Testing: Functional, Performance and Adversarial\n\n**Functional testing** asks whether the system does the task, sliced by subgroup and condition, since one headline metric hides where it fails.\n\n**Performance testing** asks whether the system survives its workload: latency and throughput, resource use under load, and behaviour during outages. **Stress testing** exercises expected and edge-case workloads; it is a performance technique, not a security one.\n\n**Adversarial testing** asks what an attacker can do. **Red teaming** adds prompt injection, including indirect injection through retrieved content, jailbreaks, model extraction, and poisoning of training or retrieval data.\n\n**Misuse and abuse testing** asks what a careless or hostile user does after release, including over-trust and workarounds around the control; it is the family most often skipped.\n\n**Security testing** is a fourth family, and it is distinct from the adversarial one. Adversarial testing asks whether the model can be made to behave wrongly; security testing asks whether the surrounding system can be breached, treating the model as one component of an attack surface that also includes the API, the retrieval store, the tool and plugin layer, the training pipeline and the deployment environment. Penetration testing, dependency and supply-chain scanning, and access-control review belong here. A model that resists jailbreaks can still sit behind an unauthenticated endpoint or a retrieval corpus an attacker can write to, which is why the two families are reported separately rather than collapsed into one line.\n\nTwo rules decide whether evidence counts: test the release candidate, since evidence attaches to an artifact, and test the system rather than the model alone, since the deployed system is a pipeline of feeds and features.\n\n### 10.3 Why Normal-Use Testing Fails Against Hostile Use\n\n**Microsoft Tay** is the case that made the gap visible. Microsoft launched a conversational bot on Twitter on 23 March 2016; within a day it was posting inflammatory content and the company shut it down. The failure was not a model that worked for cooperative testers and then broke: it was a system released into a population containing people who wanted to break it, with no testing that modelled them, and the learning loop made the attack durable because hostile input became training signal.\n\n### 10.4 Model Cards and System Documentation as Release Artifacts\n\nThree documents are often conflated. A **model card** is a short document released with a trained model (Mitchell et al., 2019). A **system card** describes the deployed system: components, human process, environment and monitoring. A **datasheet** describes the dataset.\n\nA model card carries four contents that do governance work:\n\n- **Intended use:** who the system is for, on which population; the reference point for secondary use.\n- **Out-of-scope use:** the settings the evidence does not cover, where the commonest harm sits.\n- **Performance by subgroup:** a distribution rather than a headline figure, where a fairness problem surfaces before a complaint.\n- **Limitations:** the boundary of what the organization will defend in writing.\n\nArticle 13 of the EU AI Act requires instructions for use stating intended purpose, performance and limitations.\n\n### 10.5 Staged Rollout: Pilots, Canary and Shadow\n\n**Shadow deployment** runs the new version on real inputs and records its outputs without using them. No decision changes, so it is the cheapest comparison, but with nothing following from those outputs there is no outcome data for the new version.\n\n**A pilot** is limited real use, with human cover over a bounded population. A pilot is a release, not a test: once real people are affected, the gate, documentation and monitoring duties apply; in the EU it carries conditions under Article 60 and informed consent under Article 61.\n\n**A canary deployment** routes a small share of real traffic to the new version, the incumbent serving the rest. The design question is the abort criterion; a canary without one is a slow full release.\n\n### 10.6 Change Control and Re-Evaluation\n\nAnything that can change an output is a change, whether or not anyone touched the model; define the change classes and gate each.\n\n| Change | What it invalidates, and what it needs |\n|---|---|\n| Threshold or configuration | The operating point, not the training. Re-run the evaluation |\n| Retrain on new data | The validation evidence. Revalidate, then compare in shadow |\n| Vendor model version update | The whole evaluation, and a change you did not make. Enforce notice and re-test |\n| Process change (new user group, workflow) | Intended-use statement and oversight design |\n\nA change invalidates the evidence for the part that changed, and the registry pairs each artifact with its approval, so a version with no approval is unapproved. Re-evaluation follows a threshold breach, an incident, a regulatory change, a scheduled review or a materially different version; Article 25 of the EU AI Act can also move provider obligations onto a deployer that substantially modifies a system.\n\nThe FDA model is the clearest: AI-enabled medical devices follow a **total product life cycle**, and a manufacturer can pre-authorize a **predetermined change control plan** covering what it intends to change, how each change will be verified and how safety will be monitored; changes inside the plan need no new submission; those outside it do.\n\n### 10.7 Monitoring in Production: What to Log and What to Alert On\n\nMonitoring reports what is happening; **observability** answers why a particular output happened, needing traceable prediction paths and logged versions.\n\nWhat to log, at minimum:\n\n- Per decision or response: model version and configuration, input features or prompt, output and score, reason codes or explanation payload, guardrail action, and retention and access control.\n- The human action (accept, override, refer, escalate) with actor and reason, because override behaviour cannot be monitored if unrecorded.\n- The outcome, when it arrives, linked to the decision: the ground truth, and the hardest part (10.8).\n\nWhat to alert on, each with a threshold, an owner and a defined response:\n\n- Input or prediction distribution movement beyond a stated tolerance against the baseline.\n- Missing-value rates, format violations, and runs of identical values in a field that should vary; a field silently set to one repeated value reads as valid data and defeats a missing-value alarm.\n- Human override rate by subgroup over time, often the first signal that the process has broken while the model behaves as built.\n\nA production failure is detected only in a comparison: inputs or outputs now against release, or intended outcomes against actual ones; accuracy needs labels that arrive late or never.\n\n### 10.8 Drift, Degradation and What Detecting Them Requires\n\n**Data drift**, also called **covariate shift**, is a change in the statistical properties of inputs, so production data diverges from training data. **Label drift** is a change in the distribution of outcomes, and **concept drift** a change in the relationship between them, the dangerous one, because the inputs look familiar while the outputs are wrong.\n\nDetection needs a recorded baseline: a two-sample test on inputs, such as Kolmogorov-Smirnov or chi-square, or the **population stability index** against the training distribution, as does monitoring predictions and confidence. Confirming degradation needs labels that often arrive late, only for cases acted on, or never.\n\nThe honest limitation is that detection requires two things frequently absent: a **baseline**, the recorded input and output distributions at release, and a **ground truth**, an outcome signal reliable enough to score against. Many deployed systems have neither, because the baseline was never a deliverable and the ground truth is missing where the decision is a judgement or a generation.\n\nThe public record is silent in a way that is itself the finding: this book's case library holds no case in which drift detection triggered a retrain or a rollback on a named production system. That absence is not evidence that monitoring works; it fits a trigger chain rarely instrumented or documented, so what the record shows is the failure to detect.\n\n**UK Post Office Horizon** is the deepest case on that failure. Horizon produced apparent shortfalls treated as reliable, and hundreds of sub-postmasters were wrongly accused of theft and fraud; the inquiry's first volume, published on 8 July 2025, called it one of the UK's biggest miscarriages of justice. The lesson is not that a threshold was missed: the system was treated as authoritative, so discrepancies were attributed to the people it accused rather than to the system or its data, and no error signal was routed back into an investigation. Automation bias turned a monitoring gap into a governance choice.\n\n### 10.9 Rollback, Kill Switch and the Safe State\n\nThree controls are routinely confused. A **fail-safe plan** is a fallback decided in advance: halt safely, hand over to a backup system, or revert to a manual process, one of the redundancy solutions in EU AI Act Article 15(4). A **rollback procedure** reverses a release by redeploying the prior approved artifact, where the fail-safe responds to behaviour. A **kill switch** disables the system, and Article 14 expects high-risk systems to be interruptible by a stop button.\n\nThree requirements decide whether these work:\n\n- Define the safe state in the domain: for a consequential decision, referral to a human rather than an automated refusal.\n- Keep the prior version deployable, because rollback is cheap only where the artifact, its configuration and its environment are versioned; an unrehearsed rollback is a plan, not a procedure.\n- Make the switch reachable by the person on duty, not only its builders; name in advance who may pull it, and log every activation.\n\nOver-trust is a fallback problem, not only a design one: where safety depends on a human staying engaged, the gate has to test the human behaviour. Tesla's 23V-838 defect report, filed on 12 December 2023 and covering 2,031,220 vehicles equipped with Autopilot, described the driver controls as possibly insufficient to prevent misuse and remedied the defect by software update; NHTSA opened Recall Query RQ24009 on 25 April 2024 to test whether the remedy worked. A corrective release is still a release, verified on the deployed population rather than before it.\n\n### Exam Focus\n\nThis chapter maps to BOK competency III.C, governing release, monitoring and maintenance.\n\n- A release gate is a decision with an artifact rather than a meeting, and its record must show what was checked, what was found and who signed. Reviewers must be independent of the team that built the system.\n- A deferral at the gate, accepted with a rationale, an owner and an expiry, is governance; the same limitation found after release is an incident.\n- The pre-market file for a high-risk EU AI Act system is the conformity assessment (Article 43), technical documentation (Article 11 and Annex IV), the declaration of conformity, CE marking and database registration, and the post-market monitoring plan (Article 72) is written before release.\n- Functional testing covers correctness and subgroup slices, performance testing covers load, latency and failover, and stress testing is a performance technique rather than a security one.\n- Red teaming is the adversarial family, and prompt injection, jailbreaks, model extraction and poisoning belong to it. Misuse and abuse testing asks what a careless or hostile user does after release.\n- Microsoft Tay is the standard pre-deployment testing failure for a generative system: the gap was adversarial and misuse testing, not normal-use testing, and the learning loop made the attack durable.\n- A model card describes the model, a system card the deployed system, a datasheet the dataset. The four contents that do governance work are intended use, out-of-scope use, performance by subgroup, and limitations.\n- Where a system is released to another organization, the card and the instructions for use travel with it, because the downstream deployer inherits duties it can only meet if it knows what it has.\n- An evaluation samples a distribution while an adversary optimizes inside it, so normal-use testing measures cooperation rather than robustness.\n- Shadow deployment changes no decision and so produces no outcome data for the new version; a pilot is a release rather than a test; a canary needs an abort criterion, an owner and a share large enough to produce a signal.\n- A corrective release is still a release: recall 23V-838 remedied 2,031,220 vehicles by software update, and NHTSA opened Recall Query RQ24009 to test whether the remedy was adequate, which put the verification after deployment.\n- Change control invalidates the evidence for the part that changed. A change outside the predetermined envelope requires a new authorization, which is the FDA predetermined change control plan model, and a substantial modification can move provider duties onto a deployer under Article 25.\n- Monitoring says what is happening and observability says why. Accuracy needs labels and is therefore lagging; the early warning sits in inputs, proxies and human override behaviour.\n- A field silently set to one repeated value reads as valid data and defeats a missing-value alarm, and a rising override rate is often the first signal that the process has broken while the model is behaving as built.\n- Data drift is a change in inputs, label drift a change in outcomes, and concept drift a change in the relationship between them. Kolmogorov-Smirnov, chi-square and the population stability index detect movement in inputs, and all require a recorded baseline.\n- No verified case in this book's case library shows drift detection triggering a retrain or rollback on a named production system. The record shows failures to detect, and the correct answer to a drift question is normally that detection requires a baseline and a ground truth, and that many systems have neither.\n- A fail-safe responds to behaviour, a rollback reverses a release, and a kill switch disables the system. Article 15(4) covers backup and fail-safe plans and Article 14 covers interruption. An unrehearsed rollback is a plan rather than a procedure, and an unrecorded threshold breach is the same as no threshold.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe following terms are used in this chapter and defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): Release Gate, Production Readiness Evaluation, Model Card, System Card, Data Sheet, Stress Testing (AI), Red Teaming, Misuse Testing, Continuous Monitoring, Observability, Data Drift, Concept Drift, Label Drift, Covariate Shift, Population Stability Index (PSI), Baseline, Ground Truth, Shadow Mode, Canary Deployment, Rollback Procedure, Fail-Safe Plan, Kill Switch, Change Control, Post-Market Monitoring, Retraining, Feedback Loop.\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [Microsoft Tay, Microsoft apology over racist chatbot fiasco (BBC News, 25 March 2016)](https://www.bbc.com/news/technology-35902104): the canonical pre-deployment testing failure for a learning system\n- [NHTSA Part 573 Safety Recall Report 23V-838 (filed 12 December 2023)](https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/rcl/2023/RCLRPT-23V838-8276.PDF): the defect report covering 2,031,220 vehicles, remedied by software update\n- [NHTSA Office of Defects Investigation, Recall Query RQ24009 (opened 25 April 2024)](https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/inv/2024/INOA-RQ24009-12046.pdf): a regulator assessing whether a released remedy was adequate\n- [Post Office Horizon IT Inquiry, final report volume 1 (8 July 2025)](https://www.postofficehorizoninquiry.org.uk/news/chair-calls-swift-action-deliver-full-and-fair-compensation-urgent-report): errors undetected for years, and over-trust in the system over the people it accused\n- [FDA, Predetermined Change Control Plans for Machine Learning-Enabled Medical Devices: guiding principles](https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/predetermined-change-control-plans-machine-learning-enabled-medical-devices-guiding-principles): pre-authorized model change and the total product life cycle\n- [FDA, Marketing Submission Recommendations for a Predetermined Change Control Plan for AI-Enabled Device Software Functions (final guidance)](https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/marketing-submission-recommendations-predetermined-change-control-plan-artificial-intelligence): what a change control plan must contain\n- [Model Cards for Model Reporting (Mitchell et al., 2019)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993): the model card format and its four governance contents\n- [Datasheets for Datasets](https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09010): the dataset documentation a system card and datasheet owe\n- [EU AI Act, Article 43 (conformity assessment)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/43/): the pre-release conformity route for high-risk systems\n- [EU AI Act, Article 15 (accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/15/): Article 15(4) is where backup and fail-safe plans sit\n- [EU AI Act, Article 14 (human oversight)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/): the ability to interrupt a high-risk system through a stop button\n- [EU AI Act, Article 9 (risk management system)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/9/): risk management as a continuous iterative process across the life cycle\n- [EU AI Act, Article 60 (testing in real world conditions)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/60/) and [Article 61 (informed consent)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/61/): the legal frame for pilots\n- [EU AI Act, Article 72 (post-market monitoring)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/72/): the monitoring plan that must be written before release\n- [EU AI Act, Article 73 (serious incident reporting)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/73/): the reporting clock that monitoring and triage must meet\n- [EU AI Act, Article 25 (responsibilities along the AI value chain)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/): how a substantial modification can move provider duties downstream\n- [EU AI Act, Article 13 (transparency and instructions for use)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/13/): intended purpose, performance characteristics and known limitations\n- [EU AI Act, Annex IV (technical documentation)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/4/): the documentation contents a readiness review has to see\n- [Bank of England PRA, SS1/23 model risk management principles for banks](https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/prudential-regulation/publication/2023/may/model-risk-management-principles-for-banks): independent validation, tiered approval and ongoing monitoring\n- [NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI 100-1)](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf) and the [AI RMF Playbook](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/): MEASURE and MANAGE actions on monitoring, change and deactivation\n- [NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf): pre-deployment testing and monitoring guidance for generative systems\n- [NIST AI 100-2, Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2023.pdf): the attack taxonomy behind red teaming and adversarial test design\n- [MITRE ATLAS](https://atlas.mitre.org/): adversarial tactics and techniques for AI systems, useful for scoping red team work\n- [OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications](https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/): prompt injection, insecure output handling and the practical attack classes\n- [NIST SP 1270, Towards a Standard for Identifying and Managing Bias in AI](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.1270.pdf): the human-cognitive category that covers automation bias\n- [ICO, explaining decisions made with AI](https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/): keeping explanations accurate and available after deployment\n- [Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems (Sculley et al., 2015)](https://papers.nips.cc/paper/5656-hidden-technical-debt-in-machine-learning-systems.pdf): why the surrounding pipeline, not the model, is where production failure concentrates\n- [Failing Loudly: an empirical study of methods for detecting dataset shift](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.11953): what drift detection methods actually require to work\n- [OECD AI Incidents Monitor](https://oecd.ai/en/incidents): where AI incidents and hazards are recorded, including the reporting often absent from the record\n\n# Part IV: Governing AI Deployment and Use\n\n*Can we live with what we chose?*\n\nThe fourth domain covers choosing a system, adopting it and living with it. It is the most scenario-heavy part of the syllabus, and most items test one link in a chain of decisions. Its most common trap is a distractor that sounds prudent but belongs to a different link: renegotiating a vendor contract after an incident is Chapter 12 work, while the immediate response to that incident is containment, correction and customer communication under Chapter 13. Expect a scenario that asks which EU AI Act role an organization occupies, because the duties divide by role.\n\n### Exam at a Glance\n\nDomain IV carries 21 to 25 of the 100 scored items, tied with Domain III for the largest share, and it is the most scenario-heavy part of the syllabus.\n\n| Competency | What it covers | Questions | Chapter |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| IV.A Evaluate key factors and risks in the decision to deploy | Context of the use case; AI model types; deployment options, including agentic architectures | 6 to 8 | 11 |\n| IV.B Perform key assessment activities | An impact assessment on the selected system; key terms and risks in the vendor or licensing agreement; risks and opportunities of a proprietary model | 5 to 7 | 12 |\n| IV.C Govern deployment and use | Policies, procedures and ethics; continuous monitoring and maintenance; periodic assessments; documentation; secondary use and downstream harm; external communication; deactivation and localization | 9 to 11 | 13 |\n\nThe ranges sum to 20 to 26, not the domain's 21 to 25, so learn each figure as published. IV.C alone is larger than either other competency and than most whole knowledge areas elsewhere. IV.B is small but holds the proprietary-model distinction, which separates good answers from bad ones in deployment scenarios.\n\nBOK v2.1 made four changes to Domain IV.\n\n- IV.A now names agentic architectures beside fine-tuning and retrieval augmented generation as ways of deploying a model, the domain's only new technical content and the reason agentic AI appears in the blueprint's introduction as something the certification covers.\n- IV.B's \"open source agreement\" became \"licensing agreement\", putting proprietary and open-source terms under one heading.\n- IV.B's proprietary-model item became \"risks and opportunities\". The older wording read as pure exposure, and v2.1 wants the control and capability argument beside it.\n- The \"identify applicable laws\" indicators left III.A and IV.B, and \"model\" became \"system\" throughout, matching the BOK's shift from governing a model to governing a system in its deployment context.\n\n**Chapters.** Chapter 11 covers the decision to deploy: whether the use case justifies the tool, which model fits, where and how it runs, and what changes when the system acts rather than advises. Chapter 12 covers assessing what you adopt: an impact assessment on the selected system, the contract's key terms and risks, licences and open weights, and proprietary models bought and built. Chapter 13 covers the long work: risk management and forecasting, audits and assurance, incident management, business records, external communication, and retirement, deactivation and localization.\n\n## Chapter 11: Deciding to Deploy\n\nThe decision to deploy is the first link in the chain Domain IV follows: whether the use case justifies a model at all, which route to a model to take, whether the organization is ready to run one, what would count as success, how much autonomy the system gets, and who answers for the go decision. Every later control inherits these answers, because the record written before launch sets the risk surface that impact assessment, contract review, monitoring and retirement govern.\n\nTwo questions organize the chapter: what would have to be true for this deployment to be justified, and what would have to be true for it to keep running. A deployment that cannot answer the first has no success metric; one that cannot answer the second has no owner, no threshold and no exit. Three framing points hold: not deploying is a legitimate outcome a governed process must be able to reach; the route to a model is a governance choice, fixing how much control the organization keeps over data, model change and exit; and a model plausible case by case can still be wrong in aggregate.\n\n- Section 11.1 vets the use case against the go/no-go criteria applied before commitment.\n- Section 11.2 compares building, buying, fine-tuning and adopting an API, and what each costs in control.\n- Section 11.3 assesses readiness across data, people, process, monitoring and legal basis.\n- Section 11.4 sets success metrics before deployment, harm metrics as well as business ones.\n- Section 11.5 fixes the level of autonomy, and the conditions for raising it.\n- Section 11.6 describes the pilot and what it must be designed to prove.\n- Section 11.7 covers the decision record and who owns the go decision.\n- Section 11.8 treats scaling risk, plausible case by case and wrong in aggregate.\n- Section 11.9 treats deciding not to deploy as a legitimate governed outcome.\n- Section 11.10 follows the second-order effects downstream.\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Zillow Offers | Zillow Group Q3 2021 shareholder letter, SEC Form 8-K exhibit 99.3, 2 November 2021 | Scaling risk, a valuation model plausible case by case and collectively wrong, and a controlled wind-down |\n| DORA and NIS2 | Regulation (EU) 2022/2554 (DORA); Directive (EU) 2022/2555 (NIS2) | Third-party ICT risk, concentration risk, mandatory contractual provisions, supply chain security, exit |\n\n### 11.1 Vetting the Use Case\n\nThe commonest failure here is starting from what a model can do. A deployment justified by capability has no objective in the organization's terms, so no later measurement can say whether it worked; it is vetted as an objective.\n\n**Least agency** comes first, named by the OWASP GenAI Security Project in its [Top 10 for Agentic Applications](https://genai.owasp.org/resource/owasp-top-10-for-agentic-applications-for-2026/): a system gets no more autonomy than its task requires. Where a process is fixed and repeatable, a deterministic workflow is cheaper, testable and harder to attack, so eligibility rules and lookups belong in code; the first question about a proposed agent is whether the use case needs one at all.\n\nFour criteria do the vetting. Is the use prohibited outright? EU AI Act [Article 5](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/5/) lists practices no readiness argument can justify. Does the task need inference at all, or does a rule answer it? Who is affected, and can they refuse?\n\nVetting ends in one of four outcomes, and three are legitimate: approved as proposed, approved with a narrower scope, deferred until a stated condition is met, or rejected. ISO/IEC [42005:2025](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/107659) treats impacts as two-sided, benefits as well as harms.\n\n### 11.2 Build, Buy, Fine-Tune or Call an API\n\nFour routes to a model are open, and they differ less in capability than in three controls: over data, over model change and over exit.\n\n**Adopting a vendor API** is the fastest route. The organization sends inputs to a model it does not host, giving up visibility into training data and behavior and accepting binding terms and vendor dependency for updates and continuity. **Buying a finished system** packages it into a product with a workflow attached, moving more of the build cost and the design decisions out of reach.\n\n**Fine-tuning an open-weights model** adapts weights through additional training on targeted data, buying consistent behavior and domain fit at the cost of a retraining pipeline and knowledge frozen at training time; because it changes the model, it triggers change management, fresh evaluation and, in a regulated context, reassessment. **Building from scratch** is rarely proportionate, justified only for a capability nobody offers.\n\nProprietary and open-source are not a safety ranking: open weights are still licensed, and licence terms govern commercial use, attribution and what happens to code that incorporates the model, which is why BOK v2.1 renamed \"open source agreement\" to **licensing agreement**.\n\nTwo further axes decide what a route costs in practice. **Model size** separates frontier models from compact ones: a smaller model is cheaper to serve, easier to place on premises or at the edge, and often sufficient for a narrow task, while a larger one buys general capability the use case may never exercise. **Quantization** lowers the precision of a model's weights and **distillation** trains a smaller model to imitate a larger one; both cut inference cost and latency, and both trade away some accuracy. Either is a change of system for validation purposes rather than a deployment detail, so the re-evaluation runs against the same subgroup and robustness tests as the original model.\n\nThe route's consequence is contractual before it is technical. DORA, Regulation (EU) [2022/2554](https://www.eiopa.europa.eu/digital-operational-resilience-act-dora_en), requires financial entities to manage ICT third-party risk, including concentration risk, and fixes key contractual provisions for ICT providers, applying from 17 January 2025. NIS2, Directive (EU) [2022/2555](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/nis2-directive), requires supply chain security between an entity and its suppliers, with a transposition deadline of 17 October 2024. Monitoring, audit rights and exit terms are therefore a legal expectation rather than a negotiating preference, and one provider's outage reaches every deployer that chose it.\n\n### 11.3 Readiness Across Data, People, Process, Monitoring and Legal Basis\n\nThe BOK names five **readiness factors**, applied in order: business objectives, performance requirements, data availability, ethical considerations and workforce readiness. Each can stop the deployment; a later factor cannot repair an earlier one.\n\n**Business objectives** restate the vetting outcome as a measurable claim in the organization's terms. **Performance requirements** turn that claim into the thresholds monitoring will later measure against, graded on the data the system will actually see: accuracy against an approved source, response time, and a floor on escalation to a human. That escalation floor earns its own line because its inverse, containment rate, the share of contacts resolved without a person, is the metric a misaligned system optimizes: a cost metric wearing a success metric's clothes.\n\n**Data availability** and **data suitability** are separate questions: availability asks whether the data exists at the volume and quality the task needs; suitability adds whether the organization is legally entitled to use it, whether it represents the affected population, and whether its lawful basis is documented. Repurposing records to train a model can breach the purpose limitation and minimisation principles of GDPR [Article 5](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-5-gdpr/), so a reused corpus needs a compatibility analysis or a new lawful basis first.\n\n**Ethical considerations** are weighed rather than measured: who benefits, who bears the harm, and whether the use would survive public explanation. Some are settled by law, such as a prohibited practice under Article 5 or a DPIA under GDPR [Article 35](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-35-gdpr/).\n\n**Workforce readiness** is a deployment gate, not a training afterthought: can intended users operate the system and tell when to distrust it, can administrators manage and monitor it, and does enough skill survive to carry on if the system is unavailable? A fallback nobody can execute is not a fallback. Readiness on the **process** side is who owns each document the system draws on and which limits are enforced in code rather than in a prompt; on the **monitoring** side, which thresholds belong to named owners.\n\n### 11.4 Success Metrics, Business and Harm\n\nMetrics are set before deployment, because a metric invented after go-live measures whatever the system is doing. Two sets are required, and the second is usually missing.\n\nBusiness metrics state the objective: handling time, resolution rate, cost per contact. Harm metrics state what must not happen: which groups are treated differently and by how much, how often the system is wrong and who bears it, and how often a human overrides and whether the overrides cluster.\n\nEvery threshold needs a number, an owner and a consequence. One with no owner is monitored by nobody, and one with no consequence is monitored by nobody twice. The consequence, whether escalation, pause, rollback or recorded acceptance, is written into the decision record before launch so the response to a breach is not negotiated after one.\n\n### 11.5 Setting the Level of Autonomy\n\nAutonomy is chosen at deployment, not inherited from the model. Two independent dials describe it. **Action space** is what the system can do, fixed by the tools it may call and the permissions on them: sandbox only, internal or external systems, read or write. **Autonomy** is how freely it decides, set by its instructions and the level of human involvement.\n\nSingapore's Infocomm Media Development Authority, in its [Model AI Governance Framework for Agentic AI](https://www.imda.gov.sg/-/media/imda/files/about/emerging-tech-and-research/artificial-intelligence/mgf-for-agentic-ai.pdf), names four levels of human involvement: the agent proposes and a human approves each step; collaboration; exception-based approval; and the agent operates while a human observes.\n\nThe five harms to map are erroneous actions, unauthorised actions, biased or unfair actions, data breaches, and disruption to connected systems. Six controls answer: least-privilege, scoped and non-transferable permissions never greater than the approving person's own; deterministic controls over prompt-based ones, so a tool is blocked by an access control rather than by a prompt; system-level approval checkpoints audited for rubber-stamping; a unique verifiable identity per agent; a kill switch that revokes credentials and has a manual fallback; and fail-safe behavior, denying the action when the approval service is down, where a time-out that lets the agent proceed is **fail-open**.\n\nNo separate legal category exists for agents. EU AI Act [Article 3(1)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/) defines an AI system as one designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy whose output can influence physical or virtual environments, and agents fit squarely. The risk tier follows the use, not the architecture: job screening falls under Annex III point 4(a), credit scoring under 5(b), and a deployer that repurposes a general-purpose system into a high-risk use becomes its provider under [Article 25(1)(c)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/). An agent that decides makes an automated decision governed by GDPR [Article 22](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-22-gdpr/), and Recital 71 names automatic refusal of a credit application and e-recruiting without human intervention.\n\n### 11.6 The Pilot and What It Must Prove\n\nA pilot is a limited rollout with pre-registered success criteria, and the second half of that definition decides whether it proves anything. A pilot run to demonstrate that the system works will demonstrate that the system works; one designed to test the thresholds returns a number.\n\nA pilot must prove that the system meets its thresholds on real inputs rather than test data; that the harm metrics hold, which usually requires enough volume to see a subgroup at all; and that overseers intervene in practice, evidenced by an override log rather than a policy statement. A pilot at too low a volume proves nothing about subgroup harm. Expansion should be gated, with each stage carrying its own thresholds and exit.\n\n### 11.7 The Decision Record and Who Owns the Go Decision\n\nThe deployment decision is recorded, and the record is deliberately short: one page carrying the objective, the performance thresholds, the data sources and their lawful basis, the benefits and the harms, the autonomy setting, the fallback staffing and the exit conditions, each line with an owner. It beats a long assessment nobody rereads, and it exists so a later question about performance or accepted risk has an answer.\n\nOwnership of the go decision needs one named accountable person, and accountability is not delegable. It does not sit with the vendor, which supplies a component; it does not sit with the model, which is not a legal actor; and it does not sit with a committee in which everyone shares the decision and nobody owns the outcome. A council may set risk appetite and approve sensitive uses, but the deployment decision has one accountable executive named in the record, and the record holds the baseline for every later reassessment.\n\n### 11.8 Scaling Risk\n\nSpeed and scale is the characteristic that makes deployment decisions hard. A flaw in a manual process harms the people it touches and is caught locally; one mis-weighted feature in a model harms every case it processes before anyone notices.\n\nZillow Offers is the worked example. Zillow Group announced on 2 November 2021 that it would wind down its algorithmic home-buying business and cut roughly 25 percent of its workforce, after the business could not forecast future home prices accurately enough to buy homes safely. The [Q3 2021 shareholder letter](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1617640/000161764021000085/exhibit993.htm), filed as exhibit 99.3 to a Form 8-K, records the wind-down and the workforce reduction. The governance lesson is the shape of the failure: the valuation model was plausible case by case and, across an inventory bought at pace, collectively wrong. No individual purchase had to look absurd for the portfolio to be mispriced.\n\nScaled error is invisible to case-by-case review, which is why it needs different instruments: aggregate outcome monitoring rather than sample checking, pre-committed rollback criteria, and a limit on the rate at which the deployment may grow relative to the rate at which its errors are observed. Zillow's outcome was a controlled retirement rather than an uncontrolled collapse: what a governed rollback looks like when the decision to stop is taken early enough.\n\n### 11.9 Deciding Not to Deploy\n\nDeciding not to deploy is a legitimate governed outcome, and the vetting stage in Section 11.1 exists to make it reachable. Three forms are worth distinguishing. A **rejection** concludes that the use should not be deployed at all, on this scope or any other. A **deferral** concludes that the use is sound but a precondition is missing: a data set, a lawful basis, a monitoring capability or a fallback. A **narrowing** approves the use with less scope, less autonomy or fewer modalities.\n\nThe record differs by form: a rejection records the reason so the proposal is not re-litigated every quarter; a deferral records the condition and the trigger that will reopen the decision; a narrowing records what was removed and why.\n\n### 11.10 Second-Order Effects Downstream\n\nThose effects belong in the record, not the post-mortem.\n\nThe first is the workload the system takes and the skill it removes: if a team's routine cases move to a model and its people handle only the residue, the residue is harder and the skill that let the team catch a wrong output atrophies, so readiness has to include the ability to catch a bad output on day 300.\n\nThe second is automation bias with a witness: a review step supplies cover for the system's decision, and a reviewer who approves everything makes the deployment worse than one with no human at all. The controls are structural: overrides removed from productivity measures, and review volumes small enough for real attention.\n\nThe third is the process the deployment quietly rewrites: an exception route that reached a supervisor now reaches a queue with no owner, a source-document change alters the answers with no release, and a vendor update changes behavior mid-contract. Each needs an owner, a change process and a notification clause written down before go-live.\n\n### Exam Focus\n\n- The decision to deploy is Domain IV.A, and it is the first link in the chain that runs through impact assessment, contract review, monitoring and retirement.\n- Least agency, named by OWASP, means the right answer is sometimes that a process should not be agentic, or not use AI at all, and the first question about any agent is whether the use case needs one.\n- Vetting has four permitted outcomes (approved, narrowed, deferred, rejected), and all four are legitimate results of a governed process.\n- The five readiness factors run in order (business objectives, performance requirements, data availability, ethical considerations, workforce readiness), and each can stop a deployment.\n- Data availability differs from suitability, which adds quality, legal entitlement, representativeness and a documented lawful basis.\n- Success metrics are set before deployment and come in two sets; containment rate is a cost metric wearing a success metric's clothes, and treating it as the objective is misalignment.\n- Neither proprietary nor open source is safer by default, and treating open as unencumbered is a trap, because open weights are licensed and GPL or AGPL terms reach code that incorporates the model.\n- Build versus buy versus fine-tune versus API is decided on control over data, control over model change and control over exit, and DORA and NIS2 make monitoring, audit rights and exit terms a legal expectation rather than a preference.\n- Pilots must be designed against pre-registered criteria, and a pilot at too low a volume proves nothing about subgroup harm.\n- The go decision has one named accountable owner, accountability is not delegable to a vendor or a model, and the decision record holds the baseline for every later reassessment.\n- Scaling risk is the difference between plausible case by case and wrong in aggregate; Zillow Offers is the case, and it is caught by aggregate outcome monitoring, not by sample review.\n- Deciding not to deploy is a legitimate governed outcome, and a rejection, a deferral and a narrowing each need a different record.\n- Agent risk runs on two dials, action space and autonomy, with IMDA's four levels of human involvement and five harms: erroneous, unauthorised, and biased or unfair actions, data breaches, and disruption to connected systems.\n- Deterministic controls beat prompt instructions, and denying the action when the approval service is down is fail-safe, while a time-out that lets the agent proceed is fail-open.\n- The legal tier follows the use (job screening under Annex III 4(a), credit scoring under 5(b)), repurposing a general-purpose system into a high-risk use makes a deployer its provider under Article 25(1)(c), and an agent's automated refusal engages GDPR Article 22 and Recital 71.\n- BOK version 2.1 added agentic architectures to the deployment options and renamed \"open source agreement\" to \"licensing agreement\" so that proprietary contracts get the same reading.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe following terms are used in this chapter and defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): Action Space (Agentic AI), Agentic AI, Agent Goal Hijack, Agent Kill Switch, Business Objectives, Classic AI, Cloud, On-Premises & Edge Deployment, Deterministic Output, Fine-Tuning, Human Approval Checkpoint, Language vs. Multimodal Models, Least-Privilege Agent Permissions, Multi-Agent System, Prompt Engineering, Proprietary vs. Open-Source Models, Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG), Small vs. Large Models, Tool Misuse, Workforce Readiness.\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [NIST AI RMF Playbook (MAP and MANAGE functions)](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/): MAP 1 on context and intended purpose, and MANAGE 2.1 on non-AI alternatives\n- [NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf): deployment and operational considerations in the MEASURE and MANAGE functions\n- [EU AI Act, Article 5 (prohibited AI practices)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/5/): the uses no readiness assessment can justify\n- [EU AI Act, Article 3 (definitions)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/): the AI system definition and its varying levels of autonomy\n- [EU AI Act, Article 14 (human oversight)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/): the oversight design duty for high-risk systems\n- [EU AI Act, Article 25 (responsibilities along the AI value chain)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/): the provider shift when a deployment repurposes a system\n- [ISO/IEC 42005:2025 (AI system impact assessment)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/107659): scope, intended and unintended uses, and two-sided impacts\n- [ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (concepts and terminology)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839): AI model, AI component and the learning paradigms\n- [NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf): the risks a use-case assessment should weigh for generative deployments\n- [GDPR Article 5 (principles relating to processing)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-5-gdpr/): purpose limitation and minimisation when data is reused for AI\n- [GDPR Article 22 (automated individual decision-making)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-22-gdpr/): the right, its safeguards and the Recital 71 examples\n- [GDPR Article 35 (data protection impact assessment)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-35-gdpr/): when an assessment is required before processing\n- [Singapore IMDA, Model AI Governance Framework for Agentic AI](https://www.imda.gov.sg/-/media/imda/files/about/emerging-tech-and-research/artificial-intelligence/mgf-for-agentic-ai.pdf): action space, autonomy and the four levels of human involvement\n- [OWASP Top 10 for Large Language Model Applications](https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/): prompt injection, and why prompt-layer controls are not a boundary\n- [OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications 2026](https://genai.owasp.org/resource/owasp-top-10-for-agentic-applications-for-2026/): least agency and the risks of autonomous action\n- [Zillow Group, Q3 2021 shareholder letter (SEC Form 8-K exhibit 99.3)](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1617640/000161764021000085/exhibit993.htm): the Zillow Offers wind-down and the roughly 25 percent workforce reduction\n- [DORA (European Insurance and Occupational Pensions Authority)](https://www.eiopa.europa.eu/digital-operational-resilience-act-dora_en): third-party ICT risk, concentration risk and the key contractual provisions\n- [NIS2 Directive (European Commission)](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/nis2-directive): supply chain security between an entity and its suppliers\n\n## Chapter 12: Assessing What You Adopt\n\nChapter 3 set out vendor policy. This chapter answers a different question: what is actually true about the system the organization is about to adopt, and how anyone came to know it.\n\nAssurance is routinely confused with policy, and the confusion buys the wrong instrument: an attestation where a test was needed, a certificate where subgroup data was needed, a benchmark as an acceptance criterion. Each is a category error, and the exam tests the categories directly.\n\nAssurance is not a gate that closes at go-live: the vendor retrains the model, the population shifts, the corpus ages, the certificate expires. Whatever was established at adoption has a shelf life: someone must own the re-check and define what triggers it. That is competency IV.B.\n\n- **12.1** six assurance instruments, and the question each one answers.\n- **12.2** what a vendor's own testing proves, and what to demand instead.\n- **12.3** the model card against the system-level assessment.\n- **12.4** independent evaluation, third-party audit, and what a certificate to a management system standard proves.\n- **12.5** acceptance testing and benchmarking before adoption.\n- **12.6** the monitoring obligations that survive adoption.\n- **12.7** concentration, dependency and exit.\n- **12.8** the failure mode this chapter exists to prevent: an overstated capability.\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Apple Card underwriting review | New York State Department of Financial Services report, 23 March 2021 | What an assurance examination can and cannot establish about a third-party credit model |\n| DORA and NIS2 | Regulation (EU) 2022/2554; Directive (EU) 2022/2555 | Audit, monitoring and exit terms as a legal expectation |\n| FTC v. Evolv Technologies | FTC complaint and stipulated order, 26 November 2024 | Overstated capability, and the deployer that relied on it |\n| KPMG Australia certified to ISO/IEC 42001 | KPMG Australia and BSI Australia media release, 17 October 2024 | What third-party certification of an AI management system looks like |\n| New York State Comptroller audit of Local Law 144 enforcement | Office of the New York State Comptroller, audit 2024-N-6, 2 December 2025 | The gap between a bias-audit duty and the assurance it is meant to create |\n| Change Healthcare ransomware attack | U.S. Congressional Research Service, IN12330 | Concentration risk when one provider anchors a sector |\n| FDA predetermined change control plans | FDA guidance, content current 18 August 2025 | The regulatory model for pre-authorised model change |\n\n### 12.1 Six Instruments, Six Questions\n\nA **test** exercises a system with defined inputs and compares output with an expected result; it alone produces evidence about your configuration and users.\n\nA **benchmark** is a fixed dataset and scoring convention, usually published; it establishes relative capability on a public task, not fitness for your use case.\n\nAn **attestation** is a declaration that a state of affairs is true, usually a self-attestation such as a security questionnaire; it establishes that a statement was made, not that it is true.\n\nAn **audit** is a systematic, documented examination of evidence against defined criteria by a party with the competence and independence to form an opinion; it gives findings and reasonable, not absolute, assurance.\n\nA **certification** is a third party's formal confirmation, issued by a body accredited for the scope, that an organization, a management system, a person or a product conforms to a named standard; it establishes conformance of a scope, not that a model or output is correct.\n\nA **conformity assessment** is the process under which conformity to requirements is demonstrated at all; it establishes that a legal compliance route was followed, not that the system performs well.\n\n### 12.2 What a Vendor's Own Testing Proves\n\nA vendor's testing is evidence produced by the party with an interest in the result: it tests what it designed the system for, on data it chose, and reports the result that best describes the product.\n\nDemand the method before the number. Four requests do most of the work.\n\n**Fairness testing by subgroup.** Aggregate accuracy conceals subgroup performance: a system can be accurate overall while materially worse for a subgroup that matters. Ask for results broken out by relevant subgroups with the metric named, because fairness metrics disagree by construction: disparate impact, equal opportunity difference and group calibration can rank the same model differently.\n\nThe Apple Card examination shows how much turns on what is measured. After complaints that women received lower credit limits, the New York State Department of Financial Services reviewed underwriting data for about 400,000 New York applicants and found no fair lending violation, but also that credit scoring and the anti-discrimination laws governing credit access need modernising and that the model's complexity made the allegation hard to test.\n\n**Robustness results.** Performance on clean, in-distribution data says nothing about distribution shift, adversarial input, or out-of-scope questions. Ask for the failure modes, not only the success rate.\n\n**Model documentation.** What the system was trained on, on whose data and under what rights, what it was evaluated against, and the vendor's stated limitations and out-of-scope uses.\n\n**Evaluation methodology.** Who ran the evaluation, on what data, whether it was held out from tuning, the sample size and pass threshold, and whether anyone independent reproduced it.\n\n### 12.3 Model Cards and System-Level Assessment\n\nA **model card** documents a model: intended use, training data, evaluation metrics and results, limitations, and the authors' stated ethical considerations. It describes the model, not your deployment, and it dates.\n\nRisk is composed at deployment, not inherited from the model. The card says nothing about your prompt template, retrieval corpus, guardrails, served population, or the decision your process extracts from the output.\n\nA **system card** documents the deployed system. A system-level assessment is what the law demands of the adopting organization, and three instruments do it: the AI impact assessment, which ISO/IEC 42005 explains as the method behind the ISO/IEC 42001 requirement; the **data protection impact assessment** under GDPR Article 35; and the **fundamental rights impact assessment** under EU AI Act Article 27.\n\nRead the card as an input to an assessment of the system you will run, not a substitute for one. Its absence is a diligence finding, not a legal breach; no regulator has yet acted against a deficient model card, but a vendor that cannot describe its training data cannot be assessed at all.\n\n### 12.4 Independent Evaluation, Third-Party Audit, and What a Certificate Proves\n\nAssurance worth the name comes from someone who does not sell the system.\n\n**Independent evaluation** is testing or review by a party with no commercial stake in the result: your own red team, an accredited laboratory or a public evaluator. Independence means declared conflicts, a published method, and a result a third party could reproduce.\n\n**Third-party audit** reaches the vendor's evidence through a right you hold rather than a favour, and EU regulation makes the direction explicit. The Digital Operational Resilience Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554, requires financial entities to manage ICT third-party risk, names concentration risk explicitly, and sets a defined list of contractual provisions for ICT agreements. The NIS2 Directive, Directive (EU) 2022/2555, requires supply chain security measures between an entity and its suppliers.\n\n**Certification** is the third route. A certificate to a management system standard confirms that the organization has defined, operates and has been independently assessed against a management system within a stated scope. ISO/IEC 42001 is the AI management system standard, and on 17 October 2024 KPMG Australia announced it had been certified to it by BSI, the first organization globally in that position.\n\n- It does not prove that any particular model is accurate, fair or lawful, that any output is correct, or that the scope covers the product you are buying; nor does it bind a version, so the configuration you deploy may not be the one assessed.\n- It does not transfer your duties: deployer obligations under EU AI Act Article 26 and controller duties under the GDPR stay where they are, and an organization that repurposes a third party's general-purpose system into a high-risk use becomes the provider of that use under Article 25(1)(c), certified or not.\n\n### 12.5 Acceptance Testing Before Adoption\n\nThe instrument that converts an assessment into a decision is the acceptance test, agreed in writing before signature. The criteria state what the system must do, in numeric terms, for the use case. The data is a test set drawn from your population, ideally with a portion the vendor has not tuned against. The threshold is the pass mark, set before results are known. The remedy is what happens on failure: the right to reject, require remediation or terminate.\n\nBenchmarks belong at selection, not at acceptance. A leaderboard score measures capability on a public task, on data that may be contaminated by the training corpus, and does not predict performance on your inputs, so use it to shorten a longlist and test the survivors. The licence review belongs at acceptance too: a component you may not lawfully deploy fails for a reason no performance test surfaces.\n\nWhere a statute imposes an audit, the acceptance file has to carry it. New York City's Local Law 144 requires an annual independent bias audit of automated employment decision tools before an employer uses the tool. The New York State Comptroller's audit of the enforcement regime, published 2 December 2025, found enforcement ineffective: the responsible department received two complaints between July 2023 and June 2025, and a sweep of 32 companies' audits identified a single non-compliance issue. A statutory audit requirement is an obligation, not assurance, so the organization must still read the audit and act on it; weak enforcement does not reduce the duty.\n\n### 12.6 Monitoring That Survives Adoption\n\nAssurance decays. The contract may say the vendor will not change the model; the release notes will say otherwise eventually, so adoption must include the obligations that keep the assessment current.\n\nThe contractual ones are the six clauses Chapter 3 covers, notably model-change notification with notice to re-test and a re-testing right on material change.\n\nThree obligations are operational. Monitor the input distribution, because a shift in user inputs signals behavioural change. Re-run subgroup performance on live traffic, since the acceptance result is a snapshot of a moving population. Track override and reversal rates, because a step change there reports failure before any complaint does.\n\nOne obligation is structural. A material change to the model, the data or the use calls for re-acceptance: run the test again against the original criteria and record the result. FDA guidance on predetermined change control plans, current as of 18 August 2025, is the clearest template: a manufacturer states what may change, how it will be verified, and how safety will be monitored.\n\n### 12.7 Concentration, Dependency and Exit\n\nFour questions frame the dependency review: what happens if the vendor changes the model, withdraws it, fails, or is breached?\n\n**Concentration** is the systemic question. When many organizations in a sector depend on one provider, a single failure is a sector failure. DORA requires financial entities to manage ICT concentration risk and hold exit strategies. The Change Healthcare ransomware attack illustrates it: disclosed on 21 February 2024, it disrupted healthcare payment flows nationwide because so much of the claims pipeline ran through one provider. Count how many of your critical systems depend on the same supplier.\n\n**Change** is the performance question: a retrained or version-swapped model behaves differently, and the protection is the re-test clause in 12.6 plus a retained acceptance baseline.\n\n**Withdrawal** is the continuity question. A retired model or discontinued API becomes a migration project the deployer cannot schedule. Contract for notice, for continuity of a supported version, and for data portability. Where the vendor will not commit, keep the interface to the model narrow, so substituting a provider is a configuration change rather than a rebuild.\n\n**Failure** is the exit question. An exit plan names the alternative, the data return and deletion terms, and any escrow arrangement. An internally built model gives control of training data and direct answers to a regulator, but it also makes the organization the provider, with provider duties. Dependency is a choice between two accountabilities, not an escape from one.\n\n### 12.8 Evaluating Claims Honestly\n\nThe failure mode this chapter exists to prevent: a capability is overstated, the deployer builds its assessment on the claim, and the harm arrives through the gap. **FTC v. Evolv Technologies** is the case. The Commission alleged that the company overstated its AI weapons screening system's detection capability while schools buying it were locked into multi-year contracts, and the stipulated order of 26 November 2024 bars unsupported AI claims and allows affected K to 12 customers to cancel. The harm landed on the deployers, who had relied on the vendor's claims for the safety of children.\n\nTreat every claim as requiring substantiation rather than assuming it. Record the claim, the evidence behind it, who verified it and when, and the residual gap. A capability claim needs the test set, the conditions and the subgroup breakdown; a compliance claim needs the scope statement and the validity dates; a performance claim needs the measurement method, not the percentage. Anything unsubstantiated is a residual risk with a named owner.\n\nDo not repeat unverified claims in your own documents: an organization that adopts a vendor's overstated number and passes it on has made its own representation, and its exposure does not depend on the vendor's. The assessment phase ends with a signed record of what the system is, what was tested, what could not be verified, and who owns the gap.\n\nAssurance is a cycle of instruments rather than a certificate on a wall: what matters is what the organization knows about this system, how it came to know it, and what it will do when the answer changes.\n\n### Exam Focus\n\n- An attestation is a representation and its value depends on the maker's accountability, not on verification. Anything a vendor says about itself, including a completed questionnaire, is an attestation.\n- A benchmark is a public test suite for general capability, so it belongs at selection, never as the sole acceptance criterion.\n- A certificate to a management system standard is a point-in-time statement about an organization's management system within a scope. It does not certify a model, an output, a configuration or a version.\n- The distractor is \"the vendor is certified to ISO/IEC 42001, so the system is compliant\". Certification is issued by an accredited certification body, and the standard certifies the AI management system rather than the product.\n- A model card documents the model, so it is an input to a system-level assessment, never a substitute for one.\n- No regulator has yet enforced specifically against a deficient model card, so the absence of one is a diligence and documentation finding.\n- The DPIA falls on the controller under GDPR Article 35, the FRIA falls on specified deployers before first use under EU AI Act Article 27, and an ISO/IEC 42005 assessment is voluntary guidance no one can be certified to.\n- The demand on vendor testing is the method before the number: subgroup breakdown with the metric named, robustness and failure modes, model documentation, and evaluation methodology including whether the data was held out and by whom.\n- Fairness metrics disagree by construction, so \"the vendor tested for bias\" is incomplete until the metric and the subgroups are named.\n- Aggregate accuracy can be high while subgroup performance is materially worse, which is the specific reason subgroup testing is required.\n- DORA (Regulation (EU) 2022/2554) and NIS2 (Directive (EU) 2022/2555) make audit rights, monitoring, concentration management and exit terms an EU legal expectation, not a negotiating preference.\n- A 24-hour vendor incident-notification window is a negotiated term, not a legal rule.\n- The four dependency questions are change, withdrawal, failure and breach, and an internally built model substitutes provider duties for vendor dependency rather than removing accountability.\n- An overstated capability claim is the named failure mode, and the FTC's Evolv order shows the harm landing on the deployer that relied on the claim.\n- Acceptance testing needs criteria, population-drawn data, a pre-set threshold and a remedy on failure. A benchmark score is not acceptance.\n- Re-acceptance is required after a material model, data or use change, and the FDA predetermined change control plan is the template for pre-authorising change with verification and monitoring attached.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe following terms are used in this chapter and defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): AI Assurance, AI Impact Assessment, AI Management System (AIMS), Audit, Benchmarking, Bias Audit (NYC LL 144), Conformity Assessment, Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA), Model Card, Open Source Agreement, Proprietary Model Deployment Obligations, Red Teaming, Robustness, System Card, Third-Party Risk Management (TPRM), Vendor Agreement, Verification & Validation.\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [NYDFS report on the Apple Card investigation (23 March 2021)](https://www.dfs.ny.gov/reports_and_publications/press_releases/pr202103231): an assurance examination of a third-party underwriting model\n- [DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2554/oj): ICT third-party risk, concentration risk and exit strategies\n- [EIOPA overview of the Digital Operational Resilience Act](https://www.eiopa.europa.eu/digital-operational-resilience-act-dora_en): what DORA requires of entities and their ICT providers\n- [NIS2, Directive (EU) 2022/2555](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj): supply chain security duties with suppliers\n- [FTC v. Evolv Technologies (complaint and order, 26 November 2024)](https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2024/11/ftc-takes-action-against-evolv-technologies-deceiving-users-about-its-ai-powered-security-screening): overstated detection capability and the deployers who relied on it\n- [KPMG Australia certified to ISO/IEC 42001 by BSI (17 October 2024)](https://kpmg.com/au/en/media/media-releases/2024/10/kpmg-first-company-to-achieve-ai-management-system-standard-certification-by-bsi.html): the first certification to the AI management system standard\n- [New York State Comptroller, audit of Local Law 144 enforcement (2 December 2025)](https://www.osc.ny.gov/state-agencies/audits/2025/12/02/enforcement-local-law-144-automated-employment-decision-tools): the gap between a bias-audit duty and its enforcement\n- [U.S. Congressional Research Service, the Change Healthcare cyberattack (IN12330)](https://www.congress.gov/crs_external_products/IN/HTML/IN12330.web.html): concentration risk when a sector depends on one provider\n- [FDA, predetermined change control plans for machine learning enabled devices](https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/predetermined-change-control-plans-machine-learning-enabled-medical-devices-guiding-principles): the template for pre-authorising change with verification and monitoring\n- [Model Cards for Model Reporting (Mitchell et al., 2019)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993): the model card as a documentation artifact\n- [ISO/IEC 42001:2023 (AI management system)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/90574): the certifiable AI management system standard\n- [ISO/IEC 42005:2025 (AI system impact assessment)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/107659): the method behind the impact assessment ISO/IEC 42001 requires\n- [International Accreditation Forum](https://iaf.nu/): why the accreditation behind a certification body matters\n- [EU AI Act, Article 15 (accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/15/): the performance and robustness requirements behind vendor test evidence\n- [EU AI Act, Article 43 (conformity assessment)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/43/): how conformity is demonstrated for high-risk systems\n- [EU AI Act, Article 25 (responsibilities along the value chain)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/): when a deployer becomes the provider, certification or not\n- [EU AI Act, Article 26 (obligations of deployers)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/): the deployer duties that no certificate transfers\n- [EU AI Act, Article 27 (fundamental rights impact assessment)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/27/): who must perform a FRIA and when\n- [EU AI Act, Article 72 (post-market monitoring)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/72/): the monitoring obligation that survives market placement\n- [GDPR, Article 35 (data protection impact assessment)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-35-gdpr/): the DPIA trigger and required content\n- [NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI 100-1)](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf): Measure and Manage functions as the process behind recurring assurance\n- [NIST AI 600-1, generative AI profile](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf): evaluation, testing and third-party claims for generative systems\n\n## Chapter 13: Operating, Monitoring and Retiring\n\nA system that passed its release gate is not thereby governed. Governance of a live system is a set of habits that repeat: someone watches, something is written down, and when the ground moves the record is revisited. This chapter is competency IV.C, the largest single block of governance content in Domain IV, and it follows a system from go-live to switch-off.\n\nThe material tests distinctions distractors are built from: oversight on paper is not oversight, a log that cannot be trusted is not evidence, and a threshold nobody owns is decoration. A security incident, a performance incident and a harmful-output incident are not the same event, and classifying one wrongly decides who leads, which clock runs and who outside the organization must be told. Decommissioning is a governed process with risks of its own.\n\n- 13.1 Operating the System with Human Oversight\n- 13.2 What to Log, and Why Logs Are the Evidence Base\n- 13.3 Monitoring as a Designed Capability\n- 13.4 Detecting Degradation, Drift and Emergent Failure\n- 13.5 Incident Management for AI Systems\n- 13.6 Breach and Incident Notification Duties\n- 13.7 External Communication and Crisis Response\n- 13.8 Internal Audit, Assurance and Management Review\n- 13.9 Corrective Action and Algorithmic Disengagement\n- 13.10 Retirement, Decommissioning and the Governance of End of Life\n\n| Case | Citable as | Drawn on for |\n|---|---|---|\n| UK Post Office Horizon scandal | Post Office Horizon IT Inquiry, Volume 1 of the final report, 8 July 2025 | Automation bias, over-trust, years of undetected error |\n| Change Healthcare ransomware attack | U.S. Congressional Research Service report IN12330, disclosed 21 February 2024 | Security incident, supply concentration, HIPAA investigation |\n| Moffatt v. Air Canada | 2024 BCCRT 149, decided 14 February 2024 | A harmful-output incident, and accountability for what a system says |\n| FTC v. Rite Aid | FTC press release and consent order, 19 December 2023 | Oversight failure, corrective action, algorithmic disgorgement |\n| KPMG Australia ISO/IEC 42001 certification | KPMG Australia and BSI media release, 17 October 2024 | Third-party assurance of an AI management system |\n| NYC Local Law 144 enforcement audit | New York State Comptroller, audit 2024-N-6, 2 December 2025 | The gap between an audit duty and changed behaviour |\n| Zillow Offers wind-down | Zillow Group Q3 2021 shareholder letter, 2 November 2021 | The governed retirement of an AI-driven operation |\n| Tesla Autopilot recall and NHTSA recall query | NHTSA recall 23V-838, recall query RQ24009 | Corrective action, and a regulator testing the remedy |\n| Garante v. OpenAI | Garante decision of 20 December 2024, annulled by the Court of Rome in judgment 4153/2026 | An enforcement action reversed on appeal |\n\n### 13.1 Operating the System with Human Oversight\n\nHuman oversight separates a deployed system from an operated one. Chapter 3.1 set the three levels: human-in-the-loop approves each consequential decision, human-on-the-loop supervises and can intervene, human-in-command sets bounds and can halt.\n\nThe four conditions from Chapter 3 hold: competence, information (a reason and a confidence), authority (an override exercisable without penalty), and time and volume (a review load small enough for attention).\n\nTwo failure modes are examinable. Automation bias is where a person treats the output as authoritative and stops testing it: the UK Post Office Horizon scandal is the deep case. An accounting system produced apparent shortfalls the Post Office treated as reliable, and hundreds of sub-postmasters were wrongly accused of theft and fraud; the inquiry, across about 225 days of hearings and 298 witnesses, found staff and management had deferred to the system over the people it accused.\n\nThe second is review that supplies cover rather than scrutiny: in FTC v. Rite Aid, the FTC found a facial recognition system that falsely tagged shoppers as shoplifters.\n\n### 13.2 What to Log, and Why Logs Are the Evidence Base\n\nAlmost every duty in this chapter consumes logs: monitoring reads them, incident response reconstructs from them, and a person contesting a decision is owed them.\n\nA useful log set records the input, output, confidence, model version, retrieved documents, any human reviewer, timestamps and the calling identity. Provenance means every output traces to the model version and the data behind it. Integrity means logs are append-only and hash-protected so they cannot be quietly altered; the purpose is nonrepudiation.\n\nThe legal floor is thin but real: deployers of high-risk systems must keep automatically generated logs for at least six months (EU AI Act Article 26(6)), providers carry their own duty (Article 19), and GDPR Article 30 requires records of processing activities. Preserve the evidence before retraining, because retraining destroys the state needed to explain what happened. Never disable logging during an incident. Log content is personal data where it is: over-retention breaches storage limitation and early deletion destroys evidence.\n\n### 13.3 Monitoring as a Designed Capability\n\nMonitoring is designed before launch, not started afterwards. It must be specified: what is measured, against what baseline, at what threshold, and who acts; a metric with no owner and no action is a dashboard.\n\nBaselines are recorded before go-live: input and output distributions, accuracy and error rates. The most common defect is a global baseline; a model can hold overall accuracy while failing badly for one group, so baselines belong per segment where the population is not homogeneous. Thresholds turn a reading into an action: each monitored quantity gets a warning level, an action level and a named person who acts. Leading indicators move before harm arrives; a tripwire is a leading indicator where any single crossing is treated as an incident.\n\nPost-market monitoring is the legal form of this capability: EU AI Act Article 72 requires a documented plan, set before release, of what data is collected, how it is reviewed, and what thresholds trigger action. Monitoring reaches outside the system: the Change Healthcare ransomware attack was a concentration event, so dependencies belong in scope.\n\n### 13.4 Detecting Degradation, Drift and Emergent Failure\n\nDrift is movement in the world the model was fitted to. Covariate drift is a change in the distribution of inputs. Label drift is a change in the target. Concept drift is a change in the input-to-outcome relationship, the one that silently invalidates a score that still looks well calibrated. The operating task is to detect them and route a crossing to a named decision.\n\nDegradation is loss of performance against the stated criteria. It is often gradual: accuracy falls a point, latency creeps up, the override rate rises. No single reading alarms; the trend does. It is also often invisible: if outcomes are learned only for approved cases, the declined cases never return a label and the model's error there stays unmeasured.\n\nEmergent failure appears only in combination, at scale, or after a change elsewhere, and is invisible in the test set.\n\nDetection is not response. The Tesla Autopilot matter shows the corrective and regulatory side: Tesla filed a defect report covering more than two million vehicles whose controls it described as possibly insufficient to prevent driver misuse, remedied it by software update, and NHTSA opened a recall query to test the remedy.\n\n### 13.5 Incident Management for AI Systems\n\nAn incident is unexpected behaviour involving an AI system that causes, or could cause, harm, operational disruption or regulatory concern. The boundary against an issue is severity plus context: a misformatted date is an issue, while the same falsehood about a fee or entitlement is an incident because of who relied on it.\n\nClassification determines the leader, the evidence and the clock.\n\nA **security incident** concerns the confidentiality, integrity or availability of the system or its data. Change Healthcare is the worked case: ransomware on a critical claims-processing provider disrupted nationwide payments and drew a HIPAA investigation, a lesson in concentration.\n\nA **performance incident** is the system failing its own stated criteria with no adversary behind it: accuracy degrades, a model update changes behaviour, an integration breaks. Horizon is the unrecognised performance incident and the Tesla recall query the corrective one.\n\nA **harmful-output incident** is an output that harms a person though the system worked as designed. Moffatt v. Air Canada is the canonical case: a chatbot gave a customer wrong information about a bereavement fare, the customer relied on it, and the British Columbia Civil Resolution Tribunal held the airline liable for negligent misrepresentation, awarded about CAD 812, and rejected the argument that the chatbot was a separate legal entity responsible for its own statements. The organization answers for what its system says; Rite Aid is the same category at scale.\n\nRansomware becomes a performance incident when the system cannot run. The response order is contain, assess, investigate, remediate, notify, learn, with internal communication at triage and external notification on the legal clocks.\n\n### 13.6 Breach and Incident Notification Duties\n\nNotification is where an incident meets a deadline. The clock starts at awareness, not at confirmation, so an organization that waits to be sure may already be late; and the analysis of whether something must be notified is itself a record.\n\n| Obligation | Who owes it | To whom | Clock |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GDPR Article 33, personal data breach | The controller | The supervisory authority | 72 hours from awareness |\n| GDPR Article 34, high-risk personal data breach | The controller | Affected data subjects | Without undue delay |\n| EU AI Act Article 73, serious incident | Providers of high-risk AI systems | The market surveillance authority | Generally 15 days from awareness; 10 days for a death; sooner for widespread infringement or serious and irreversible disruption |\n| HIPAA Breach Notification Rule | Covered entities and business associates | Affected individuals, and the Secretary for large breaches | Without unreasonable delay and no later than 60 days from discovery |\n| NIS2 Article 23, significant incident | Essential and important entities | The competent authority or CSIRT | Early warning within 24 hours, incident notification within 72 hours, final report within one month |\n\nArticle 73 is a provider duty for high-risk systems, so a limited-risk chatbot giving a wrong answer to consumers is not an Article 73 case even when it is an incident. GDPR Article 33 is a controller duty attached to personal data, not to AI as such, so an AI incident with no personal data breach does not start the 72 hours. HIPAA reaches the healthcare pipeline; the Change Healthcare attack ran on both HIPAA and security lines. Clocks can run in parallel; the shortest is the working deadline, and the decision to notify belongs in the playbook with a named owner.\n\nIn December 2024 the Italian data protection authority announced a 15 million euro fine against OpenAI with a six-month public information campaign; the Court of Rome annulled that fine in judgment 4153/2026, decided in March 2026, on one-stop-shop jurisdiction grounds, without reaching the merits. The fine was never collected; the case illustrates an enforcement action reversed on appeal, not a standing penalty.\n\n### 13.7 External Communication and Crisis Response\n\nWhen an AI system has harmed people, how the organization speaks is part of the governance record. Ad hoc statements are how an organization contradicts itself; the plan is written before the incident.\n\nThe plan names people: an authorised voice, one spokesperson per incident type, a technical explainer, legal counsel, and a named approver for anything that leaves the building.\n\nWhat gets said follows a defensible template: what happened, what is known and not yet known, who is affected, what the organization is doing, and how to reach a person. A communication log records each statement, its time, its approver and the facts it rested on. Two temptations are fatal: speculating about the cause before the investigation supports it, and overclaiming.\n\nCrisis response is the harder judgement, how fast to speak against how much is known. Silence while a harm is visible reads as evasion; Horizon is the cautionary example, where the institution defended the system for years against the people it had accused. Separate what is known from what is under investigation, commit to a time for an update, and keep it.\n\n### 13.8 Internal Audit, Assurance and Management Review\n\nAssurance is the program of checking whether a system deserves trust and showing that work to others; an audit is one technique inside it. A first-party audit is internal, a second-party audit is a customer or partner auditing a supplier, and a third-party audit is by an independent external auditor.\n\nInternal audit is the organization's independent check on its own controls, and its value depends on independence and a reporting line that reaches the governing body. Its AI scope maps onto four questions: performance, model validation, red teaming and compliance.\n\nKPMG Australia became the first organisation globally certified to ISO/IEC 42001 by the certification body BSI, announced on 17 October 2024.\n\nManagement review is the internal counterpart and a scheduled duty. New York City's Local Law 144 requires a bias audit of automated employment decision tools, and the New York State Comptroller's audit of the city's enforcement, published on 2 December 2025, found enforcement ineffective: two complaints between July 2023 and June 2025, and a sweep of 32 companies that found a single non-compliance issue. A duty existed; it had changed little behaviour. Assurance tests whether controls work, not whether the paperwork exists.\n\n### 13.9 Corrective Action and Algorithmic Disengagement\n\nCorrective action is the repair phase: fix the problem, verify the fix, and update the record so the correction is traceable to the finding that caused it; a fix that is not recorded leaves the risk register asserting a state that is no longer true. Options scale with severity: retrain, roll back a version, add a control at the system layer, constrain the use case, or retire it. The EU AI Act gives high-risk providers a duty at the top of that scale, in Article 20, to withdraw, disable or recall a non-conforming system and inform the supply chain. The Tesla Autopilot recall is a corrective release a regulator then tested.\n\n**Algorithmic disgorgement** is the destruction of a model, and of the models and artifacts built from it, when the data behind it should never have been used. FTC v. Rite Aid is the enforcement example: alongside a five year ban on using facial recognition for security or surveillance, the consent order required deletion of the images and of the algorithms built from them. **Algorithmic disengagement** names the organizational counterpart: a model built on unlawfully obtained data may have to be taken out of service entirely. Enforcement on unlawfully collected biometric data, such as the Dutch data protection authority's decision on Clearview AI, turns on the absence of a lawful basis for building the database. Every model trained on the tainted data is then a candidate for removal, along with checkpoints, embeddings and downstream models.\n\n### 13.10 Retirement, Decommissioning and the Governance of End of Life\n\nA system ends, and the ending is governed. Decommissioning is a process with its own risks, records and failure modes, and the organization must plan its exit before it needs one.\n\nRetirement is triggered by regulatory change, drift-driven performance degradation, business reprioritisation, an unmitigable security or ethical finding, withdrawal of a third-party model, or localisation requirements. When a vendor deprecates a model the deployer faces a forced migration on the vendor's timetable, so the notice period and the re-evaluation right belong in the contract, and in the EU DORA and NIS2 make exit terms and concentration risk a legal expectation.\n\nThe deactivation sequence is repeatable; its order is examinable.\n\n1. Map stakeholders and their transition needs.\n2. Analyse usage and dependencies to find every downstream tool calling the system.\n3. Notify and educate users within the contractual and legal notice periods.\n4. Capture a final audit snapshot: inputs, weights, performance metrics and decision logs.\n5. Disable APIs, terminate model endpoints and update access controls.\n6. Monitor after deactivation for consequences the analysis missed.\n\nStep two is where retire-in-haste fails: a dependency analysis routinely finds tools and feeds still calling an endpoint nobody listed, and each needs a fallback before the endpoint is terminated. The operational risks are business continuity, data integrity, access revocation and residual shutdown fallout. The Zillow Offers wind-down retired a large AI-driven operation in public, cutting roughly 25 percent of its workforce after the valuation model failed to forecast home prices accurately at scale.\n\nPast applicants can contest decisions under ECOA and Regulation B or exercise their rights under the GDPR, so decision outputs may need to be kept for years. Archive final model versions, training-data lineage, performance logs, incident records and regulated decision outputs; destroy redundant training data, checkpoints and unneeded personal data.\n\nTwo obligations complete the end of life: notice to users, customers, partners and regulators within the contractual and legal periods, because abrupt retirement carries litigation risk; and a post-retirement review of the root cause, the impact on users, and the knowledge the next team needs.\n\nThe deactivation capability must exist before it is needed. For high-risk systems the EU AI Act sets the floor: Article 14(4)(e) requires a stop function that halts the system in a safe state, and Article 26(5) requires a deployer aware of a risk to suspend use and inform the provider and the relevant authority. NIST AI RMF MANAGE 2.4 asks for mechanisms to supersede, disengage or deactivate a system. A deactivation policy states who may trigger a stop and what a safe state is.\n\n### Exam Focus\n\nThis chapter maps to competency IV.C, the largest block in Domain IV.\n\n- Oversight fails in two ways: automation bias, where the human defers to the system, and review that supplies cover rather than scrutiny. Horizon is the case for the first and Rite Aid for the second.\n- The four conditions for real oversight (competence, information, authority, time and volume) decay silently after launch, so they need a periodic check, not a one-time design.\n- Logs are the evidence base for monitoring, incident response, audit and notification; the two properties that matter are provenance and integrity, with nonrepudiation the purpose of append-only, hash-protected storage.\n- The log floors: EU AI Act Article 26(6) at least six months for deployers of high-risk systems, Article 19 for providers, GDPR Article 30 records of processing. Preserve evidence before retraining and never disable logging during an incident.\n- Baselines come before launch and belong per segment, because a stable aggregate can hide a failing subgroup. A threshold with no owner and no action is a dashboard, not monitoring.\n- Tripwires are leading indicators where any single crossing is treated as an incident.\n- Drift has three types: covariate (inputs move), label (the target moves), concept (the input to outcome relationship moves). Degradation is usually gradual and often invisible because declined cases never return a label.\n- Emergent failure appears only at scale, in combination, or after a change elsewhere, and detection is not the same as response: a drift alert must route to a named decision.\n- Classify an incident before responding. A security incident turns on confidentiality, integrity or availability (Change Healthcare). A performance incident is the system failing its own criteria with no adversary (Horizon, Tesla). A harmful-output incident harms a person though the system worked as designed (Moffatt, Rite Aid). The class picks the leader, the evidence and the clock.\n- Ordering rules: preserve evidence before retraining, never disable logging, internal communication starts at triage, and external notification follows the legal clocks.\n- The notification clocks: GDPR Article 33 in 72 hours to the authority, GDPR Article 34 to data subjects without undue delay, EU AI Act Article 73 for high-risk providers at generally 15 days, 10 days for a death, sooner for widespread infringement, HIPAA no later than 60 days, NIS2 at 24 hours early warning, 72 hours notification, one month final report.\n- Article 73 is a provider duty for high-risk systems, so a limited-risk chatbot's wrong answer to consumers is not an Article 73 case. GDPR Article 33 attaches to personal data, not to AI.\n- The clock generally starts at awareness, not at confirmation, and the analysis of whether notification is owed is itself a record, whether or not it was owed.\n- Garante v. OpenAI is an enforcement action reversed on appeal: the Court of Rome annulled the 15 million euro fine in judgment 4153/2026 on one-stop-shop jurisdiction grounds and it was never collected. Do not treat it as a standing penalty.\n- Assurance is the program and an audit is one technique; audits are first party (internal), second party (a customer or partner auditing a supplier) or third party (independent). ISO/IEC 42001 certification, as with KPMG Australia's in October 2024, is a third-party assessment of the management system, not of a single model.\n- The NYC Local Law 144 enforcement audit shows a duty on paper that changed little behaviour, so assurance tests whether controls work rather than whether documents exist.\n- Corrective action is fix, verify, and update the record; EU AI Act Article 20 gives high-risk providers a duty to withdraw, disable or recall a non-conforming system.\n- Algorithmic disgorgement destroys a model and its derivatives when the data behind it should not have been used (FTC v. Rite Aid deleted the images and the algorithms built from them). Algorithmic disengagement is the organizational version, and it depends on forward lineage tracing.\n- Decommissioning is a governed process with its own risks (business continuity, data integrity, access revocation, residual consequences), the controlled sequence captures a final audit snapshot before endpoints are disabled, and dependencies are analysed before termination.\n- An exit must be planned before it is needed: vendor model deprecation is a forced migration on the vendor's timetable, so notice and re-evaluation rights belong in the contract, with DORA and NIS2 the EU baseline for exit terms.\n- The deactivation floor: EU AI Act Article 14(4)(e) stop function to a safe state, Article 26(5) suspend and inform, NIST AI RMF MANAGE 2.4 disengage. A safe state and a drilled fallback are the parts most often missing.\n\n### Key Terms\n\nThe following terms are used in this chapter and defined in the Glossary (Appendix B): AI Assurance, Audit, Automation Bias, Corrective Action, Deactivation Policy, Decommissioning, Drift Detection, External Communication Plan, Incident Management, Issue Management, Localization Policy, MITRE ATLAS, Nonrepudiation, Operation & Monitoring, Post-Market Monitoring, Probability Severity Matrix, Public Disclosure, Record Keeping, Resilience, Risk, STRIDE, Tripwire.\n\n### Further Reading\n\n- [EU AI Act, Article 26 (obligations of deployers)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/): Article 26(6) log retention and Article 26(5) suspend and inform\n- [EU AI Act, Article 73 (reporting of serious incidents)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/73/): who reports, what counts as serious, and the timelines\n- [EU AI Act, Article 72 (post-market monitoring)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/72/): the monitoring plan that supplies incident detection\n- [EU AI Act, Article 20 (corrective actions and duty of information)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/20/): withdrawal, disabling and recall for non-conforming systems\n- [EU AI Act, Article 14 (human oversight)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/): the stop function that halts a high-risk system in a safe state\n- [EU AI Act, Article 19 (automatically generated logs)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/19/): the provider's log-keeping duty that pairs with Article 26(6)\n- [EU AI Act, Article 11 (technical documentation)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/11/): the provider documentation duties and the Annex IV contents\n- [GDPR, Article 33 (notification of a personal data breach)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-33-gdpr/): the 72-hour supervisory authority clock\n- [GDPR, Article 34 (communication of a breach to the data subject)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-34-gdpr/): when affected individuals must be told directly\n- [GDPR, Article 30 (records of processing activities)](https://gdpr-info.eu/art-30-gdpr/): the controller's record-keeping duty behind automated decision logging\n- [HIPAA Breach Notification Rule](https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/breach-notification/index.html): individual and Secretary notice, no later than 60 days from discovery\n- [NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf): the MANAGE function, including MANAGE 2.4 on disengaging a system\n- [NIST AI RMF Playbook](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/): the subcategories on monitoring, incident response and management review\n- [ISO/IEC 42001:2023 (AI management system)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/90574): documented information, logging, incident reporting and management review\n- [ISO/IEC 42006:2025 (requirements for AIMS audit and certification bodies)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/108460): who may certify an ISO/IEC 42001 management system\n- [ISO/IEC 23894:2023 (AI risk management)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/82914): the AI extension of ISO 31000 and the dynamic principle\n- [OECD AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor](https://oecd.ai/en/incidents): real-world incident evidence for monitoring and forecasting\n- [Post Office Horizon IT Inquiry, Volume 1 of the final report (8 July 2025)](https://www.postofficehorizoninquiry.org.uk/news/chair-calls-swift-action-deliver-full-and-fair-compensation-urgent-report): automation bias, over-trust and years of undetected error\n- [Change Healthcare cyberattack and response, CRS report IN12330](https://www.congress.gov/crs_external_products/IN/HTML/IN12330.web.html): a security incident, supply concentration and a HIPAA investigation\n- [Moffatt v. Air Canada, 2024 BCCRT 149](https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bccrt/doc/2024/2024bccrt149/2024bccrt149.html): a harmful-output incident and accountability for what a system says\n- [FTC v. Rite Aid](https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2023/12/rite-aid-banned-using-ai-facial-recognition-after-ftc-says-retailer-deployed-technology-without): oversight failure, use ban and algorithmic disgorgement\n- [KPMG Australia ISO/IEC 42001 certification (17 October 2024)](https://kpmg.com/au/en/media/media-releases/2024/10/kpmg-first-company-to-achieve-ai-management-system-standard-certification-by-bsi.html): third-party assurance of an AI management system\n- [NYC Local Law 144 enforcement audit (2 December 2025)](https://www.osc.ny.gov/state-agencies/audits/2025/12/02/enforcement-local-law-144-automated-employment-decision-tools): the gap between an audit duty and changed behaviour\n- [Zillow Group Q3 2021 shareholder letter](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1617640/000161764021000085/exhibit993.htm): a controlled retirement of an AI-driven operation\n- [NHTSA Part 573 recall report 23V-838](https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/rcl/2023/RCLRPT-23V838-8276.PDF): a corrective release for an automated driving feature, later tested by a recall query\n- [Garante v. OpenAI, decision of 20 December 2024](https://www.garanteprivacy.it/home/docweb/-/docweb-display/docweb/10085432): the fine and the six-month information campaign\n- [Court of Rome annuls the Garante's OpenAI fine, judgment 4153/2026](https://www.dpoworkspace.eu/en/news/tribunale-roma-annulla-sanzione-openai-15-milioni-sportello-unico): the annulment on one-stop-shop jurisdiction grounds\n- [DORA (Regulation (EU) 2022/2554)](https://www.eiopa.europa.eu/digital-operational-resilience-act-dora_en): ICT third-party risk, concentration and exit terms for financial entities\n- [NIS2 (Directive (EU) 2022/2555)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj): supply-chain security and the Article 23 incident reporting clocks\n- [FDA predetermined change control plans for machine learning enabled devices](https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/predetermined-change-control-plans-machine-learning-enabled-medical-devices-guiding-principles): the model for pre-authorised model change\n- [Clearview AI, Dutch DPA decision (3 September 2024)](https://www.autoriteitpersoonsgegevens.nl/en/documents/decision-fine-clearview-ai): unlawfully collected data as the premise failure behind disengagement\n\n# Where the Four Domains Meet\n\nThe four domains are not four subjects. They are four views of one chain of decisions, and the exam tests the chain.\n\nDomain I installs the vocabulary and the discipline: what an AI system is, what can go wrong, which characteristic of AI makes that failure possible, who owns the decision, and where it is written down. Domain II attaches legal duties to those decisions, and the duties divide by role, so an organization can be a developer, a provider, a deployer and a controller at the same time and owe a different thing under each. Domains III and IV run the chain, first through the build and then through adoption and operation, and each link produces a record that the next link depends on.\n\nThree questions recur in every domain, and they are the plain-language form of what the BOK asks:\n\n- **Who owns this decision?** Accountability is a named human, it is never delegable to a model or a vendor, and the person who runs governance is not the person who owns the systems.\n- **What could go wrong, and for whom?** The sort decides the control and the owner. A harm that is not assigned to a category tends not to get one.\n- **Where is it written down?** Governance that stops at approval ends up describing a system that no longer exists, because AI systems change underneath the documents written about them.\n\nMost scenario items are one link of that chain, and the most common trap is a distractor that sounds prudent but belongs to another link. A contract term, an incident response and a monitoring threshold are all reasonable answers to \"what should the organization do,\" and only one of them is the current link. Read the stem for where in the chain the organization actually stands.\n\nTwo habits make the domains cohere. Learn the **pairings**, because most applied questions are a pairing with half missing: a characteristic and its control, a legal role and its duty, a risk and its owner, a documentation artifact and the decision it evidences. And keep **status** in view, because the EU AI Act in particular now exists in two versions, and the difference between adopted, in force and applies from is itself testable.\n\n## Preparing for the exam\n\nThe book covers the material in order. The rest of the app is built for the work around it, and the two are meant to be used together.\n\n1. **Read this book straight through once.** The material accumulates, and later chapters assume the earlier vocabulary.\n2. **Work the Mind Map.** Open a section, read the node's notes in the drawer, then open its children to go deeper. Each node has buttons that filter the Quiz and the Flashcards to that node, and every question and card is tagged down to a key term, so completing all of a node's leaves turns it green by definition. The goal is a fully green map.\n3. **Drill the weak nodes.** Nodes colour by performance: grey untested, then red, amber and green. Re-drill whatever is still red before moving on.\n4. **Sit timed exams.** The Test tab offers a full exam of 100 questions in three hours and a practice quiz of 25 in one hour, drawn to the blueprint's domain weights, with multi-select questions scored all-or-nothing as on the real exam. Answers are revealed only on submission.\n\nConsistent scores above 85% under exam conditions are a reasonable signal that you are ready.\n\nOne last caution. The exam is written against the BOK, and this book follows BOK v2.1 with the law as it stands in September 2026. Both move. When a fact in this book turns on a date, a status or a question range, check it against the current Body of Knowledge before you rely on it.\n\n# Appendix A: Standards and Frameworks at a Glance\n\nThe chapters teach each of the instruments below in context, showing how they fit together and where each one bites. This appendix does a narrower job. It sets them side by side so that you can check quickly who issued each one, whether it binds anyone, whether an organization can be certified against it, and which question it exists to answer. The last column sends you to the section that holds the detail, and the detail is what settles a hard question.\n\nThe rows follow the order in which the book teaches the instruments.\n\n| Instrument | Issuer and date | Binding or voluntary | Certifiable | The question it answers | Where taught |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| GDPR | European Union, Regulation (EU) 2016/679 | Binding EU law | Not applicable (law) | When is processing personal data lawful, including for training AI and for automated decisions? | Chapter 4 |\n| GPAI Code of Practice | Facilitated by the European Commission under EU AI Act Article 56. Published 10 July 2025, adequacy confirmed 1 August 2025 | Voluntary (adherence is evidence of compliance, with no presumption of conformity) | No (providers sign it, in full or by chapter) | How can a general-purpose AI model provider demonstrate compliance with Articles 53 and 55? | Chapter 6 (6.3) |\n| OECD AI Principles | OECD Recommendation on AI (OECD/LEGAL/0449). Adopted 22 May 2019, definition revised 8 November 2023, Recommendation revised 3 May 2024 | Voluntary (a political commitment by 47 adherents as of September 2026) | No | What does trustworthy AI look like, and what counts as an AI system? | Chapter 7 (7.1) |\n| NIST AI RMF | NIST, AI RMF 1.0 (NIST AI 100-1), January 2023 | Voluntary | No (a framework, with no certification) | What must an AI risk management process contain? | Chapter 7 (7.2) |\n| NIST AI RMF Playbook | NIST, issued with AI RMF 1.0, with a revision to follow the 1.0 update | Voluntary (suggested actions) | No | What should an organization do about each of the RMF's 72 subcategories? | Chapter 7 (7.3) |\n| NIST AI 600-1 | NIST, Generative AI Profile, July 2024, under Executive Order 14110 | Voluntary (recognized in the defences of Texas's TRAIGA) | No | Which risks does generative AI create or worsen, and how does the RMF apply to them? | Chapter 7 (7.4) |\n| ISO/IEC 22989 | ISO and IEC, 2022 | Voluntary | No | What do the core AI terms mean, from AI system and trustworthiness to the five roles (provider, manufacturer, customer, partner, subject)? | Chapter 7 (7.5) |\n| ISO/IEC 42001 | ISO and IEC, December 2023 (first edition, current as of September 2026) | Voluntary | Yes | How does an organization run an AI management system that an auditor can certify? | Chapter 7 (7.6) |\n| ISO/IEC 42006 | ISO and IEC, 2025 | Voluntary | No (it governs the bodies that certify to 42001) | What must a body meet, beyond ISO/IEC 17021-1, to audit and certify against 42001? | Chapter 7 (7.6) |\n| ISO/IEC 42005 | ISO and IEC, 28 May 2025 (first edition) | Voluntary guidance | No | How and when should an AI system's impacts on individuals, groups and societies be assessed and recorded? | Chapter 7 (7.7), applied in 8.4 and 12.1 |\n| ISO/IEC 23894 | ISO and IEC, 2023 | Voluntary guidance | No | How should AI risk to the organization be managed, building on ISO 31000? | Chapter 7 (7.8) |\n\nRead down the third and fourth columns and a pattern appears. The GDPR is the only law in the table. The GPAI Code of Practice sits beside a binding law, the EU AI Act that Chapter 6 teaches in full, and adhering to it gives a provider evidence of compliance but no presumption of conformity, which Articles 53(4) and 55(2) reserve for harmonised standards. Everything else is voluntary, though a voluntary instrument can still carry legal weight, as the TRAIGA defences show for NIST AI 600-1. Only ISO/IEC 42001 can be certified against. Several entries also carry a status that can move. The EDPB's 2026 guidelines on anonymization and web scraping are drafts, open for consultation to 30 October 2026, and the Commission's Digital Omnibus amendments to the GDPR are proposals, so apply the GDPR as it stands.\n\nFour distinctions cause most of the confusion. ISO/IEC 42001 requires an AI system impact assessment (clause 6.1.4 defines the process, clause 8.4 has it performed, and Annex A objective A.5 adds controls), while ISO/IEC 42005 explains how to carry one out. 42005 asks about impacts on people, and ISO/IEC 23894 asks about risk to the organization, with 42005's Annex B showing how impact findings feed a 23894-style risk process. ISO/IEC 42006 regulates the certifiers and never the certified organization. Within the NIST family, a question about practical, per-subcategory guidance belongs to the Playbook, a question about the risk process itself belongs to the AI RMF, and a question about generative AI specifically belongs to AI 600-1. Keep one list apart from all of these: BOK v2.1 names exactly three core ISO standards (22989, 42001 and 42005), and 23894 is useful background outside that closed set.\n\n## Sources\n\nThe official texts and pages behind each row are listed below, grouped by instrument.\n\n**GDPR**\n\n- [GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj)\n- [EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/opinion-of-the-board-art-64/opinion-282024-on-certain-data-protection-aspects-related-to_en)\n- [EDPB press release on draft Guidelines 02/2026 (anonymization) and 03/2026 (web scraping)](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/news/edpb-sheds-light-on-anonymisation-and-web-scraping-for-generative-ai-and-adopts-final-version_en): both guidelines remain drafts, open for consultation to 30 October 2026\n- [CJEU SCHUFA, C-634/21](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/C/2024/913/oj/eng)\n- [CJEU Dun & Bradstreet Austria, C-203/22](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=celex:62022CJ0203)\n\n**GPAI Code of Practice and the EU AI Act**\n\n- [GPAI Code of Practice (European Commission)](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/contents-code-gpai)\n- [Code of Practice on Marking and Labelling (transparency of AI-generated content)](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/code-practice-ai-generated-content)\n- [Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (EU AI Act), Article 56](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj)\n\n**OECD AI Principles**\n\n- [OECD Recommendation on AI (OECD/LEGAL/0449)](https://legalinstruments.oecd.org/en/instruments/OECD-LEGAL-0449)\n- [OECD.AI: AI principles](https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles)\n- [OECD.AI: classification framework](https://oecd.ai/en/classification)\n- [OECD.AI: incidents and hazards](https://oecd.ai/en/incidents)\n\n**NIST AI RMF**\n\n- [NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI 100-1)](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf)\n- [NIST AI RMF landing page](https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework)\n\n**NIST AI RMF Playbook**\n\n- [NIST AI RMF Playbook](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/)\n- [NIST AI RMF Playbook (JSON, public domain)](https://airc.nist.gov/docs/playbook.json)\n\n**NIST AI 600-1**\n\n- [NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf)\n\n**ISO/IEC 22989**\n\n- [ISO/IEC 22989 (IEC webstore, free)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839)\n- [ISO/IEC 22989 full text (ISO OBP)](https://www.iso.org/obp/ui/en/#!iso:std:74296:en)\n\n**ISO/IEC 42001**\n\n- [ISO/IEC 42001 (IEC webstore)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/90574)\n\n**ISO/IEC 42006**\n\n- [ISO/IEC 42006 (IEC webstore)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/108460)\n\n**ISO/IEC 42005**\n\n- [ISO/IEC 42005 (IEC webstore)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/107659)\n- [BSI press release on ISO/IEC 42005](https://www.bsigroup.com/en-GB/insights-and-media/media-centre/press-releases/2025/june/framework-published-to-assess-real-world-ethical-and-operational-impact-of-ai/)\n\n**ISO/IEC 23894**\n\n- [ISO/IEC 23894 (IEC webstore)](https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/82914)\n\n# Appendix B: Glossary\n\nEvery key term that appears in the chapters is defined here, in alphabetical order. Where a definition follows a particular source, such as ISO/IEC 22989, the EU AI Act or IAPP's key terms list, the entry says so.\n\n### A\n\n**A/B Testing.** A comparative testing method where two versions of an AI model run simultaneously to evaluate which performs better. Commonly used for deployment validation. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Accountability.** The state of being accountable (responsible for actions, decisions, and performance). Refers to an assigned responsibility based on regulations, agreements, or delegation. A person or entity is accountable to another using specific means and criteria. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Accountable and Transparent.** NIST trustworthiness characteristic: information about AI system design decisions and outputs is available to stakeholders, with clear ownership of responsibility. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Action Space (Agentic AI).** The range of actions an agent can actually take, fixed by the tools it can call and the permissions on those tools (sandbox-only, internal or external systems; read or write). The IMDA framework contrasts it with autonomy, which is how freely the agent decides what to do. A wider action space raises potential impact. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Activation Function.** A function applied to the weighted combination of all inputs to a neuron. Typically nonlinear. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Adoption.** Needs analysis, feasibility study, stakeholder engagement, requirements definition *(Chapter 2)*\n\n**Adversarial Example.** An intentionally crafted input designed to cause an AI model to make incorrect predictions. Often imperceptible to humans but catastrophic for model behavior. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Adverse Action Notice (ECOA/Reg B).** The notice a creditor must give after taking adverse action on an application, generally within 30 days of a completed application, including a statement of the specific principal reasons (12 CFR 1002.9). Saying the applicant failed to achieve a qualifying score is insufficient, so AI credit models must support accurate reasons. The rule still applies after the CFPB withdrew its AI adverse action circulars on 12 May 2025. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Agent Goal Hijack.** Manipulation that redirects an agent's objectives, task choices or plan, typically through indirect prompt injection hidden in content it reads (web pages, emails, documents, tool outputs, other agents' messages). OWASP ASI01. Distinct from memory poisoning (persistent corruption of stored context) and rogue agents (drift without an active attacker). *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Agentic AI.** AI systems made up of one or more agents that pursue a goal over several steps by planning, deciding and acting through tools, with some independence from human direction. Today's agents usually pair a language model with instructions, memory, tools and controls; governance shifts from managing outputs to managing actions. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Agent Kill Switch.** An emergency control that immediately stops an agent, or disables a specific tool, prompt or connection, and revokes its credentials when it malfunctions or is compromised. The operational core of a deactivation policy (BOK IV.C); pair it with a fallback process and preserved, tamper-proof logs. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**AI Act Milestone: August 2, 2026.** The date Article 50 transparency duties and the Commission's enforcement of general-purpose AI obligations begin. As originally enacted, it was also the date Annex III high-risk requirements took effect; as amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus, those moved to 2 December 2027 (Annex III) and 2 August 2028 (Annex I). *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**AI Act Milestone: August 2, 2028.** As amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus, the date high-risk requirements start for AI that is a safety component of, or itself, a product under Annex I harmonisation law (Article 6(1)), such as medical devices. As originally enacted, this was 2 August 2027. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**AI Act Milestone: December 2, 2027.** As amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus, the date the AI Act's high-risk requirements (Chapter III, Sections 1 to 3) start for Annex III systems such as recruitment, credit-scoring and education tools, including deployer duties and FRIAs. As originally enacted, this was 2 August 2026. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**AI Act Milestone: February 2, 2025.** The date prohibited AI practices and AI literacy requirements began to apply. The first substantive obligations under the EU AI Act. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**AI Actors (NIST AI RMF).** The OECD definition adopted by AI RMF 1.0: 'those who play an active role in the AI system lifecycle, including organizations and individuals that deploy or operate AI'. Appendix A groups their tasks (design, development, deployment, operation and monitoring, TEVV, human factors, domain experts, impact assessment, procurement, governance and oversight) and adds third parties, end users, affected communities, other AI actors and the general public. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**AI Agent.** Automated entity that perceives and reacts to its environment and takes actions to achieve its goals. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**AI Assurance.** The combined work of checking, and showing to others, whether an AI system deserves trust, using techniques such as impact assessments, bias and compliance audits, conformity assessment, performance testing, formal verification and certification. An audit or a red-team test is one assurance technique; assurance is the overall program that builds justified confidence for regulators, customers and the board. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**AI Business Operator (Korea).** The Korean Act's umbrella term for any corporation, organization, individual or government body in the AI business. There are two types: AI developers, which develop and provide AI, and AI-using business operators, which offer products or services built on a developer's AI. Both carry the Act's transparency and high-impact duties. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**AI Component.** Functional element that constitutes an AI system. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**AI Customer.** Entity that acquires/uses the AI system *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**AI Data Retention Policy.** Policy defining how long training data is retained after model development, and addressing that data may effectively persist inside the trained model itself. A common gap when updating privacy policies for AI. *(Chapter 3)*\n\n**AI Deployer.** The entity that operationalizes AI models in production. Responsible for integration, monitoring for risk, and ensuring reliable real-world function. *(Chapter 2)*\n\n**AI Developer.** The entity that builds and trains AI models. Responsible for curating datasets, running fairness evaluations, and ensuring ethical design and testing. *(Chapter 2)*\n\n**AI Distributor.** EU AI Act role: entity in the supply chain that makes an AI system available on the EU market without affecting its compliance. Must verify CE marking and documentation. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**AI Governance Stakeholder.** Any individual or group with a role in AI governance. Includes executive leadership, technical teams, legal/compliance, product owners, and end users. *(Chapter 2)*\n\n**AI Impact Assessment.** A structured analysis of how an AI system affects individuals, groups, organizations, and society. Covers privacy, bias, safety, fairness, and fundamental rights. *(Chapter 12)*\n\n**AI Importer.** EU AI Act role: entity that places an AI system from a third country on the EU market. Must verify conformity and ensure CE marking before distribution. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**AI Incident vs. AI Hazard (OECD Definitions).** OECD terms (AI Papers No. 16, May 2024). An AI incident is an event where an AI system's development, use or malfunction directly or indirectly leads to harm to health, critical infrastructure, rights, or property, communities or the environment. An AI hazard is an event that could plausibly lead to an incident; near misses count as hazards. Serious incidents, AI disasters and serious hazards are narrower tiers. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**AI Liability Directive (Withdrawn).** A Commission proposal of 28 Sep 2022 (COM(2022) 496) to ease fault-based claims for AI harm through court-ordered disclosure on high-risk AI systems and a rebuttable presumption of causality. Withdrawal was announced in the February 2025 work programme and published in OJ C/2025/5423 on 6 Oct 2025. The revised Product Liability Directive and national tort law fill the gap. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**AI Management System (AIMS).** A set of interrelated organizational elements to establish AI policies, objectives, and processes to achieve those objectives. The core of ISO/IEC 42001. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**AI Manufacturer.** Entity that builds/assembles the AI system *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**AI Partner.** Entity that collaborates in AI development or deployment *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**AI Provider.** Entity that makes an AI system available *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**AI Risk Sources (ISO 23894 Annex B).** Informative annex of ISO/IEC 23894 cataloging AI-specific risk sources; Annex A lists common AI-related objectives and Annex C maps risk management processes to the AI system life cycle. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**AI RMF Subcategory.** A numbered outcome statement under a category of the AI RMF Core, e.g. MEASURE 2.11: fairness and bias are evaluated and results documented. AI RMF 1.0 has 72 across 19 categories, and the Playbook gives one entry of suggested actions for each. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**AI Subject.** Individual affected by or interacting with an AI system *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**AI System.** A technical system that generates results such as content, forecasts, recommendations, or decisions for a specific, human-defined objective. May use various techniques and approaches related to AI to develop a model for representing data, knowledge, processes, etc. Designed to operate with varying degrees of automation. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**AI System Impact Assessment (ISO/IEC 42005).** A formal, documented process for identifying, evaluating and addressing the benefits and harms an AI system and its foreseeable uses can have for individuals, groups and society. ISO/IEC 42001 requires one (clauses 6.1.4 and 8.4); ISO/IEC 42005 explains how to do it. Distinct from the organizational risk assessment of ISO/IEC 23894. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**AI User.** The person or entity that interacts with AI in real-world scenarios. Often closest to operational impact and first to detect unintended outcomes. *(Chapter 2)*\n\n**AI Vendor Due Diligence.** Evaluation of AI vendors covering AI-specific risks: model and training-data provenance, labeling quality, explainability, safety filters, and vulnerable dependencies. Imposed by procurement policy before contracting. *(Chapter 3)*\n\n**Algorithmic Bias.** Systematic and repeatable errors in an AI system that create unfair outcomes, such as privileging one group over another, often stemming from biased training data. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Annex SL.** ISO's harmonized structure for management system standards. Provides identical clause structure (4-10) across ISO 42001, 27001, 9001 enabling integration. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Anonymization.** The process of irreversibly transforming personal data so the data subject can no longer be identified. If fully achieved, GDPR no longer applies. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Article 22 GDPR.** The GDPR provision granting data subjects the right not to be subject to solely automated decisions producing legal or similarly significant effects. Requires human intervention capability. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Article 35 GDPR.** The GDPR provision mandating Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs) for processing likely to result in high risk to individuals. Frequently triggered by AI systems. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Article 56 (EU AI Act).** Legal basis for the GPAI Code of Practice, published by the European AI Office. Adherence is a compliance-demonstration aid, NOT conclusive evidence of compliance or a safe harbor. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Article 9 GDPR.** The GDPR provision prohibiting processing of special category data (race, religion, health, biometrics, etc.) unless a specific exception applies. Critical for AI training data. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Artificial General Intelligence (AGI).** A theoretical form of AI capable of understanding, learning, and applying intelligence across any task a human can perform. Does not yet exist publicly. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Artificial Intelligence (AI).** *\\<Discipline>* Research and development of mechanisms and applications of AI systems. R&D can take place in fields such as computer science, data science, humanities, mathematics, and natural sciences. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Artificial Superintelligence (ASI).** A theoretical AI that surpasses human intelligence in all domains: creativity, problem-solving, emotional understanding, and decision-making. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Audit.** A systematic, independent examination of an AI system's design, data, outputs, and governance against established criteria. Can be internal (first/second party) or external (third party). *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Automated Decision-Making (ADM).** Decisions made by a system rather than a person, with no meaningful human role in reaching the outcome, such as software approving or refusing a loan from a score. GDPR Art. 22 gives people the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing, including profiling, that has legal or similarly significant effects, unless it is necessary for a contract, authorised by law, or based on explicit consent. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Automatic Summarization.** Task of shortening a portion of natural language content or text while preserving important semantic information. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Automation.** Relating to a process or system that operates under certain conditions without human intervention. *(Chapter 3)*\n\n**Autonomy.** A characteristic of a system that is capable of changing its intended domain of use or its objective without external intervention, control, or supervision. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Availability.** The property of being accessible and usable when an authorized entity has a need. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n### B\n\n**Bayesian Network.** Probabilistic model that uses Bayesian inference for probability calculations using a directed acyclic graph. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Benchmarking.** Evaluating AI system performance against established standards, reference models, or industry baselines to assess relative quality and identify improvement areas. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Best-Efforts Impact Assessment (Korea).** Art. 35 of Korea's AI Basic Act: operators providing high-impact AI must make efforts to assess its impact on fundamental rights in advance, but the assessment itself is not mandatory. Skipping it isn't fined, but public bodies must give priority to assessed products. The decree lists what the assessment covers. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Bias Audit (NYC LL 144).** The impartial evaluation by an independent auditor that NYC Local Law 144 requires no more than one year before an AEDT is used. It calculates selection or scoring rates and impact ratios by sex, race/ethnicity and intersectional categories. The impact ratio is a category's rate divided by the rate of the highest category. Results must be published, but the law sets no pass/fail threshold. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Bias (Systematic Deviation).** Systematic differential treatment of certain objects, persons, or groups compared to others. Treatment includes perception, observation, representation, prediction, or decision. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Bias Testing.** Evaluating AI system outputs for unfair differences across demographic groups, sensitive attributes, or protected characteristics to detect and mitigate algorithmic bias. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Biometric Data.** Personal data resulting from specific technical processing relating to physical, physiological, or behavioral characteristics allowing unique identification. Special category under GDPR. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Breach Notification.** GDPR requirement to notify the supervisory authority within 72 hours of becoming aware of a personal data breach, and to communicate high-risk breaches to affected data subjects. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Brittleness.** A model's tendency to fail catastrophically when encountering inputs slightly outside its training distribution. Indicates lack of robustness and generalization. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Business Context.** The organizational environment, objectives, constraints, and stakeholder expectations within which an AI system is designed, developed, and deployed. *(Chapter 8)*\n\n**Business Objectives.** The organizational goals that an AI system is intended to serve. Deployment decisions must start with objectives rather than model capability alone. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n### C\n\n**California AI Transparency Act (SB 942).** California law requiring covered providers (generative AI systems with over 1 million monthly users that are publicly accessible in California) to offer a free AI detection tool, embed latent provenance disclosures in AI-generated image, video and audio, and give users the option of a visible label. As amended by AB 853 it became operative on 2 Aug 2026, adding platform duties in 2027 and capture-device duties in 2028. Penalties are $5,000 per violation per day, enforced by the AG, city attorneys and county counsel. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**California TFAIA (SB 53).** California's Transparency in Frontier Artificial Intelligence Act, in force 1 Jan 2026. Frontier developers (models trained with more than 10^26 operations) must publish transparency reports and report critical safety incidents to Cal OES within 15 days, or within 24 hours if there is imminent danger. Large frontier developers (over $500M revenue) must also publish and follow a frontier AI framework and send Cal OES summaries of internal-use risk assessments every three months, or on another reasonable schedule. It adds whistleblower protections, and the AG can seek up to $1M per violation. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**CCPA ADMT Regulations.** California Privacy Protection Agency rules, effective 1 Jan 2026, for businesses that use automated decision-making technology to make significant decisions about consumers (lending, housing, education, employment, health care). A business must give a pre-use notice, offer an opt-out unless an exception applies (such as a human appeal) and answer access requests about the ADMT's logic and outcome. Compliance is required by 1 Jan 2027. Companion rules require risk assessments and cybersecurity audits. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**CE Marking.** The physical or digital marking indicating conformity with EU health, safety, and environmental requirements. Required for high-risk AI systems under the EU AI Act. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Choice.** A privacy principle giving individuals options about how their personal data is collected and used, including the ability to opt out of certain processing. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Classic AI.** Traditional AI approaches using rule-based systems, statistical methods, and simpler ML algorithms. Contrasted with modern generative AI and LLMs. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Cloud, On-Premises & Edge Deployment.** The deployment-environment decision: cloud offers scalability and managed infrastructure but raises data-residency and third-party questions; on-premises maximizes control and data sovereignty at higher operational cost; edge deployment puts inference on local devices for latency and privacy benefits with harder update and monitoring logistics. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Code of Practice on Marking and Labelling.** The voluntary Code of Practice on Transparency of AI-generated Content, final 10 June 2026 under AI Act Article 50(7). Section 1 covers providers' machine-readable marking and detection; Section 2 covers deployers' labelling of deepfakes and AI-generated public-interest text. The Commission and AI Board assessed it as adequate. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Cognitive Computing.** A category of AI systems that enables more natural interaction between humans and machines. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Colorado ADMT Act (SB 26-189).** Colorado's 2026 law (signed 14 May 2026) that repealed and replaced the never-effective Colorado AI Act (SB 24-205). For consequential decisions made from 1 Jan 2027 in education, employment, housing, lending, insurance, health care and government services, it requires developer documentation, deployer notice, a plain-language explanation within 30 days of an adverse outcome, data correction and human review. The AG enforces exclusively, with a 60-day cure; there is no private right of action. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Competent Authorities.** Regulatory or oversight bodies *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Complexity.** A characteristic of AI systems requiring governance: advanced techniques like neural networks create interrelationships between inputs and outputs that are difficult to trace or understand. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Computer Vision.** The ability of a functional unit to capture, process, and interpret image or video data. May include wavelengths beyond visible light (e.g., infrared). *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Concept Drift.** A change in the underlying relationship between input data and expected outputs over time, often due to real-world changes that invalidate the model's learned patterns. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Confabulation.** NIST AI 600-1 GAI risk: a generative model confidently producing false or fabricated content (often called hallucination). Classified among the technical/model risks unique to or exacerbated by GAI. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Conformity Assessment.** The process of verifying that high-risk AI systems meet EU AI Act requirements before market placement. May involve self-assessment or third-party notified bodies. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Conformity Requirements.** The specific standards, regulations, and internal policies an AI system must satisfy before deployment, including technical, legal, and ethical compliance obligations. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Connectionism (Connectionist Paradigm).** A form of cognitive modeling that uses a network of interconnected units, which are generally simple computational units. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Consent.** A privacy principle requiring freely given, specific, informed, and unambiguous indication of the data subject's agreement to processing of their personal data. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Consumer Protection.** Laws and regulations prohibiting unfair, deceptive, or abusive acts and practices (UDAAP/UDAP) in consumer transactions. Applies to AI-driven products and services. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Content Provenance (NIST AI 600-1).** Tracking the origin and history of digital content. One of the four primary GAI considerations in NIST AI 600-1, alongside governance, pre-deployment testing, and incident disclosure. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Contestability.** The ability of people affected by an AI system's output or decision to challenge it and obtain a review and, where justified, a correction; it needs enough transparency about how the result was reached to argue with. Reflected in OECD AI Principle 1.3 and in the GDPR Art. 22(3) right to contest solely automated decisions; IAPP also calls it redress. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Context of the Organization.** ISO 42001 Clause 4: understanding internal and external factors affecting the AI Management System, including stakeholder needs, legal requirements, and organizational objectives. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Continuous Monitoring.** Ongoing observation of deployed AI system performance, data inputs/outputs, and environmental conditions to detect drift, degradation, or unexpected behavior in real time. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Continuous Validation.** Ongoing verification that system meets requirements in production *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Controllability.** A property of an AI system that enables a human or another external agent to intervene in the system's operation. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Convolutional Neural Network (CNN).** Feedforward neural network with convolution in at least one of its layers. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Copyright.** Legal protection for original works of authorship fixed in a tangible medium. AI training on copyrighted content without authorization is subject to ongoing litigation (e.g., Getty v. Stability AI). *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Copyright Chapter (GPAI CoP).** GPAI Code of Practice chapter applying to ALL GPAI providers: a copyright policy, compliance with text-and-data-mining opt-outs under Art 4(3) CDSM, and measures against infringing outputs. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Counterfactual Fairness.** A fairness criterion assessing whether a model's decision would change if a sensitive attribute (gender, race) were altered while keeping all other factors constant. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Cross-Border Data Transfer.** The transfer of personal data outside the EU/EEA, requiring appropriate safeguards such as adequacy decisions, standard contractual clauses, or binding corporate rules. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Cross-Functional Collaboration.** Multiple departments (legal, engineering, ethics, product, compliance) sharing responsibility for AI governance decisions to ensure diverse perspectives. *(Chapter 2)*\n\n### D\n\n**Data Acquisition.** The process of collecting and sourcing data for AI training, including verification of lawful rights, quality assessment, and documentation of origin. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Data Annotation.** A process in which data is provided with a set of descriptive information without the data itself being altered. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Data Augmentation.** The process of creating synthetic samples by modifying or utilizing existing data. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Data Controller.** The entity that determines the purposes and means of processing personal data. Carries primary accountability for GDPR compliance in AI contexts. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Data Dependency.** A characteristic of AI requiring governance: most AI systems depend heavily on data quality, volume, and diversity. Poor or biased data leads to inaccurate or unfair outcomes. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Data Drift.** A change in the statistical properties of input data over time, potentially degrading model performance as the production data diverges from training data. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Data Governance.** The overall management of data availability, usability, integrity, and security. Ensuring data is lawfully collected, accurate, sufficient, and fit for its intended AI use. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Data Integrity.** The accuracy, consistency, and trustworthiness of data throughout its lifecycle. Critical for AI systems where corrupted or tampered data produces unreliable outputs. *(Chapter 8)*\n\n**Data Leak.** Accidental exposure of confidential, proprietary or personal data to people or systems that should not have it, for example through a misconfigured cloud bucket, a misdirected email or an employee entering records into an external AI tool. When personal data is involved it is a GDPR personal data breach even when no attacker is involved (Art. 4(12)), so the 72-hour notification duty to the supervisory authority can apply (Art. 33). *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Data Lineage.** The tracking of data's lifecycle and processing. Where it came from, how it was transformed, where it moved. Essential for traceability and audit readiness. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Data Minimization.** A GDPR principle requiring that only personal data necessary for the specific task be collected and processed. Critical for AI systems that tend to hoard data. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Data Poisoning.** An attack that corrupts a model's training data, for example by planting mislabeled or trigger-laden samples in a scraped or third-party dataset, so the trained model learns wrong or hidden behaviour such as a backdoor. It strikes during training, unlike evasion attacks and prompt injection, which manipulate inputs at inference time; the EU AI Act expects high-risk providers to address it where appropriate (Art. 15(5)). *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA).** A mandatory risk assessment under GDPR Article 35 required when processing is likely to result in high risk to individuals, including when AI processes sensitive data. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Data Protection Officer (DPO).** A mandatory role under GDPR for certain organizations. Responsible for advising on data protection obligations, monitoring compliance, and serving as contact point for authorities. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Data Provenance.** Documentation of data's origin, collection method, licensing, and conditions of collection. Distinct from lineage which tracks movement and transformation. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Data Quality.** The degree to which a dataset is fit for the job it will do, judged on dimensions such as correctness, completeness, consistency, timeliness, validity and representativeness; weak data produces weak or biased outputs. For high-risk systems the EU AI Act requires training, validation and testing data to be relevant, sufficiently representative and, to the best extent possible, free of errors and complete for the intended purpose (Art. 10(3)). *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Data Quality Review.** A process in which data is checked for completeness, systematic deviations (bias), and other factors that impair its usefulness for an AI system. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Data Sampling.** Process of selecting a subset of data samples expected to exhibit similar patterns and trends as the larger dataset. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Data Set.** Collection of data with a common format. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Data Sheet.** A standardized document describing a dataset's composition, collection process, recommended uses, and limitations. Supports responsible data governance for AI training. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Data Subject Access Request (DSAR).** A formal request by an individual to an organization to access their personal data and information about how it is processed. Challenging when AI models have memorized training data. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Data Subject Rights.** GDPR-granted rights including access (Art 15), rectification (Art 16), erasure (Art 17), restriction (Art 18), portability (Art 20), and objection (Art 21). *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Deactivation Policy.** Documented procedures for safely taking an AI system offline. Triggered by regulatory requirements, performance issues, or safety concerns with controlled shutdown steps. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Decision Tree.** Model in which inference is encoded as paths from the root to a leaf node in a tree structure. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Decommissioning.** Planned retirement, data disposition, model archival *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Deepfake.** Image, audio or video content generated or manipulated with AI so that it resembles real people, objects, places or events closely enough to pass as authentic; a common tool for impersonation fraud, disinformation and harassment. The EU AI Act defines a deep fake in Art. 3(60) and requires deployers who use AI to generate or manipulate one to disclose that it is artificial (Art. 50(4)). *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Deep Learning.** *\\<Artificial Intelligence>* An approach to creating rich hierarchical representations through the training of neural networks with many hidden layers. Deep learning is a subset of ML. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Deidentification.** Techniques to remove or obscure personally identifying information from datasets. Includes pseudonymization, aggregation, and generalization approaches. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Deployment.** Integration into operational environment, user training *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Design & Construction.** Data collection/preparation, model design, training, tuning *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Design Defect.** A product liability claim where the product's design is inherently unsafe. AI systems with flawed architectures or inadequate safety features may face design defect claims. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Deterministic Output.** System outputs that are entirely predictable given the same inputs, characteristic of traditional rule-based systems. Contrasts with probabilistic AI outputs. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Differential Privacy.** A mathematical framework for quantifying privacy loss when analyzing datasets. Ensures individual records cannot be inferred from aggregate outputs. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation 2026/1744).** EU regulation of 8 July 2026 amending the AI Act, in force 27 July 2026. It moves the high-risk dates to 2 December 2027 (Annex III) and 2 August 2028 (Annex I), softens the AI literacy duty, adds two Article 5 bans from 2 December 2026, creates Article 4a on bias-detection data, widens the AI Office's competence, pushes national sandboxes to 2 August 2027 and extends SME relief to small mid-caps. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Disinformation.** False or manipulated content that is created or spread deliberately to deceive or cause harm, such as a fabricated video of a candidate released to sway an election. Intent is what separates it from misinformation; NIST AI 600-1 notes that generative AI makes it cheaper to produce at scale and to target at specific groups. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Disparate Impact.** Unintentional discrimination where an AI system's neutral policy disproportionately harms a protected group. Still unlawful under civil rights laws even without intent. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Disparate Treatment.** Intentional discrimination where an AI system explicitly treats individuals differently based on protected characteristics. Prohibited under civil rights laws. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Documentation and Reporting.** The systematic recording of AI lifecycle activities, decisions, risks, and incidents to establish compliance, traceability, and audit readiness. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Domestic Representative (Korea AI Basic Act).** A Korea-based agent that a foreign AI business operator with no Korean address must appoint in writing, and report to MSIT, if it meets any one decree threshold. The thresholds are KRW 1 trillion prior-year revenue, KRW 10 billion AI-service revenue, 1 million average daily Korean users, or a prior fine for ignoring an order. The operator is liable for the agent's violations. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Downstream Harm.** Negative consequences that ripple from an AI system's outputs to affect individuals, groups, or systems further along the value chain beyond the immediate deployment context. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Dun & Bradstreet Austria Ruling (C-203/22).** CJEU judgment of 27 February 2025 on GDPR Article 15(1)(h): 'meaningful information about the logic involved' means a concise, intelligible explanation of the procedure and principles actually applied to the person's data. Handing over a formula is not enough. Claimed trade secrets must be given to the supervisory authority or court to balance. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n### E\n\n**Echo Chamber.** A phenomenon where AI-driven content recommendation systems reinforce existing beliefs by limiting exposure to diverse perspectives, contributing to polarization. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**EDPB Guidelines 03/2026 on Web Scraping.** Draft EDPB guidelines, adopted 7 July 2026 for consultation until 30 October 2026, on scraping personal data to train generative AI. Consent is rarely workable; legitimate interest is the usual basis if necessity and balancing are met. Special-category data needs both an Article 6 basis and an Article 9(2) exception. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**EDPB Opinion 28/2024.** EDPB opinion of 17 December 2024 on AI models under the GDPR. A model is anonymous only if extracting training subjects' data from it, directly or through queries, is insignificantly likely. Legitimate interest follows a three-step test (interest, necessity, balancing). Unlawful training can taint deployment unless the model was properly anonymised. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Entry into Force (EU AI Act).** August 1, 2024. The AI Act formally entered into force (published in the Official Journal July 12, 2024). Entry into force started the clock but imposed no immediate obligations. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Ethical Considerations.** Moral principles and values that should guide AI development and deployment. Includes fairness, dignity, autonomy, transparency, and prevention of harm. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Ethics by Design.** Integrating ethical considerations at every stage of AI system development, from problem definition through deployment and monitoring. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**EU AI Act.** Regulation (EU) 2024/1689. The world's first comprehensive AI law establishing a risk-based regulatory framework. Effective August 2024, general application August 2026. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**EU AI Act Penalty Tiers.** Prohibited AI practices: up to \u20ac35M or 7% of global annual turnover (whichever is higher). Non-compliance with high-risk obligations: up to \u20ac15M or 3%. Supplying incorrect or misleading information to authorities: up to \u20ac7.5M or 1%. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**European AI Office.** EU body housed within the European Commission that coordinates supervision of GPAI models with systemic risk, maintains the public database of high-risk AI systems, and issues guidance and best practices. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Executive Order 14110.** US Executive Order on Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy AI (October 2023) that directed NIST to develop the Generative AI Profile (NIST AI 600-1) as a companion to the AI RMF. Revoked on 20 January 2025 by EO 14148; the NIST profile it produced remains published. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Executive Order 14281.** US executive order of 23 Apr 2025, 'Restoring Equality of Opportunity and Meritocracy', declaring a policy to eliminate disparate-impact liability to the maximum degree possible and directing federal agencies to deprioritise its enforcement. It cannot amend statutes: Title VII's \u00a7703(k) disparate-impact standard, the Fair Housing Act theory recognised in Inclusive Communities, and private lawsuits remain. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Expert System.** An AI system that accumulates, combines, and integrates knowledge provided by one or more human experts in a specific domain to derive solutions to problems. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Explainability.** A property of an AI system that allows important factors influencing the results of the AI system to be expressed in a way that is understandable to humans. Intended to answer \"Why?\" without claiming optimality. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Explainable and Interpretable.** NIST trustworthiness characteristic: AI system's mechanisms and outputs can be understood by humans. Explainability (can justify) + interpretability (can trace reasoning). *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Exploding Gradient.** A phenomenon in backpropagation training in a neural network in which large error gradients accumulate and lead to very large updates of the weights, causing the model to become unstable. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA).** Initial examination of the data to identify its salient features and assess its quality. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**External Communication Plan.** A documented strategy for communicating about AI system incidents, capabilities, limitations, and changes to stakeholders including customers, regulators, and the public. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n### F\n\n**F1 Score.** The harmonic mean of precision and recall. A balanced metric combining both false positives and false negatives, preferred when class distributions are uneven. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Face Recognition.** Automatic pattern recognition that compares stored images of human faces with an actual face image and displays any match with identifying data. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Fail-Safe Plan.** A fallback decided in advance that kicks in if an AI system starts behaving anomalously or unsafely, or operates outside its set limits: for example halting safely, handing over to a backup system, or reverting to a manual process. The EU AI Act lists backup or fail-safe plans among the technical redundancy solutions that support the robustness of high-risk systems (Art. 15(4); Recital 75). *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Fairness.** A core principle of responsible AI: AI systems must not discriminate against individuals or groups based on race, gender, religion, ethnicity, disability, or socioeconomic status. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Fair Use.** A legal doctrine permitting limited use of copyrighted material without permission for purposes such as criticism, comment, news reporting, teaching, scholarship, or research. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Fair Use (AI Training).** How US courts apply the four fair use factors of 17 U.S.C. \u00a7107 to training AI on copyrighted works. Training was held fair use on the records in Bartz v. Anthropic and Kadrey v. Meta (June 2025) but not in Thomson Reuters v. Ross (Feb 2025, a competing non-generative tool). How the data was acquired and market harm, including market dilution, are decisive; no appellate ruling as of Sep 2026. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Federated Learning.** A training approach where models are trained across decentralized devices using local data, without transferring raw data to a central server. Enhances privacy. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Feedback Loop.** A cycle where AI system outputs influence future inputs, potentially amplifying biases or errors over time. Requires monitoring and circuit-breaking mechanisms. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Feedforward Neural Network (FFNN).** Neural network in which information is directed from the input layer to the output layer in only one direction. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Fine-Tuning.** The process of adapting a pre-trained AI model to a specific task or domain by additional training on targeted data. Balances customization with resource efficiency. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Fit-for-Purpose.** Assessment that data is appropriate and adequate for the specific AI use case. Considering quality, quantity, representativeness, and relevance to the intended task. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Foundation Model.** A large model pretrained on very large, diverse data, usually by self-supervision, that can be adapted through prompting, fine-tuning or RAG to many downstream tasks; LLMs and large image generators are examples. The EU AI Act's legal term for the concept is general-purpose AI model (Art. 3(63)), and IAPP also lists frontier AI as an alternative name. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n### G\n\n**General AI (GAI).** A type of AI system that performs a broad spectrum of tasks at a satisfactory level of performance. Note: AGI is often used in a broader sense. Systems that can perform all tasks a human is capable of. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR).** EU Regulation 2016/679. Comprehensive data protection framework governing processing of personal data, with significant implications for AI systems processing EU residents' data. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**General-Purpose AI (GPAI).** EU AI Act category: AI models with significant generality, capable of performing a wide range of tasks. Those posing systemic risk face additional obligations. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Generative AI.** AI systems that create new content (text, images, code, audio, video) based on patterns learned from training data. A subset of AI exemplified by LLMs and diffusion models. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Generative AI Labeling (Korea).** The Art. 31(2)-(3) duties to indicate that generative AI outputs are AI-generated, and to flag realistic synthetic audio, images or video clearly. The label may be human-recognizable or machine-readable; a machine-readable mark needs at least one text or voice notice as well. Artistic works may use a less intrusive notice. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Generative AI Profile (NIST AI 600-1).** Cross-sectoral NIST AI RMF profile for generative AI (July 2024), developed under Executive Order 14110. Defines 12 GAI-specific risks and suggests actions organized by the Govern, Map, Measure, Manage functions. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Govern Function.** NIST AI RMF function: establishing organizational culture of risk management. Policies, accountability structures, workforce diversity, and third-party oversight. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**GPAI Transitional Period.** GPAI models placed on the market before August 2, 2025 have until August 2, 2027 to reach full compliance with the EU AI Act's GPAI obligations. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Ground Truth.** Value of the target variable for a specific element of the labeled input data. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n### H\n\n**Hallucination.** A phenomenon where an AI model generates plausible-sounding but factually incorrect or fabricated information. A significant risk in LLM deployment. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Heteronomy.** A characteristic of a system that functions under the constraint of external intervention, measures, or supervision. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**High-Impact AI (Korea).** Under Korea's AI Basic Act (Art. 2(4)), an AI system that could significantly affect or endanger life, physical safety or fundamental rights and is used in a listed domain. The domains are energy, drinking water, health care, medical devices, nuclear, biometric analysis for policing, hiring or lending-type judgments, transport, public-sector decisions and K-12 student assessment; the decree can add more. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**High-Performance AI (Korea).** Commentators' label for AI systems subject to Korea's Art. 32 safety duties: trained with at least 10^26 FLOPs, built on state-of-the-art technology, and posing wide-ranging, significant risks. Their operators must manage lifecycle risks, monitor and respond to safety incidents, and report the results to MSIT. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Hiroshima AI Process Reporting Framework.** Voluntary reporting framework launched by the OECD in February 2025, through which organizations report in a common format on how they align with the G7 Hiroshima Process International Code of Conduct for Organisations Developing Advanced AI Systems. Reports are published on OECD.AI; the first round had 25, and version 2.0 widens participation to organizations deploying and providing advanced AI. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Human Approval Checkpoint.** A predefined point in an agent's workflow where a human must approve before it proceeds, typically for high-stakes actions, irreversible ones (payments, permanent deletions, outgoing messages), atypical behaviour, or user-set thresholds. Best enforced at system level and audited for rubber-stamping. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Human Authorship Requirement.** The US rule that copyright protects only works authored in the first instance by a human being. Upheld in Thaler v. Perlmutter (D.C. Cir., 18 Mar 2025; cert. denied 2 Mar 2026). AI-assisted works remain protectable to the extent of the human's expressive contribution, selection or arrangement; prompts alone are not enough (Copyright Office Part 2, Jan 2025). *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Human-Centricity.** A principle of responsible AI: AI systems should be designed with human well-being and dignity in mind, augmenting rather than replacing human capabilities. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Human in the Loop.** A safeguard where a human reviews, overrides, or validates AI decisions in high-stakes scenarios, maintaining accountability in consequential decision-making. *(Chapter 3)*\n\n**Human-Machine Teaming.** Integration of human interaction and machine intelligence. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Human Oversight.** EU AI Act requirement that high-risk AI systems be designed to allow natural persons to oversee their operation, intervene when necessary, and override automated decisions. *(Chapter 3)*\n\n**Hyperparameter.** A feature of a machine learning algorithm that influences its learning process. Selected **prior** to training. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n### I\n\n**Illinois HB 3773.** Public Act 103-0804, amending the Illinois Human Rights Act from 1 Jan 2026. Employers may not use AI that has the effect of discriminating on a protected basis in recruitment, hiring, promotion, discipline, discharge or terms of employment. They may not use zip codes as a proxy for protected classes, and must notify employees when AI is used for these decisions. This is an effect standard, unlike Texas's intent standard. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Image Recognition.** An image classification process that classifies objects, patterns, or concepts in an image. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Impact Assessment.** A systematic evaluation of potential harms, benefits, compliance gaps, and ethical issues an AI system may create. Required for high-risk systems under the EU AI Act. *(Chapter 8)*\n\n**Impact Reassessment Trigger.** An event that should prompt an organization to revisit an existing AI system impact assessment: a change in intended use or purpose, a significant change to the system (new data, a retrained or replaced model), or a change in applicable law or policy. ISO/IEC 42001 clause 8.4 also calls for assessments at planned intervals or when significant changes are proposed. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Imputation.** A procedure in which missing data are replaced by estimated or modeled data. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Incident Management.** The process of detecting, containing, assessing, remediating, and documenting events where AI systems deviate from intended behavior or cause harm. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Inference.** A form of reasoning in which conclusions are derived from known premises. In AI, a basic premise is either a fact, a rule, a model, a feature, or raw data. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Input Data.** Data for which an AI system calculates a predicted result or inference. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Integration Testing.** Validating that all components of an AI system work together correctly. Data flows, API calls, model inference, and downstream system interactions. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Intellectual Property.** Legal rights protecting creations of the mind (copyright, patents, trademarks, trade secrets) increasingly challenged by AI training on protected content. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Interpretability.** The ability to understand the relationships between patterns in data used by an AI system. Related to explainability but focused on internal mechanics. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Interpretability Testing.** Assessing whether AI system decisions can be understood and explained in human terms. Critical for high-stakes domains like healthcare and finance. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**ISO 31000:2018.** The generic risk management standard that ISO/IEC 23894 mirrors clause-by-clause and extends with AI-specific guidance. Principles (Clause 4), framework (Clause 5), and process (Clause 6). ISO 23894 extends it; it does not replace it. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**ISO/IEC 22989.** International standard establishing terminology and definitions for AI systems, capabilities, and lifecycle stages. Foundational reference for all other ISO AI standards. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**ISO/IEC 42001.** The first AI-specific management system standard organizations can certify against (like ISO 27001 for security). Specifies requirements for an AI Management System (AIMS). *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**ISO/IEC 42005.** International standard (first edition, 28 May 2025) giving guidance on AI system impact assessment: how to set up the process (Clause 5), what each assessment records (Clause 6), and how it fits with ISO/IEC 42001 and 23894 (Annexes A and B). Voluntary guidance for any organization that develops, provides or uses AI; not certifiable. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**ISO/IEC 42006.** ISO/IEC 42006:2025 (published 7 July 2025): requirements, additional to ISO/IEC 17021-1, for bodies that audit and certify AI management systems against ISO/IEC 42001. It governs the certifiers, not the organizations being certified. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Issue Management.** The systematic process of identifying, tracking, prioritizing, and resolving problems with deployed AI systems. Bridging incidents to long-term remediation. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n### K\n\n**Knowledge.** *\\<Artificial Intelligence>* Abstracted information about objects, events, concepts, or rules, their relationships, and properties, organized for purposeful systematic use. Does **not** imply cognitive abilities. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Korea AI Basic Act Enforcement Decree.** Presidential Decree No. 36053 (21 Jan 2026), in force with the Act on 22 Jan 2026. It fills in the Act's thresholds and procedures, including the 10^26 FLOP safety test, the domestic-representative thresholds, labeling methods, the 30-day high-impact confirmation process and five-year document retention. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n### L\n\n**Language vs. Multimodal Models.** A model-type choice in deployment evaluation: language models process and generate text only, while multimodal models handle combinations of text, images, audio, or video. Multimodality expands use cases and also expands the risk surface. Each added modality brings its own failure modes, biases, and content risks. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Large Frontier Developer.** Under California SB 53, New York's RAISE Act and Illinois SB 315: a developer that has trained or begun training a frontier model (more than 10^26 operations) and that, with its affiliates, had more than $500 million in gross revenue in the preceding calendar year. Large frontier developers carry the heaviest duties: a published frontier AI framework, regulator filings and, in Illinois from 2028, annual independent audits. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Large Language Model (LLM).** A type of AI model trained on vast text corpora, capable of understanding and generating human-like text. Examples: GPT-4, Claude, Gemini. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Least-Privilege Agent Permissions.** Granting each agent only the minimum tools, data and rights its task needs: scoped, time- or session-bound and non-transferable, with explicit escalation for anything more, and never beyond what the authorising human may do. A core control for bounding agent risk up front, enforced by access controls rather than prompts. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Life Cycle.** The development of a system, product, service, project, or other human-made entity from planning through to decommissioning. *(Chapter 8)*\n\n**Limited Memory AI.** AI systems that retain information for short periods and use past experiences to improve decisions. Example: autonomous vehicles processing recent traffic data. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Localization Policy.** Procedures for restricting an AI system's operation to specific geographic regions, jurisdictions, or contexts. Often required by data residency or regulatory mandates. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM).** A type of recurrent neural network that processes sequential data with satisfactory performance for both long- and short-term dependencies. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n### M\n\n**Machine Learning Algorithm.** Algorithm for determining parameters of a machine learning model from data according to specified criteria. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Machine Learning (ML).** The process of optimizing model parameters using computer-aided methods so that the behavior of the model corresponds to the data or experience. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Machine Learning Model.** A mathematical construct that makes an inference or a prediction based on input data or information. Result of training based on a ML algorithm. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Machine-Readable Opt-Out (TDM).** A rightsholder's express reservation of rights against Article 4 text and data mining. For content made publicly available online, the DSM Directive requires the reservation to be machine-readable, for example in metadata or website terms. GPAI providers must identify and honour these reservations with state-of-the-art technologies under AI Act Art. 53(1)(c). *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Machine Translation (MT).** Task of automatically translating text or speech from one natural language into another using a computer system. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Maintenance Schedule.** A planned cadence for AI system updates, retraining, and maintenance. Frequency determined by risk level, data volatility, and regulatory requirements. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Manage Function.** NIST AI RMF function: allocating risk resources to mapped and measured risks. Prioritizing, responding to, and recovering from AI incidents, plus ongoing monitoring. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Manufacturing Defect.** A product liability claim where a product deviates from its intended design during production. AI systems with deployment errors or corrupted models. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Map Function.** NIST AI RMF function: establishing context to frame AI risks. Understanding system purpose, categorization, benefits/costs, and mapping risks to impact on individuals and society. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Market Surveillance Powers.** EU AI Act inspection powers: require access to technical documentation, perform unannounced inspections and digital audits, mandate corrective actions, suspend or prohibit marketing, and require withdrawal or recall of non-compliant systems. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Measure Function.** NIST AI RMF function: employing quantitative and qualitative methods to analyze and track AI risks. Testing, evaluation, verification, and validation (TEVV). *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Membership Inference Attack.** A privacy attack that determines whether a specific individual's record was part of a model's training set. A key AI-specific threat that existing privacy policies typically fail to address. *(Chapter 3)*\n\n**Misalignment.** A risk where an AI system's behavior, benign in testing, leads to unintended harmful consequences when deployed. Often due to poorly specified objectives. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Misinformation.** False or misleading content shared without intent to deceive, such as a user reposting a chatbot's invented statistic in the belief that it is true. The falsehood can be identical to disinformation; the difference is the absence of intent. NIST AI 600-1 flags generative AI as easing its unintentional production and spread. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**MITRE ATLAS.** Adversarial Threat Landscape for Artificial-Intelligence Systems. A knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques specific to AI systems, modeled after MITRE ATT&CK. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Model.** A physical, mathematical, or other logical representation of a system, an entity, a phenomenon, a process, or data. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Model Card.** A short document released with a trained model that states its intended and out-of-scope uses, how it was evaluated, how it performs across relevant groups and conditions, and its known limitations (format proposed by Mitchell et al., 2019). BOK III.C lists creating the model card as part of release readiness; a system card covers the whole deployed system and a datasheet covers the dataset. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Model Inversion Attack.** A privacy attack that reconstructs sensitive training data from a model's outputs or parameters. AI-era privacy and security policies must add controls for this threat. Traditional policies assume data minimization applies only to storage, not to what a trained model retains. *(Chapter 3)*\n\n**Multi-Agent System.** A system in which several agents cooperate, for example in sequential, supervisor or swarm patterns. Splitting work lets each agent be scoped narrowly, but interaction adds risks: cascading errors, miscoordination, conflicting goals, collusion, emergent behaviour, and sensitive data leaking through shared memory. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n### N\n\n**Named Entity Recognition (NER).** Task of recognizing and labeling the names of entities and their categories for sequences of words. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Narrow AI (Weak AI).** A type of AI system designed to perform specific tasks in order to solve a particular problem. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**National Competent Authorities (EU AI Act).** One or more authorities per member state responsible for market surveillance, handling complaints, investigating breaches, and imposing penalties within their jurisdiction. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Natural Language.** A language that is or was actively used in a community of people and whose rules can be derived from usage. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Natural Language Generation (NLG).** Task of converting semantic data into natural language. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Natural Language Processing (NLP).** AI systems that understand, interpret, and generate human language by combining linguistics and machine learning. Example: ChatGPT. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Natural Language Understanding (NLU).** Extraction of information from text or speech conveyed in natural language, creating a description of the given text/speech and its representation. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Neural Network (NN).** *\\<Artificial Intelligence>* A network consisting of one or more layers of neurons connected by weighted links with adjustable weights, which accepts input data and generates an output. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Neuron.** *\\<Artificial Intelligence>* A primitive processing element that accepts one or more input values and generates an output value by combining the input values and applying an activation function. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF).** NIST AI 100-1. Voluntary framework with four functions (Govern, Map, Measure, Manage) providing organizations a structured approach to AI risk management. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**NIST AI RMF Playbook.** Companion guide to the NIST AI RMF providing practical implementation guidance, use-case examples, and templates for operationalizing the framework's four functions. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Non-Discrimination Law.** Laws prohibiting unfair treatment based on protected characteristics. Applies to AI in employment (Title VII, ADA, ADEA), credit (ECOA), housing (FHA), and insurance. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Notice.** A privacy principle requiring organizations to inform individuals about what personal data is collected and how it will be used. Foundational to AI transparency. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Nudification and CSAM Prohibitions.** Two bans added to EU AI Act Article 5 by the 2026 Digital Omnibus (points (ba) and (bb)), applying from 2 December 2026: AI that generates or manipulates realistic intimate or sexual imagery of an identifiable person without explicit consent, and AI that generates child sexual abuse material. Placing on the market is caught where that output is the intended purpose, or a foreseeable result the system has no adequate safeguards against. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**NYC Local Law 144.** New York City's automated employment decision tool (AEDT) law, in force 1 Jan 2023 and enforced by DCWP from 5 Jul 2023. Employers and employment agencies may use an AEDT on candidates or employees who live in NYC only if it had an independent bias audit within the past year, the audit summary is public, and notice was given at least 10 business days before use. Penalties run up to $500 for a first violation and $500-1,500 for each later one. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n### O\n\n**OECD AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor (AIM).** OECD.AI tool that tracks AI incidents and hazards reported in the news, automatically classifying articles against the OECD's incident and hazard definitions. Because it relies on public reporting, it captures only a subset of the incidents that actually occur. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**OECD AI Principles.** The first intergovernmental standard on AI (2019, updated 2024). Five principles: inclusive growth, human-centered values, transparency, robustness, and accountability. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**OECD AI System Definition (2023 Update).** Definition adopted by the OECD Council on 8 November 2023: a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers from the input it receives how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments. Systems vary in autonomy and adaptiveness after deployment. The EU AI Act's Art. 3(1) definition closely tracks it. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**OECD Framework for the Classification of AI Systems.** OECD tool (Digital Economy Papers No. 323, February 2022) for characterizing AI systems from a policy perspective along five dimensions: People & Planet, Economic Context, Data & Input, AI Model, and Task & Output. Used for registries and inventories, sector frameworks, risk assessment, incident reporting and risk management. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Opacity.** A characteristic of AI systems requiring governance: the 'black box' nature of some AI models where it is challenging to understand how inputs are transformed into outputs. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Open Source Agreement.** License terms governing the use of open-source AI models. May include restrictions on commercial use, attribution requirements, or copyleft provisions. *(Chapter 12)*\n\n**Operational Controls.** Policies, procedures, and technical mechanisms governing how an AI system functions in production, including access controls, monitoring thresholds, and intervention protocols. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Operation & Monitoring.** Performance monitoring, drift detection, incident management *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Overfitting.** A modeling error where an AI model learns training data too precisely, including noise, and fails to generalize to new data. Results in poor real-world performance. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n### P\n\n**Parameter (Model Parameter).** Internal variable of a model that affects how the model calculates its results. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Patent.** Legal protection for novel, useful, and non-obvious inventions. AI-generated inventions raise questions about inventorship (e.g., DABUS/Thaler v. Vidal). *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**PDCA Cycle.** Plan-Do-Check-Act. The continuous improvement model underlying ISO management system standards including ISO 42001's AI Management System. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Performance.** Measurable result. Can refer to quantitative or qualitative findings. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Personal Data (PbD).** All information that can be used to identify the natural person to whom such information relates, or that is/could be directly or indirectly related to a natural person. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Personally Identifiable Information (PII).** Any information that can identify a specific individual, directly or indirectly. Name, email, IP address, location data, or online identifiers. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Planning.** *\\<Artificial Intelligence>* Data processing operations that assemble a workflow from a series of actions to achieve a specific objective. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Post-Market Monitoring.** EU AI Act requirement for ongoing surveillance of high-risk AI systems after deployment. Documenting incidents, tracking performance, and reporting to authorities. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Potential for Harm.** A characteristic of AI requiring governance: AI can be used maliciously or cause unintended negative consequences through surveillance, autonomous weapons, or malfunction. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Precision.** The proportion of positive predictions that are actually correct. Measures how many selected items are relevant. High precision = few false positives. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Predictability.** A property of an AI system that enables interested parties to make reliable assumptions about the outcome. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Prediction.** Primary output of an AI system given input data or information. Does **not** necessarily mean forecasting something in the future. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Privacy.** A core principle of responsible AI: organizations must ensure AI systems comply with privacy regulations, track all uses of PII, and provide means for inquiries and corrections. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Privacy by Design.** Integrating privacy considerations at every stage of system development, from requirements through deployment, rather than bolting privacy on afterward. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Privacy-Enhanced.** NIST trustworthiness characteristic: AI system incorporates safeguards for human autonomy, identity, and dignity through data minimization, anonymization, and consent mechanisms. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Probabilistic Output.** Outputs based on probabilities rather than certainties, common in neural networks and ML models. Contrasts with deterministic systems where outputs are fully predictable. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Probability Severity Matrix.** A risk assessment tool that quantifies and prioritizes risks by plotting likelihood (probability) against potential impact (severity), typically on a grid. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Production Data.** Data collected during the operational phase of an AI system for which a deployed AI system calculates a predicted result or inference. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Production Readiness Evaluation.** Structured pre-deployment review that an AI system is production-worthy: safety, accountability, suitability for intended context, regulatory conformity, performance benchmarking, and operational planning. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Product Liability.** Legal responsibility of manufacturers and sellers for defective products that cause harm. Applies to AI systems under design defect, manufacturing defect, and failure to warn theories. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Product Liability Directive (EU) 2024/2853.** The EU's revised no-fault product liability law, replacing Directive 85/374/EEC. Software, including AI systems, is a product however supplied; free and open-source software supplied outside commercial activity is excluded. It adds court-ordered disclosure, rebuttable presumptions of defect and causation, and compensation for medically recognised psychological harm and loss of non-professional data. Transposition by 9 Dec 2026; applies to products placed on the market after that date. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Prohibited AI.** EU AI Act category: AI practices banned entirely. Includes social scoring, real-time remote biometric identification in public spaces (with narrow exceptions), and subliminal manipulation. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Prompt Engineering.** Crafting and iterating on the instructions, context and examples sent to a generative model, including system prompts and few-shot examples, to steer it toward more useful, accurate and safe output. It changes no model weights, unlike fine-tuning, and adds no external knowledge source, unlike retrieval augmented generation. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Prompt Injection.** An attack where malicious input manipulates an AI model's behavior by overriding system instructions or injecting harmful content through the user prompt. *(Chapter 3)*\n\n**Proprietary Model Deployment Obligations.** The heightened duties that come with deploying a proprietary third-party model: increased contractual obligations, reliance on vendor claims that cannot be independently verified, potential for higher liability exposure, and dependence on the vendor for updates, incident support, and continuity. *(Chapter 12)*\n\n**Proprietary vs. Open-Source Models.** A core deployment-evaluation choice: proprietary models offer vendor support and cutting-edge capability but limited visibility into training data and terms-of-service constraints; open-source models offer transparency, control, and customization but shift security, hosting, and compliance burden onto the deployer. License terms bind in both cases. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Pseudonymization.** Processing personal data so it can no longer be attributed to a specific data subject without additional information kept separately. GDPR encourages as a safeguard. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Public Disclosure.** Transparency obligation to publish information about AI system capabilities, limitations, training data, evaluation results, and intended use. Supports accountability. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Purpose Limitation.** A GDPR principle requiring personal data be collected for specified, explicit, and legitimate purposes and not further processed in an incompatible manner. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n### Q\n\n**Quality Management System (QMS).** A formalized system documenting processes, procedures, and responsibilities for ensuring AI system quality and compliance throughout the lifecycle. *(Chapter 8)*\n\n**Question Answering.** Task of determining the most appropriate answer to a question posed in natural language. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n### R\n\n**RACI Matrix.** A responsibility assignment framework: Responsible (does the work), Accountable (owns the outcome), Consulted (provides input), Informed (kept updated). *(Chapter 2)*\n\n**RAISE Act.** New York's Responsible AI Safety and Education Act, signed December 2025 and re-enacted by a March 2026 chapter amendment modelled on California SB 53; effective 1 Jan 2027. It uses the same 10^26-operation and $500M thresholds. Frontier developers report critical safety incidents to a new office within the Department of Financial Services within 72 hours, and large developers publish frameworks and file disclosure statements. The AG can seek up to $1M for a first violation and $3M for later ones; there is no private right of action. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Reactive Machines.** AI systems that respond to specific stimuli without memory or learning capability. They operate on pre-programmed rules. Example: IBM Deep Blue. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Reasoning AI.** AI systems with advanced pattern analysis and prediction capabilities. Most current LLMs fall in this category, capable of passing standardized tests. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Reassessment.** Periodic review of continued fitness for purpose *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Recall.** The proportion of actual positives correctly identified. Measures how many relevant items are selected. High recall = few false negatives. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Record Keeping.** The obligation for controllers and processors to maintain records of processing activities, including purposes, data categories, recipients, and retention periods. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Recurrent Neural Network (RNN).** A neural network in which the outputs of both the previous layer and the previous processing step are fed into the current layer. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Red Teaming.** Adversarial testing where a team simulates attacks or misuse scenarios to uncover vulnerabilities that standard testing might miss. Critical for AI safety evaluation. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Reinforcement Learning (RL).** Learning an optimal sequence of actions to maximize a reward through interaction with an environment. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Reinforcement Learning with Human Feedback (RLHF).** A training technique in which people compare or rank a model's outputs, a reward model is trained on those preferences, and reinforcement learning then tunes the model toward the responses people prefer; a common way chat-style LLMs are steered toward helpful, harmless answers. Also called reinforcement learning from human feedback. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Reliability.** Property of the correspondence between intended behavior and the results. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Resilience.** The ability of a system to quickly recover its operational state following an incident. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Responsible AI.** A framework ensuring AI systems are designed, developed, and deployed to respect human rights, promote fairness, and minimize risks while benefiting individuals and society. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Retraining.** Updating a trained model through training with other training data. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG).** A technique where an AI model retrieves relevant external data at query time to augment its responses, improving accuracy and reducing hallucinations. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Right to Erasure.** GDPR Article 17: the right of individuals to have their personal data deleted in certain circumstances. Raises 'machine unlearning' challenges for AI systems. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Risk.** The effect of uncertainty on objectives. Deviation can be positive, negative, or both. Characterized by risk drivers, potential events, consequences, and likelihood. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Robotics.** AI systems capable of performing kinetic physical tasks such as carrying objects, moving, and more advanced tasks like cooking. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Robustness.** The ability of a system to maintain its performance level under **all** circumstances. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n### S\n\n**Safe.** NIST trustworthiness characteristic: AI system does not, under defined conditions, lead to a state in which human life, health, property, or the environment is endangered. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Safety & Security Chapter (GPAI CoP).** GPAI Code of Practice chapter applying ONLY to systemic-risk GPAI providers: safety and security framework, risk identification and analysis, mitigations, serious-incident reporting, and cybersecurity. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Sample.** An atomic data element that is processed in batches by a machine learning algorithm or an AI system. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**SCHUFA Ruling (C-634/21).** CJEU judgment of 7 December 2023: a credit bureau's automated score is itself an automated decision under GDPR Article 22 when a lender that receives it draws strongly on it to enter into, perform or end a contract. Upstream score providers can therefore owe Article 22 and Article 15(1)(h) duties. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Secondary Use.** The use of an AI system or its outputs for purposes beyond the original intended use case. Creates unanticipated risks requiring governance oversight. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Secure and Resilient.** NIST trustworthiness characteristic: AI system can withstand adverse events and recover to normal function while maintaining confidentiality, integrity, and availability. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Security.** A core principle of responsible AI: protecting sensitive data (PII, IP, copyrighted content) used by AI systems from unauthorized access, compromise, and theft. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Security Testing.** Evaluating AI system resistance to attacks. Includes adversarial example testing, prompt injection testing, model extraction attempts, and API security validation. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Self-Aware AI.** The highest theoretical level of AI possessing consciousness and self-awareness. Would recognize its own existence. Currently does not exist. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Semi-Supervised Learning.** A training method using a small amount of labeled data combined with a large amount of unlabeled data. The AI learns from labeled examples then predicts labels for the rest. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Semi-Supervised Machine Learning.** Machine learning in which both labeled and unlabeled data are used during training. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Sensitive Data.** Data requiring heightened protection due to potential for harm if misused. Includes special category data plus financial, location, and children's data depending on jurisdiction. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Sentiment Analysis.** Task to computationally identify and categorize opinions expressed in text, speech, or image to determine a range of sentiments (e.g., positive to negative). *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Shadow AI.** Use of AI tools or systems inside an organization without approval, or outside the view of its governance process, such as staff putting company data into a personal chatbot account; it usually breaks the organization's rules on acceptable AI use, confidentiality or personal data. Managed through discovery, an AI inventory, an acceptable use policy and approved alternatives. *(Chapter 3)*\n\n**Small vs. Large Models.** A deployment-evaluation trade-off: larger models generally offer broader capability but cost more to run, respond slower, and carry a larger risk surface; smaller models are cheaper, faster, and easier to control but may underperform on complex tasks. The governing question is fitness for the specific use case, not maximum capability. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Soft Computing.** A field of data processing that tolerates and exploits imprecision, uncertainty, and partial truth to make problem-solving more transparent and resilient. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Special Category Data.** GDPR Article 9 data types subject to stricter processing prohibitions: race, ethnicity, politics, religion, trade union membership, genetics, biometrics, health, sex life, sexual orientation. *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Speech Recognition (STT).** Conversion of a speech signal into a representation of the speech content by a functional unit. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Speech Synthesis (TTS).** Generation of artificial speech. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Speed and Scale.** A characteristic of AI systems requiring governance: AI processes data and makes decisions far faster than humans and at massive scale, creating both opportunities and risks. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Stakeholder (Interested Party).** An individual, group, or organization that can influence a decision or activity, be affected by it, or feel affected by it. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Stakeholder Mapping.** The process of identifying all parties affected by an AI system, understanding their concerns, interests, and influence. Essential for comprehensive risk management. *(Chapter 2)*\n\n**Stress Testing (AI).** Pre-release testing under expected and edge-case workloads: latency and throughput benchmarks, memory/compute usage under load, failover behavior during outages, and evaluation against adversarial and malicious prompts. *(Chapter 10)*\n\n**STRIDE.** A threat modeling framework: Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege. Adapted for AI security analysis. *(Chapter 13)*\n\n**Subsymbolic AI.** AI based on techniques and models that use an implicit encoding of information derived from experience or raw data. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Sub-Tier Dependencies.** Hidden supply-chain risk beneath a contracted AI vendor: subcontracted data-labeling teams, models trained on scraped or copyrighted data, and outdated code libraries. A core challenge is how many levels down the TPRM program should probe. *(Chapter 3)*\n\n**Suggested Actions (AI RMF Playbook).** The Playbook's voluntary, per-subcategory steps for reaching an AI RMF outcome, e.g. MAP 1.1's step to identify whether a non-AI or non-technology alternative would give more trustworthy outcomes. Organizations use as many or as few as fit; they are not a checklist or an ordered sequence. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Supervised Learning.** A training method using labeled data where the AI learns from examples with known correct answers. Example: spam filter trained on labeled spam/legitimate messages. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Supervised Machine Learning.** Machine learning in which only labeled data is used during training. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Support Vector Machine (SVM).** Machine learning algorithm that finds decision boundaries with maximum margin. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Symbolic AI.** AI based on techniques and models that manipulate symbols and structures according to explicitly defined rules. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Synthetic Data.** Artificially generated records that imitate the patterns and statistical make-up of a real dataset without copying actual records; used where genuine data is scarce, skewed toward some groups, or too risky to use or share. It lowers but does not remove privacy risk, since a generator trained on personal data can reproduce details of real people. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**System Card.** A transparency document describing an AI system's architecture, training data, intended use, limitations, and ethical considerations. Broader scope than a model card. *(Chapter 8)*\n\n### T\n\n**TAKE IT DOWN Act.** Federal law (Pub. L. 119-12, signed 19 May 2025). It makes it a crime to knowingly publish nonconsensual intimate images, including AI digital forgeries. Covered platforms that host user content must run a notice-and-removal process and take reported images down within 48 hours, along with known identical copies. The platform duties have applied since 19 May 2026 and are enforced by the FTC, including against nonprofits. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Task.** *\\<Artificial Intelligence>* Action required to achieve a specific goal. Examples: classification, regression, ranking, clustering, dimensionality reduction. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Technical Documentation.** Required documentation for high-risk AI systems demonstrating compliance. Includes system design, training methodologies, testing results, and risk management measures. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Test Data (Evaluation Data).** Data used to evaluate the performance of a final model. Distinct from training data and validation data. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Texas Responsible AI Governance Act (TRAIGA).** Texas HB 149, effective 1 Jan 2026. It bans developing or deploying AI with intent to push people toward self-harm or crime, to discriminate unlawfully (disparate impact alone is not enough), to infringe constitutional rights, or to produce child sexual abuse material or sexual deepfakes. AI disclosure, social-scoring and biometric-identification limits apply to government. The AG alone enforces, with a 60-day cure, penalties up to $200,000 per uncurable violation, a NIST-framework defence and a 36-month sandbox. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Text and Data Mining Exception.** EU copyright exceptions in the DSM Directive (EU) 2019/790 for automated analysis of text and data. Article 3 lets research organisations and cultural heritage institutions mine lawfully accessed works for scientific research, with no opt-out. Article 4 lets anyone mine lawfully accessible works unless the rightsholder has reserved its rights. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Theory of Mind AI.** A theoretical advanced AI that could understand and simulate human emotions, beliefs, intentions, and thoughts. Example: HAL-9000 (fictional). *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Third-Party Processor.** An entity that processes personal data on behalf of the data controller. Must have a contract specifying scope, safeguards, and responsibilities (GDPR Art 28). *(Chapter 4)*\n\n**Third-Party Risk Management (TPRM).** Governance of outsourced AI components. Pretrained models, cloud infrastructure, data labeling, algorithmic APIs. For AI it must evolve beyond traditional vendor checklists to cover black-box algorithms, datasets of unknown origin, and unpredictable LLM APIs. *(Chapter 3)*\n\n**Tool Misuse.** An agent using a tool it is legitimately allowed to use in an unsafe or unintended way, such as deleting valuable data, over-calling a costly API or leaking information, often after prompt injection or an ambiguous instruction. OWASP ASI02 (Tool Misuse and Exploitation); gaining privileges it should not have is ASI03 instead. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n**Trade Secret.** Confidential business information providing competitive advantage. AI models and training methodologies may be protected as trade secrets if reasonable secrecy measures are maintained. *(Chapter 5)*\n\n**Trained Model.** Result of model training. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Training and Awareness Program.** An organizational program delivering AI terminology, strategy, and governance education to all stakeholders, tailored to each role's interaction with AI. *(Chapter 2)*\n\n**Training and Testing.** The phase where AI models learn from data (training) and are evaluated for accuracy, bias, and performance (testing) before deployment. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Training Data.** Data used to train a machine learning model. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Training (Model Training).** Process for determining or improving the parameters of a machine learning model, based on a ML algorithm, using training data. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Transfer Learning.** A set of methods in which data intended for solving one problem is used to apply the knowledge gained from it to another problem. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Transparency and Documentation Prompts (AI RMF Playbook).** The questions in each Playbook entry's Transparency and Documentation section, under 'Organizations can document the following', e.g. GOVERN 1.6's 'Who is responsible for documenting and maintaining the AI system inventory details?'. Each list is followed by AI Transparency Resources such as GAO-21-519SP. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Transparency Chapter (GPAI CoP).** GPAI Code of Practice chapter applying to ALL GPAI providers: model documentation, information to downstream providers, and the Annex XI/XII technical documentation obligations of Articles 53(1)(a)-(b). *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Transparency (Organization).** The characteristic of an organization whereby relevant activities and decisions are communicated to relevant interested parties in a comprehensive, accessible, and understandable manner. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Transparency (System).** A characteristic of a system whereby appropriate information about the system is made available to relevant interested parties. May include: characteristics, performance, limitations, components, procedures, measures, design objectives, data sources, labeling protocols. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Trustworthiness.** The ability to meet the expectations of interested parties in a verifiable manner. Characteristics include: reliability, availability, resilience, security, privacy, accountability, transparency, integrity, authenticity, quality, and user-friendliness. Applies to services, products, technologies, data, information, and organizations. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Trustworthy AI.** AI that is built and governed so that people can justifiably rely on it, in line with principles such as safety, fairness and accountability; IAPP treats the term as largely synonymous with ethical AI and responsible AI. The NIST AI RMF's seven characteristics of trustworthy AI are: valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; and fair with harmful bias managed. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n### U\n\n**Underfitting.** A modeling error where an AI model is too simple to capture underlying patterns in the data. Results in poor performance on both training and new data. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Unfair and Deceptive Acts and Practices (UDAP).** FTC-enforced standard prohibiting misleading or harmful business practices. AI systems making unsubstantiated claims or hiding AI involvement may violate UDAP. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Unintended Use.** Application of an AI system in ways not anticipated by its developers. May produce harmful outcomes due to mismatched capabilities and context. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Unit Testing.** Testing individual components or functions of an AI system in isolation to verify each part works correctly before integration. *(Chapter 9)*\n\n**Unsupervised Learning.** A training method using unlabeled data where the AI must detect patterns independently. Example: customer segmentation from a CRM database. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Unsupervised Machine Learning.** Machine learning in which only unlabeled data is used during training. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Use Case.** A specific application scenario defining what problem the AI system solves, for whom, under what conditions, and with what expected outcomes. Foundational to governance. *(Chapter 8)*\n\n**Use Case Assessment.** The process of evaluating a proposed AI application against organizational policies, risk appetite, and ethical standards before development begins. *(Chapter 8)*\n\n**User Training.** Education provided to individuals who interact with AI systems. Covering capabilities, limitations, appropriate use, and how to flag unexpected or harmful outputs. *(Chapter 1)*\n\n**Utah AI Policy Act.** Utah's 2024 law (SB 149, effective 1 May 2024) that first required generative AI disclosures and created the Office of AI Policy, the AI Learning Laboratory and regulatory mitigation agreements. As revised by SB 226 (2025), suppliers must disclose AI when a consumer clearly asks, and licensed professionals must disclose up front in high-risk interactions. Using AI is no defence to a consumer-protection violation. Fines reach $2,500 per violation, and the sunset is extended to 1 Jul 2027. *(Chapter 6)*\n\n### V\n\n**Valid and Reliable.** NIST trustworthiness characteristic: AI system produces accurate (valid) and consistent (reliable) outputs under specified conditions. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Validation.** Confirmation by providing objective evidence that the requirements for a specific intended use or a specific intended application have been met. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Validation Data (Development Data).** Data used to compare the performance of different candidate models. Used to tune hyperparameters. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Vendor Agreement.** Contract with third-party AI providers specifying data usage rights, performance guarantees, liability allocation, audit rights, and compliance obligations. *(Chapter 12)*\n\n**Verification.** Confirmation by providing objective evidence that specified requirements have been met. Ensures only that a product conforms to its specifications. *(Chapter 7)*\n\n**Verification & Validation.** Testing against specifications, intended use validation *(Chapter 7)*\n\n### W\n\n**Watermarking.** Hiding a signal in AI-generated content, or attaching one to its metadata, that people generally cannot perceive but software can detect, so the content can later be identified as machine-made; one provenance technique alongside metadata, fingerprinting and cryptographic signatures. The EU AI Act requires providers of systems generating synthetic audio, image, video or text to mark outputs in a machine-readable, detectable way (Art. 50(2)). *(Chapter 6)*\n\n**Workforce Readiness.** Assessment of whether staff have the skills, training, and awareness to effectively use, monitor, and govern AI systems in their operational context. *(Chapter 11)*\n\n### Z\n\n**Zero-Shot Learning (ZSL).** A machine learning technique where an AI model recognizes objects or concepts without having seen any training examples of those categories. *(Chapter 1)*\n";
